window.QBANK={"1":[{"i":"REG-V-A-0001","t":"Kestrel Supply, Inc., a calendar-year C corporation, is preparing its 2026 Schedule M-3. Which item is a permanent book-tax difference?","mc":{"s":"Kestrel Supply, Inc., a calendar-year C corporation, is preparing its 2026 Schedule M-3. Which item is a permanent book-tax difference?","d":"","o":["Tax depreciation on machinery that exceeds book depreciation in the year it is placed in service","An increase in an allowance for doubtful accounts that is deductible only when accounts are written off","Prepaid rent received in December 2026 that is taxed in 2026 but recognized in book income in 2027","$25,000 of interest earned on bonds issued by a state government"],"a":4,"cite":"Form 1120 Schedule M-3 instructions; IRC §103(a)","rat":"Interest on state and local bonds is recorded in book income but never taxed, so the difference never reverses.","ex":["Faster tax depreciation reverses in later years when book depreciation exceeds tax depreciation, so it is a temporary difference.","The reserve is deducted for tax when specific accounts are written off, so the difference reverses. It is temporary.","Income is taxed earlier than it is recorded for book purposes and the difference reverses in 2027. It is temporary.","Interest on state and local bonds is recorded in book income but never taxed, so the difference never reverses. It is a permanent difference."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"A","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Interest on state and local bonds is a permanent book-tax difference because it is never taxed; accelerated depreciation and reserves are temporary.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["What makes a book-tax difference permanent?","It never reverses in a later year."],["Which listed item is recorded in book income but never taxed?","Interest on state government bonds; depreciation, reserves and prepaid rent are timing items."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-A-0002","t":"Halden Tool Co., a calendar-year C corporation, reported the following for 2026: net income per books of $412,000 after recording $86,000 of federal income tax expense; $30,000 of client meals expense, of which 50% is deductible; $4,000 of fines paid to a government agency; $9,000 of interest on state bonds; and tax depreciation that exceeds book depreciation by $22,000. What is Halden's taxable income before the net operating loss deduction and special deductions?","mc":{"s":"Halden Tool Co., a calendar-year C corporation, reported the following for 2026: net income per books of $412,000 after recording $86,000 of federal income tax expense; $30,000 of client meals expense, of which 50% is deductible; $4,000 of fines paid to a government agency; $9,000 of interest on state bonds; and tax depreciation that exceeds book depreciation by $22,000. What is Halden's taxable income before the net operating loss deduction and special deductions?","d":"","o":["$400,000","$486,000","$501,000","$530,000"],"a":2,"cite":"Form 1120 Schedule M-1; IRC §162(f); IRC §274(n)(1); IRC §275(a)(1)","rat":"Start with book income of $412,000 and add back federal income tax ($86,000), the disallowed 50% of meals ($15,000) and the fines ($4,000).","ex":["Leaves out the $86,000 of federal income tax expense. Federal income tax recorded in the books is not deductible and must be added back.","Start with book income of $412,000 and add back federal income tax ($86,000), the disallowed 50% of meals ($15,000) and the fines ($4,000). Subtract tax-exempt interest ($9,000) and the excess tax depreciation ($22,000): $486,000.","Adds back all $30,000 of meals. Only the nondeductible 50% ($15,000) is added back.","Adds the excess tax depreciation instead of subtracting it. Larger tax depreciation lowers taxable income relative to book income."],"wt":["calc","","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Schedule M-1 adds back federal income tax, disallowed meals and fines, and subtracts tax-exempt interest and excess tax depreciation.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Book income 412,000","Add federal income tax expense 86,000, nondeductible meals 30,000 x 50% = 15,000, fines 4,000","Subtract tax-exempt interest 9,000 and excess tax depreciation 22,000","412,000 + 86,000 + 15,000 + 4,000 - 9,000 - 22,000 = 486,000"],"gd":[["Which book items are added back?","Federal income tax 86,000, the disallowed half of meals 30,000 x 50% = 15,000, and fines 4,000."],["Which items are subtracted?","Tax-exempt interest 9,000 and excess tax depreciation 22,000."],["What is taxable income?","412,000 + 86,000 + 15,000 + 4,000 - 9,000 - 22,000 = 486,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-A-0003","t":"For 2026, Tallis Marine, Inc. reported net income per books of $250,000 after deducting $60,000 of federal income tax expense, a $12,000 capital loss on the sale of stock (it had no capital gains), and $5,000 of premiums on a life insurance policy covering its president, for which the corporation is the beneficiary. It had no other book-tax differences. What is Tallis's taxable income before the net operating loss deduction and special deductions?","mc":{"s":"For 2026, Tallis Marine, Inc. reported net income per books of $250,000 after deducting $60,000 of federal income tax expense, a $12,000 capital loss on the sale of stock (it had no capital gains), and $5,000 of premiums on a life insurance policy covering its president, for which the corporation is the beneficiary. It had no other book-tax differences. What is Tallis's taxable income before the net operating loss deduction and special deductions?","d":"","o":["$267,000","$315,000","$322,000","$327,000"],"a":4,"cite":"Form 1120 Schedule M-1; IRC §1211(a); IRC §264(a)(1); IRC §275","rat":"Add back federal income tax ($60,000), the capital loss that exceeds capital gains ($12,000), and the officer life insurance premiums ($5,000): $327,000.","ex":["Omits the federal income tax add-back. Federal income tax expense is not deductible in computing taxable income.","Fails to add back the capital loss. A corporation may deduct capital losses only against capital gains, so the $12,000 is not deductible this year.","Fails to add back the life insurance premiums. Premiums are nondeductible when the corporation is the direct or indirect beneficiary.","Add back federal income tax ($60,000), the capital loss that exceeds capital gains ($12,000), and the officer life insurance premiums ($5,000): $327,000."],"wt":["calc","calc","calc",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Taxable income adds back federal tax, capital losses exceeding capital gains and premiums on policies where the corporation is beneficiary.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Book income 250,000","Add federal income tax 60,000, excess capital loss 12,000, officer life insurance premiums 5,000","250,000 + 60,000 + 12,000 + 5,000 = 327,000"],"gd":[["Which items are added back to book income?","Federal income tax 60,000, capital loss over capital gains 12,000, and officer life insurance premiums 5,000."],["What is the total?","250,000 + 60,000 + 12,000 + 5,000 = 327,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-A-0004","t":"Rowan Fabrication, Inc.'s preparer drafted its 2026 Schedule M-1 as shown. Rowan has no other differences. Which correction is needed?","mc":{"s":"Rowan Fabrication, Inc.'s preparer drafted its 2026 Schedule M-1 as shown. Rowan has no other differences. Which correction is needed?","d":"","o":["Remove the $8,000 of client entertainment from the add-backs, because entertainment expense is deductible at 50%","Add the $6,000 of tax-exempt interest instead of subtracting it; taxable income becomes $424,000","Remove the $70,000 of federal income tax per books, because income tax expense is deductible on the return","Subtract the $40,000 of excess tax depreciation instead of adding it; taxable income becomes $332,000"],"a":4,"cite":"Form 1120 Schedule M-1; IRC §274(a); IRC §275(a)(1)","rat":"Tax depreciation above book depreciation lowers taxable income, so it is subtracted.","ex":["Business entertainment is not deductible at all after 2017, so the $8,000 add-back is correct. Only business meals are 50% deductible.","Tax-exempt interest is in book income but excluded from taxable income, so it is properly subtracted.","Federal income taxes are not deductible by the corporation, so the book expense must be added back. The $70,000 line is correct.","Tax depreciation above book depreciation lowers taxable income, so it is subtracted. Correct line 10: $300,000 + $70,000 + $8,000 - $6,000 - $40,000 = $332,000."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"A","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Excess tax depreciation over book depreciation is subtracted on Schedule M-1; entertainment, federal tax and tax-exempt interest are treated correctly.","tb":[["Draft Schedule M-1 line","Amount"],["Net income per books","$300,000"],["Federal income tax per books (add)","$70,000"],["Client entertainment expense (add)","$8,000"],["Excess of tax depreciation over book depreciation (add)","$40,000"],["Tax-exempt interest (subtract)","$6,000"],["Income on line 10","$412,000"]],"from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Correct line 10 = 300,000 + 70,000 + 8,000 - 40,000 - 6,000 = 332,000","Draft added the depreciation, overstating income by 2 x 40,000 = 80,000 (412,000 - 332,000)"],"gd":[["Does excess tax depreciation raise or lower taxable income relative to book?","It lowers it, so it is subtracted."],["What is the correct line 10?","300,000 + 70,000 + 8,000 - 6,000 - 40,000 = 332,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-A-0005","t":"Which corporation must file Schedule M-3 instead of Schedule M-1 with its 2026 Form 1120?","mc":{"s":"Which corporation must file Schedule M-3 instead of Schedule M-1 with its 2026 Form 1120?","d":"","o":["Palo Foods, Inc., with total assets of $8.1 million and net income of $2 million","Denali Design, Inc., with total assets of $9.9 million and gross receipts of $30 million","Corbin Labs, Inc., with total assets of $6 million and three shareholders","Ridgeway Manufacturing, Inc., with total assets of $12.4 million at year-end"],"a":4,"cite":"Form 1120 instructions (Schedules M-1 and M-3); Form 1120 Schedule M-3 instructions","rat":"A corporation with total assets of $10 million or more at the end of the year must file Schedule M-3.","ex":["The requirement is based on total assets, not net income. Total assets under $10 million means Schedule M-1.","Gross receipts do not trigger the requirement, and $9.9 million of assets is below the $10 million threshold.","Neither the assets nor the number of shareholders reaches the threshold, so it files Schedule M-1.","A corporation with total assets of $10 million or more at the end of the year must file Schedule M-3."],"wt":["limit","limit","limit",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"A","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A corporation with total assets of $10 million or more at year-end files Schedule M-3 instead of M-1.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["What asset size triggers Schedule M-3?","Total assets of 10 million or more at year end."],["Which corporation has that?","The one with 12.4 million; the others fall below it."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-A-0006","t":"Vireo Corp.'s 2026 book-tax differences were: $6,000 of penalties paid to a government agency, $9,000 of state bond interest, $20,000 by which tax depreciation exceeded book depreciation, $14,000 of warranty expense accrued for books but deductible only when paid, and $10,000 of business meals disallowed by the 50% limit. What total amount of these differences is temporary?","mc":{"s":"Vireo Corp.'s 2026 book-tax differences were: $6,000 of penalties paid to a government agency, $9,000 of state bond interest, $20,000 by which tax depreciation exceeded book depreciation, $14,000 of warranty expense accrued for books but deductible only when paid, and $10,000 of business meals disallowed by the 50% limit. What total amount of these differences is temporary?","d":"","o":["$20,000","$25,000","$34,000","$59,000"],"a":3,"cite":"Form 1120 Schedule M-3 instructions; IRC §162(f); IRC §274(n)(1)","rat":"Only the depreciation difference ($20,000) and the warranty accrual ($14,000) reverse in later years, so $34,000 is temporary.","ex":["Counts only the depreciation difference. The warranty accrual also reverses when the warranty claims are paid, so it is temporary too.","This is the total of the permanent differences (penalties, state bond interest, and disallowed meals), not the temporary ones.","Only the depreciation difference ($20,000) and the warranty accrual ($14,000) reverse in later years, so $34,000 is temporary.","Adds all five differences. Three of them never reverse and are permanent."],"wt":["calc","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Temporary differences reverse later, such as depreciation and warranty accruals; fines, exempt interest and disallowed meals are permanent.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Permanent: penalties 6,000, state bond interest 9,000, meals 10,000","Temporary: depreciation 20,000, warranty accrual 14,000","20,000 + 14,000 = 34,000"],"gd":[["Which listed differences reverse in later years?","Depreciation timing and the warranty accrual."],["What is their sum?","20,000 + 14,000 = 34,000, while penalties, state bond interest and disallowed meals are permanent."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-A-0007","t":"Brightwater Ltd., a C corporation, reported 2026 net income per books of $500,000 after recording $105,000 of federal income tax expense and an $18,000 provision to its allowance for doubtful accounts. Actual bad debt write-offs of specific accounts were $11,000. In December 2026 it received $30,000 of prepaid income that it must include in taxable income in 2026 but will recognize in book income in 2027. What is taxable income before the net operating loss deduction and special deductions?","mc":{"s":"Brightwater Ltd., a C corporation, reported 2026 net income per books of $500,000 after recording $105,000 of federal income tax expense and an $18,000 provision to its allowance for doubtful accounts. Actual bad debt write-offs of specific accounts were $11,000. In December 2026 it received $30,000 of prepaid income that it must include in taxable income in 2026 but will recognize in book income in 2027. What is taxable income before the net operating loss deduction and special deductions?","d":"","o":["$612,000","$624,000","$642,000","$653,000"],"a":3,"cite":"Form 1120 Schedule M-1; IRC §166(a); IRC §451(c)","rat":"Add back tax expense ($105,000) and the reserve provision ($18,000), add the prepaid income ($30,000), and subtract the actual write-offs ($11,000): $642,000.","ex":["Omits the $30,000 of prepaid income that is taxable in 2026 but not yet in book income.","Leaves out the $18,000 provision. Reserve additions are not deductible; only actual write-offs of specific accounts are.","Add back tax expense ($105,000) and the reserve provision ($18,000), add the prepaid income ($30,000), and subtract the actual write-offs ($11,000): $642,000.","Does not subtract the $11,000 of specific write-offs, which are deductible on the return."],"wt":["calc","calc","","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Add back federal tax and reserve additions, add taxable prepaid income and subtract actual specific write-offs.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Book income 500,000","Add federal income tax 105,000, reserve provision 18,000, income taxed but not in books 30,000","Subtract specific write-offs 11,000","500,000 + 105,000 + 18,000 + 30,000 - 11,000 = 642,000"],"gd":[["What are the add-backs and adjustments to book income?","Add federal tax 105,000, reserve provision 18,000, and prepaid income 30,000."],["What is subtracted?","Actual specific write-offs of 11,000."],["What is taxable income?","500,000 + 105,000 + 18,000 + 30,000 - 11,000 = 642,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-A-0008","t":"Which item is reported as an addition to net income per books on Schedule M-1?","mc":{"s":"Which item is reported as an addition to net income per books on Schedule M-1?","d":"","o":["Tax-exempt interest included in book income","Excess of tax depreciation over book depreciation","Life insurance proceeds on a deceased officer that are included in book income","Federal income tax expense recorded in the books"],"a":4,"cite":"Form 1120 Schedule M-1; IRC §275(a)(1)","rat":"Federal income tax expense reduces book income but is not deductible, so it is added back.","ex":["Tax-exempt interest is in book income but is not taxable, so it is subtracted.","The extra tax deduction was not charged against book income, so it is subtracted.","The proceeds are excluded from taxable income, so they are subtracted from book income.","Federal income tax expense reduces book income but is not deductible, so it is added back."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"A","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Federal income tax expense in the books is added back on Schedule M-1.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Which items are added to book income on Schedule M-1?","Expenses recorded in books that are not deductible or not yet deductible."],["Which listed item is such an expense?","Federal income tax expense; the others are subtractions."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-A-0009","t":"Marlow Textiles, Inc. paid $3,000 of annual dues to a country club for its executives and deducted them in its books. What is the tax treatment of the dues?","mc":{"s":"Marlow Textiles, Inc. paid $3,000 of annual dues to a country club for its executives and deducted them in its books. What is the tax treatment of the dues?","d":"","o":["Deductible at 50% because the payment is a business meal","Deductible in full because the club is used for business purposes","Deductible only against the officers' compensation for those executives","Nondeductible in full, so the amount is added back on Schedule M-1"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §274(a)(3); Form 1120 Schedule M-1","rat":"Club dues are not deductible at all.","ex":["Dues are not a meal; the 50% limit applies to qualifying business meals, not club dues.","Business use does not make dues deductible; club dues are specifically disallowed.","There is no such rule; the dues are nondeductible to the corporation.","Club dues are not deductible at all. Because the payment reduced book income, it is added back."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"A","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Country club dues are nondeductible in full and are added back on Schedule M-1.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Are club dues deductible?","No, not at all."],["How is the book deduction handled on Schedule M-1?","The full 3,000 is added back."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-A-0010","t":"Tamsin Realty Corp. bought an apartment building in 2026 for use in its rental business. Over what period does MACRS recover the cost of the building?","mc":{"s":"Tamsin Realty Corp. bought an apartment building in 2026 for use in its rental business. Over what period does MACRS recover the cost of the building?","d":"","o":["39 years using the straight-line method","40 years using the alternative depreciation system","15 years using the 150% declining balance method","27.5 years using the straight-line method"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §168(c); Pub 946","rat":"Residential rental property is depreciated straight-line over 27.5 years.","ex":["That is the recovery period for nonresidential real property such as an office building.","This applies only to property subject to the alternative depreciation system, which is not the general rule here.","Fifteen years applies to certain land improvements and qualified improvement property, not to a residential building.","Residential rental property is depreciated straight-line over 27.5 years."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"A","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Residential rental property is depreciated straight-line over 27.5 years.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["What class is an apartment building?","Residential rental property."],["What period and method apply?","27.5 years straight-line."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-A-0011","t":"For 2026, Alder Systems, Inc. had net income per books of $180,000 after recording $47,000 of federal income tax expense and $30,000 of cash charitable contributions paid in December. Under the 2026 rule, only the part of contributions that exceeds 1% of taxable income is deductible, and only the part that does not exceed 10% of taxable income, both measured on taxable income before the charitable deduction. The company has no other book-tax differences. What is Alder's taxable income before the net operating loss deduction and special deductions?","mc":{"s":"For 2026, Alder Systems, Inc. had net income per books of $180,000 after recording $47,000 of federal income tax expense and $30,000 of cash charitable contributions paid in December. Under the 2026 rule, only the part of contributions that exceeds 1% of taxable income is deductible, and only the part that does not exceed 10% of taxable income, both measured on taxable income before the charitable deduction. The company has no other book-tax differences. What is Alder's taxable income before the net operating loss deduction and special deductions?","d":"","o":["$189,000","$227,000","$233,870","$239,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §170(b)(2); Form 1120 instructions (line 19); Public Law P.L. 119-21 §70426; IRC §170(d)(2)","rat":"Taxable income before the charitable deduction is $257,000.","ex":["Fails to add back the $47,000 of federal income tax, understating taxable income before the charitable deduction.","Treats all $30,000 as deductible. Only the band between the 1% floor and the 10% ceiling is deductible.","Taxable income before the charitable deduction is $257,000. The ceiling is 10% x $257,000 = $25,700 and the floor is 1% x $257,000 = $2,570. Contributions ($30,000) exceed the ceiling, so the deduction is $25,700 - $2,570 = $23,130 and taxable income is $257,000 - $23,130 = $233,870.","Applies the 10% ceiling to book income instead of taxable income before the charitable deduction."],"wt":["calc","calc","","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Corporate charitable deductions are limited to contributions above 1% and up to 10% of taxable income before the deduction.","from":"2026-07-01","calc":1,"steps":["Taxable income before charity = 180,000 + 47,000 + 30,000 = 257,000","Floor 1% x 257,000 = 2,570; ceiling 10% x 257,000 = 25,700","Contributions 30,000 exceed the ceiling, so deduction = 25,700 - 2,570 = 23,130 (section 170(b)(2)(A))","Taxable income = 257,000 - 23,130 = 233,870"],"gd":[["What is taxable income before the charitable deduction?","180,000 + 47,000 + 30,000 = 257,000."],["What are the ceiling and the floor?","10% x 257,000 = 25,700 and 1% x 257,000 = 2,570."],["What is the deduction and taxable income?","25,700 - 2,570 = 23,130, so 257,000 - 23,130 = 233,870."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-A-0012","t":"In 2026 Cobalt Machining, Inc. placed in service $300,000 of qualifying equipment acquired after January 19, 2025, that qualifies for 100% bonus depreciation, and it did not elect out. For books it uses straight-line over 5 years with a half-year convention (first-year book depreciation $30,000). Book net income was $600,000 after $126,000 of federal income tax expense. It has no other differences. What is taxable income before the net operating loss deduction and special deductions?","mc":{"s":"In 2026 Cobalt Machining, Inc. placed in service $300,000 of qualifying equipment acquired after January 19, 2025, that qualifies for 100% bonus depreciation, and it did not elect out. For books it uses straight-line over 5 years with a half-year convention (first-year book depreciation $30,000). Book net income was $600,000 after $126,000 of federal income tax expense. It has no other differences. What is taxable income before the net operating loss deduction and special deductions?","d":"","o":["$330,000","$426,000","$456,000","$996,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §168(k); Form 1120 Schedule M-1; Pub 946","rat":"Add back tax expense ($126,000) and subtract the excess of tax over book depreciation ($300,000 - $30,000 = $270,000): $456,000.","ex":["Does not add back federal income tax expense.","Subtracts the full $300,000 tax depreciation without adding back the $30,000 already deducted in book income.","Add back tax expense ($126,000) and subtract the excess of tax over book depreciation ($300,000 - $30,000 = $270,000): $456,000.","Adds the excess tax depreciation. Bonus depreciation lowers taxable income and is subtracted."],"wt":["calc","calc","","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Bonus depreciation lowers taxable income; subtract the excess of tax over book depreciation and add back federal tax.","from":"2026-07-01","calc":1,"steps":["Excess tax depreciation = 300,000 - 30,000 = 270,000","600,000 + 126,000 - 270,000 = 456,000"],"gd":[["How much tax depreciation exceeds book depreciation?","300,000 - 30,000 = 270,000."],["What is taxable income?","600,000 + 126,000 - 270,000 = 456,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-A-0013","t":"Ferrand Freight, Inc., a calendar-year corporation, is completing Schedule M-3. Its preparer classified certain 2026 differences as shown. Which classification is incorrect?","mc":{"s":"Ferrand Freight, Inc., a calendar-year corporation, is completing Schedule M-3. Its preparer classified certain 2026 differences as shown. Which classification is incorrect?","d":"","o":["Interest on state bonds is a temporary difference, not a permanent one, because the interest is later taxed on sale","The accrued warranty expense is permanent, not temporary, because the accrual is not deducted in any year","The disallowed 50% of business meals is temporary, not permanent, because it may be deducted in a later year","Penalties paid to a government agency are permanent, not temporary, because they are not deductible in any year"],"a":4,"cite":"Form 1120 Schedule M-3 instructions; IRC §162(f)","rat":"The penalties will never be deductible in any year, so nothing reverses.","ex":["The draft already shows it as permanent, which is correct; the interest is never taxed.","The draft shows it as temporary, which is correct; it is deducted in a later year when paid.","The draft shows it as permanent, which is correct; the disallowed part is never deducted.","The penalties will never be deductible in any year, so nothing reverses. This is a permanent difference and the draft is wrong."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Penalties paid to a government agency are permanent differences because they are never deductible.","tb":[["Item","Preparer's classification"],["Interest on state bonds","Permanent"],["Warranty expense accrued but not yet paid","Temporary"],["Penalties paid to a government agency","Temporary"],["Disallowed 50% of business meals","Permanent"]],"from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Which classification pairs a never-deductible item with the wrong label?","Penalties paid to a government agency, which never become deductible."],["What is the right classification?","Permanent, because nothing reverses."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-A-0014","t":"Preparing Norwood Steel, Inc.'s 2026 Form 1120, a reviewer compares the adjusted trial balance with the preparer's book-tax adjustments. The preparer already added back the fines and 50% of the meals and made no other adjustments. Which omitted adjustment is required?","mc":{"s":"Preparing Norwood Steel, Inc.'s 2026 Form 1120, a reviewer compares the adjusted trial balance with the preparer's book-tax adjustments. The preparer already added back the fines and 50% of the meals and made no other adjustments. Which omitted adjustment is required?","d":"","o":["Add back the full $10,000 of business meals, not only 50%","Add back $9,000 of bad debt expense from specific accounts written off as uncollectible","Add back $5,000 of salaries paid to the officers because compensation is a permanent difference","Add back $7,000 of premiums on officer life insurance policies for which the corporation is the beneficiary"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §264(a); IRC §162(a); Form 1120 Schedule M-1","rat":"Premiums on a policy for which the corporation is the beneficiary are nondeductible, and the preparer did not add them back.","ex":["Only 50% of qualifying business meals is nondeductible, and the preparer already added it back.","Specific write-offs of business receivables are deductible when worthless, so no adjustment is needed.","Reasonable compensation is deductible, so no adjustment is required.","Premiums on a policy for which the corporation is the beneficiary are nondeductible, and the preparer did not add them back."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Premiums on officer life insurance for which the corporation is beneficiary are nondeductible and must be added back.","tb":[["Trial balance account (debit)","Amount"],["Officers' salaries","$5,000"],["Fines paid to a government agency","$3,000"],["Business meals with clients (50% deductible)","$10,000"],["Bad debt expense, specific accounts written off","$9,000"],["Life insurance premiums, officers (corporation is beneficiary)","$7,000"]],"from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Which trial balance accounts have not been adjusted yet?","Officer life insurance premiums of 7,000, bad debts and salaries."],["Which one is nondeductible when the corporation is beneficiary?","The 7,000 of premiums, which must be added back."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-A-0015","t":"Sable Instruments, Inc.'s draft 2026 Form 1120 shows gross income of $900,000, deductions other than charitable contributions of $480,000, a charitable contribution deduction of $60,000 for cash gifts paid in 2026, and taxable income of $360,000. The corporation has no other items. Under the 2026 rule, only contributions above 1% of taxable income are deductible, and only the part that does not exceed 10% of taxable income, both measured on taxable income before the deduction. What correction should the reviewer make?","mc":{"s":"Sable Instruments, Inc.'s draft 2026 Form 1120 shows gross income of $900,000, deductions other than charitable contributions of $480,000, a charitable contribution deduction of $60,000 for cash gifts paid in 2026, and taxable income of $360,000. The corporation has no other items. Under the 2026 rule, only contributions above 1% of taxable income are deductible, and only the part that does not exceed 10% of taxable income, both measured on taxable income before the deduction. What correction should the reviewer make?","d":"","o":["Limit the deduction to $37,800, making taxable income $382,200, and carry forward the $22,200 not deducted","Limit the deduction to $36,000 (10% of draft taxable income), making taxable income $384,000","Make no correction, because a corporation may deduct all charitable gifts made in cash","Limit the deduction to $37,800 and carry back the $22,200 not deducted to the two prior years"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §170(b)(2); IRC §170(d)(2); Form 1120 instructions (line 19); Public Law P.L. 119-21 §70426","rat":"Income before the charitable deduction is $420,000.","ex":["Income before the charitable deduction is $420,000. The ceiling is $42,000 and the floor is $4,200, so the deductible band is $42,000 - $4,200 = $37,800 and taxable income is $382,200. The $22,200 not deducted ($18,000 over the ceiling plus the $4,200 floor amount) carries forward up to five years, because the floor amount carries over when contributions exceed the ceiling.","Applies the 10% limit to income after the charitable deduction. The base is taxable income computed before the deduction.","Corporate charitable deductions are subject to a 1% floor and a 10% ceiling, so the full $60,000 is not deductible.","Excess corporate contributions, including the floor amount, carry forward five years; they are not carried back."],"wt":["","calc","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"A","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Corporate charitable deductions are limited to 10% of taxable income before the deduction with a 1% floor; excess carries forward five years.","from":"2026-07-01","calc":1,"steps":["Income before charity = 900,000 - 480,000 = 420,000","Floor 1% x 420,000 = 4,200; ceiling 10% x 420,000 = 42,000","Deduction = 42,000 - 4,200 = 37,800; corrected taxable income = 420,000 - 37,800 = 382,200","Carryforward = 60,000 - 37,800 = 22,200 (18,000 over the ceiling + 4,200 floor amount, section 170(d)(2)(A), (C))"],"gd":[["What is income before the charitable deduction?","900,000 - 480,000 = 420,000."],["What are the ceiling, floor and deductible band?","42,000 and 4,200, so 42,000 - 4,200 = 37,800."],["What is taxable income and the carryforward?","420,000 - 37,800 = 382,200, and 60,000 - 37,800 = 22,200 carries forward."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-A-0016","t":"Two corporations each buy $200,000 of equipment in 2026 that qualifies for 100% bonus depreciation. Kiln Co. claims the bonus depreciation. Larch Co. elects out of bonus depreciation and uses regular MACRS, under which first-year depreciation is 20% of cost. Ignoring all other items, by how much is Kiln's 2026 taxable income lower than Larch's?","mc":{"s":"Two corporations each buy $200,000 of equipment in 2026 that qualifies for 100% bonus depreciation. Kiln Co. claims the bonus depreciation. Larch Co. elects out of bonus depreciation and uses regular MACRS, under which first-year depreciation is 20% of cost. Ignoring all other items, by how much is Kiln's 2026 taxable income lower than Larch's?","d":"","o":["$40,000","$160,000","$200,000","$240,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §168(k); Pub 946","rat":"Kiln deducts $200,000; Larch deducts $40,000.","ex":["Uses Larch's first-year deduction as the difference; it is Kiln's excess deduction that matters.","Kiln deducts $200,000; Larch deducts $40,000. Kiln's taxable income is $160,000 lower in 2026, a timing difference that reverses in later years.","Compares Kiln's deduction with no deduction. Larch still deducts 20% ($40,000) in the first year.","Adds the two deductions instead of subtracting them."],"wt":["calc","","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Bonus depreciation accelerates the deduction, so taxable income is lower by the excess over regular first-year depreciation.","from":"2026-07-01","calc":1,"steps":["Kiln deduction = 200,000","Larch deduction = 20% x 200,000 = 40,000","Difference = 200,000 - 40,000 = 160,000"],"gd":[["What does Kiln deduct?","200,000 with bonus."],["What does Larch deduct with regular MACRS?","20% x 200,000 = 40,000."],["What is the difference in taxable income?","200,000 - 40,000 = 160,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-A-0017","t":"Quill Publishing, Inc. bought a $500,000 life insurance policy on its chief executive in 2023, naming the corporation as beneficiary, and paid $9,000 of premiums in 2026. The chief executive died in 2026 and the corporation received $500,000 of proceeds. Which statement correctly describes the effect on the 2026 book-tax reconciliation?","mc":{"s":"Quill Publishing, Inc. bought a $500,000 life insurance policy on its chief executive in 2023, naming the corporation as beneficiary, and paid $9,000 of premiums in 2026. The chief executive died in 2026 and the corporation received $500,000 of proceeds. Which statement correctly describes the effect on the 2026 book-tax reconciliation?","d":"","o":["Both items are temporary differences that reverse when the policy is surrendered","Only the premiums are adjusted; the proceeds are taxable income and need no adjustment","Only the proceeds are adjusted; the premiums are deductible business expenses","Both items are permanent differences: add back the $9,000 premiums and subtract the $500,000 proceeds"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §264(a)(1); IRC §101(a); Form 1120 Schedule M-1; IRC §101(j)","rat":"Premiums are nondeductible and proceeds are excluded, so neither reverses.","ex":["Neither item reverses in a later year; the policy ended with the death, so both are permanent.","Life insurance proceeds paid by reason of death are generally excluded, so a subtraction is needed.","Premiums on a policy under which the corporation is beneficiary are not deductible, so they are added back.","Premiums are nondeductible and proceeds are excluded, so neither reverses. Both are permanent differences."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"A","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Life insurance premiums where the corporation is beneficiary are nondeductible and death proceeds are excluded, both permanent differences.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Are the corporation's premiums on a policy it benefits from deductible?","No, so 9,000 is added back."],["Are the death proceeds taxable?","No, so 500,000 is subtracted; both are permanent."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-A-0018","t":"Taber Outdoor, Inc.'s books show net income of $300,000 and its filed 2026 return shows taxable income of $331,000 after adding $63,000 of federal income tax expense and subtracting $8,000 of state bond interest and $24,000 of excess tax depreciation. In reviewing, the CPA finds that book expenses include $10,000 of fines that the preparer did not add back. What is the corrected taxable income?","mc":{"s":"Taber Outdoor, Inc.'s books show net income of $300,000 and its filed 2026 return shows taxable income of $331,000 after adding $63,000 of federal income tax expense and subtracting $8,000 of state bond interest and $24,000 of excess tax depreciation. In reviewing, the CPA finds that book expenses include $10,000 of fines that the preparer did not add back. What is the corrected taxable income?","d":"","o":["$321,000","$331,000","$341,000","$351,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §162(f); Form 1120 Schedule M-1","rat":"The return already reconciles to $331,000; adding back the nondeductible $10,000 fines gives $341,000.","ex":["Subtracts the fines. Fines reduced book income but are not deductible, so they are added back.","This is the taxable income shown on the filed return; it omits the fines add-back.","The return already reconciles to $331,000; adding back the nondeductible $10,000 fines gives $341,000.","Adds the fines twice instead of once, overstating taxable income by $10,000."],"wt":["calc","calc","","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Fines are nondeductible and added back to taxable income, adding to the amount already reconciled.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Return before correction = 300,000 + 63,000 - 8,000 - 24,000 = 331,000","Add nondeductible fines 10,000","Corrected taxable income = 341,000"],"gd":[["What does the filed return already reconcile to?","300,000 + 63,000 - 8,000 - 24,000 = 331,000."],["What else must be added?","Nondeductible fines of 10,000."],["What is the corrected income?","341,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-A-0019","t":"Draft 2026 Schedule M-1 additions for Kelso Machine Works, Inc. are shown. The corporation is a calendar-year C corporation with no other differences. Which addition is incorrect?","mc":{"s":"Draft 2026 Schedule M-1 additions for Kelso Machine Works, Inc. are shown. The corporation is a calendar-year C corporation with no other differences. Which addition is incorrect?","d":"","o":["The $12,000 of state income tax expense should not be added back, because state income taxes are deductible","The $40,000 of federal income tax expense should not be added back, because income tax is deductible","The $5,000 of nondeductible meals should not be added back, because meals are deductible","The $2,000 of fines should not be added back, because fines are ordinary business expenses"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §164(a)(3); IRC §275(a)(1); IRC §162(f); Form 1120 Schedule M-1","rat":"A corporation may deduct state and local income taxes, so the book expense creates no difference.","ex":["A corporation may deduct state and local income taxes, so the book expense creates no difference.","Federal income tax is not deductible, so the add-back is correct.","The 50% of business meals that is not deductible must be added back, so the add-back is correct.","Fines paid to a government are nondeductible, so the add-back is correct."],"wt":["","trap","trap","trap"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"State income tax expense is deductible by a corporation, so it is not added back on Schedule M-1; other add-backs are correct.","tb":[["Draft M-1 addition","Amount"],["Federal income tax per books","$40,000"],["State income tax expense per books","$12,000"],["Nondeductible portion of business meals","$5,000"],["Fines paid to a government agency","$2,000"]],"from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Which book expenses are nondeductible and added back?","Federal income tax, disallowed meals and fines."],["Is state income tax an addition?","No, corporations deduct state income taxes, so the 12,000 add-back is wrong."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-A-1001","t":"Pinewood Fabrication, Inc., a calendar-year C corporation, drafted its 2026 Schedule M-1 as shown. Its client meals are 50% deductible, the interest on the loan was used to buy the state bonds, and the dividends-received deduction is claimed on Schedule C. Compared with the correct amount, how is the draft's income before the net operating loss deduction and special deductions misstated?","mc":{"s":"Pinewood Fabrication, Inc., a calendar-year C corporation, drafted its 2026 Schedule M-1 as shown. Its client meals are 50% deductible, the interest on the loan was used to buy the state bonds, and the dividends-received deduction is claimed on Schedule C. Compared with the correct amount, how is the draft's income before the net operating loss deduction and special deductions misstated?","d":"","o":["Overstated by $4,000","Understated by $17,000","Overstated by $13,000","Understated by $4,000"],"a":4,"cite":"Form 1120 Schedule M-1 instructions; IRC §274(n)(1); IRC §265(a)(2); IRC §162(f); IRC §243(a)","rat":"Correct income is $595,000 (book income $480,000 + $101,000 + half of meals $13,000 + fines $4,000 - $8,000 + the $5,000 disallowed interest); the draft reaches $591,000, so it is $4,000 too low.","ex":["Reverses the direction. The draft's net errors (meals overadded $13,000, interest omitted $5,000, dividends-received deduction subtracted $12,000) make its total lower than correct, not higher.","Counts only the interest omission ($5,000) and the improper dividends-received deduction ($12,000). It misses that the draft added all $26,000 of meals when only $13,000 is nondeductible.","Counts only the meals error. It overlooks the omitted $5,000 interest add-back and the $12,000 dividends-received deduction, which is not a Schedule M-1 item.","Correct income is $595,000 (book income $480,000 + $101,000 + half of meals $13,000 + fines $4,000 - $8,000 + the $5,000 disallowed interest); the draft reaches $591,000, so it is $4,000 too low."],"wt":["calc","calc","calc",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"A","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"M-1 adds back federal tax, fines, half of meals and interest on loans that carry tax-exempt bonds; the dividends-received deduction is a Schedule C special deduction, not an M-1 item.","tb":[["Item (2026)","Amount","Draft treatment"],["Net income per books","$480,000","Starting point"],["Federal income tax expense","$101,000","Added"],["Client meals expense","$26,000","Added in full"],["Fines paid to a government agency","$4,000","Added"],["Interest on state bonds","$8,000","Subtracted"],["Interest on the loan used to buy the state bonds","$5,000","No adjustment"],["Dividends-received deduction","$12,000","Subtracted"]],"from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Draft: 480,000 + 101,000 + 26,000 + 4,000 - 8,000 - 12,000 = 591,000","Correct: 480,000 + 101,000 + 13,000 (50% of 26,000) + 4,000 - 8,000 + 5,000 (interest on loan carrying state bonds) = 595,000; no dividends-received deduction on M-1","595,000 - 591,000 = 4,000, so the draft is understated"],"gd":[["What does the draft compute?","480,000 + 101,000 + 26,000 + 4,000 - 8,000 - 12,000 = 591,000."],["Which draft items are wrong?","Only 50% of meals is added back, 13,000; the interest on the loan carrying the state bonds is disallowed and added back, 5,000; and the dividends-received deduction is not an M-1 line."],["What is the correct amount and the error?","480,000 + 101,000 + 13,000 + 4,000 - 8,000 + 5,000 = 595,000, so the draft is 595,000 - 591,000 = 4,000 too low."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-A-1002","t":"Arbor Labs, Inc. and Birch Labs, Inc. are calendar-year C corporations. Each expensed $300,000 of research costs in 2026 on its books, each has $1,000,000 of income before those costs on its books, and neither has any other book-tax difference. Arbor performed all of its research in the United States. Birch performed all of its research in a foreign country. Neither makes any special election, and amortization of any capitalized costs begins at the midpoint of the year. By how much does Birch's 2026 taxable income exceed Arbor's?","mc":{"s":"Arbor Labs, Inc. and Birch Labs, Inc. are calendar-year C corporations. Each expensed $300,000 of research costs in 2026 on its books, each has $1,000,000 of income before those costs on its books, and neither has any other book-tax difference. Arbor performed all of its research in the United States. Birch performed all of its research in a foreign country. Neither makes any special election, and amortization of any capitalized costs begins at the midpoint of the year. By how much does Birch's 2026 taxable income exceed Arbor's?","d":"","o":["$0","$270,000","$290,000","$300,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §174A(a); IRC §174(a)(2)(B); Form 1120 Schedule M-1 instructions","rat":"Arbor deducts all $300,000, for taxable income of $700,000.","ex":["Treats both alike. Only domestic research is currently deductible; foreign research is capitalized.","Uses the 5-year mid-year amortization that applied to domestic research before 2025. Foreign research is amortized over 15 years.","Arbor deducts all $300,000, for taxable income of $700,000. Birch must capitalize and amortize over 15 years starting at mid-year: $300,000 x 6/180 = $10,000, for taxable income of $990,000. The difference is $290,000.","Assumes Birch gets no deduction at all in 2026. It gets the first-year mid-year amortization of $10,000."],"wt":["concept","limit","","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"A","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Domestic research costs are currently deductible under section 174A; foreign research costs are capitalized and amortized over 15 years from the midpoint of the year.","from":"2026-07-01","calc":1,"steps":["Arbor: 1,000,000 - 300,000 = 700,000","Birch amortization: 300,000 x (1/15) x 1/2 = 10,000; taxable income 1,000,000 - 10,000 = 990,000","990,000 - 700,000 = 290,000"],"gd":[["How is Arbor's domestic research treated?","Fully deducted, so taxable income is 1,000,000 - 300,000 = 700,000."],["How is foreign research treated?","Capitalized and amortized over 15 years from mid-year, 300,000 x 1/15 x 1/2 = 10,000, so taxable income is 990,000."],["What is the difference?","990,000 - 700,000 = 290,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-A-1003","t":"Cobble Creek Partners, a calendar-year partnership, reported 2026 net income per books of $300,000. That amount is after deducting $60,000 of guaranteed payments to partners for services (deductible for tax), an $8,000 cash charitable contribution and $6,000 of client meals (50% deductible), and it includes $10,000 of tax-exempt interest. Tax depreciation exceeded book depreciation by $20,000. There are no other differences. Which statement about the 2026 Form 1065 is correct?","mc":{"s":"Cobble Creek Partners, a calendar-year partnership, reported 2026 net income per books of $300,000. That amount is after deducting $60,000 of guaranteed payments to partners for services (deductible for tax), an $8,000 cash charitable contribution and $6,000 of client meals (50% deductible), and it includes $10,000 of tax-exempt interest. Tax depreciation exceeded book depreciation by $20,000. There are no other differences. Which statement about the 2026 Form 1065 is correct?","d":"","o":["Ordinary business income is $273,000; the contribution reduces ordinary income, and only the tax-exempt interest is separately stated","Ordinary business income is $291,000; the tax-exempt interest stays in ordinary income, and only the contribution is separately stated","Ordinary business income is $341,000; the guaranteed payments are separately stated and are not deducted in computing ordinary income","Ordinary business income is $281,000; the $8,000 contribution and the $10,000 of tax-exempt interest are separately stated items on Schedule K"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §703(a)(2); IRC §702(a); IRC §707(c); IRC §274(n)(1); Form 1065 Schedule M-1 instructions","rat":"Start with $300,000, remove the $10,000 tax-exempt interest (separately stated), add back the $8,000 contribution (separately stated) and the nondeductible half of meals ($3,000), and subtract the $20,000 of excess tax depreciation: $281,000.","ex":["Leaves the $8,000 charitable contribution inside ordinary income. Partnerships cannot deduct charitable gifts in ordinary income; each partner takes a share separately.","Keeps the tax-exempt interest in ordinary income. It is a separately stated item on Schedule K, so it comes out of ordinary income.","Adds back the guaranteed payments. Guaranteed payments for services are deductible in computing ordinary income (they are also reported to the recipient partner).","Start with $300,000, remove the $10,000 tax-exempt interest (separately stated), add back the $8,000 contribution (separately stated) and the nondeductible half of meals ($3,000), and subtract the $20,000 of excess tax depreciation: $281,000. The guaranteed payments stay deducted."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"A","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A partnership's ordinary income excludes separately stated items such as tax-exempt income and charitable gifts, but guaranteed payments for services remain deductible.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["300,000 - 10,000 (tax-exempt interest is separately stated) = 290,000","+ 8,000 (charitable contribution is separately stated) + 3,000 (nondeductible 50% of meals) = 301,000","- 20,000 (excess tax depreciation) = 281,000; guaranteed payments already deducted"],"gd":[["Which items leave ordinary income and are separately stated?","Tax-exempt interest 10,000 comes out; the charitable contribution 8,000 is added back."],["What other adjustments apply?","Add the nondeductible half of meals, 3,000, and subtract excess tax depreciation, 20,000."],["What is ordinary income and are guaranteed payments affected?","300,000 - 10,000 + 8,000 + 3,000 - 20,000 = 281,000, and guaranteed payments stay deducted."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-A-1004","t":"Kappa Logistics, Inc., a calendar-year C corporation, reported 2026 net income per books of $798,400. Its book income statement includes $40,000 of state bond interest and a federal income tax provision of $201,600, made up of $159,600 of current tax and $42,000 of deferred tax. For tax purposes, depreciation exceeds book depreciation by $200,000. There are no other differences. What is Kappa's 2026 taxable income before the net operating loss deduction and special deductions?","mc":{"s":"Kappa Logistics, Inc., a calendar-year C corporation, reported 2026 net income per books of $798,400. Its book income statement includes $40,000 of state bond interest and a federal income tax provision of $201,600, made up of $159,600 of current tax and $42,000 of deferred tax. For tax purposes, depreciation exceeds book depreciation by $200,000. There are no other differences. What is Kappa's 2026 taxable income before the net operating loss deduction and special deductions?","d":"","o":["$558,400","$718,000","$760,000","$960,000"],"a":3,"cite":"Form 1120 Schedule M-1 instructions; IRC §275(a)(1); IRC §103(a); IRC §168","rat":"The M-1 adds back the entire federal income tax per books, current and deferred ($201,600), then subtracts tax-exempt interest ($40,000) and the excess tax depreciation ($200,000): $760,000.","ex":["Omits the add-back of federal income tax expense. Federal income tax per books is never deductible, so the whole provision is added back.","Adds back only the current tax. The deferred tax is also part of federal income tax per books and must be added back.","The M-1 adds back the entire federal income tax per books, current and deferred ($201,600), then subtracts tax-exempt interest ($40,000) and the excess tax depreciation ($200,000): $760,000.","Forgets the $200,000 of excess tax depreciation, which reduces taxable income below book income."],"wt":["calc","concept","","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"A","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Schedule M-1 adds back the total federal income tax expense on the books, current plus deferred, before other differences.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["798,400 + 201,600 (total federal tax provision) = 1,000,000","- 40,000 (tax-exempt interest) - 200,000 (excess tax depreciation) = 760,000","Check: 760,000 x 21% = 159,600, equal to the current tax in the provision"],"gd":[["How much federal income tax per books is added back on the M-1?","The whole provision, current and deferred, 201,600."],["What is book income after that?","798,400 + 201,600 = 1,000,000."],["What is subtracted?","Tax-exempt interest 40,000 and excess tax depreciation 200,000, giving 760,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-A-1005","t":"Ridgeline Metals, Inc., an accrual-method calendar-year C corporation, reported 2026 net income per books of $700,000 after $147,000 of federal income tax expense and after accruing the year-end bonuses shown. Each bonus was fixed and unconditionally owed at December 31, 2026. All recipients use the cash method, and Ridgeline has no other book-tax differences. What is its 2026 taxable income before the net operating loss deduction and special deductions?","mc":{"s":"Ridgeline Metals, Inc., an accrual-method calendar-year C corporation, reported 2026 net income per books of $700,000 after $147,000 of federal income tax expense and after accruing the year-end bonuses shown. Each bonus was fixed and unconditionally owed at December 31, 2026. All recipients use the cash method, and Ridgeline has no other book-tax differences. What is its 2026 taxable income before the net operating loss deduction and special deductions?","d":"","o":["$730,000","$847,000","$877,000","$947,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §267(a)(2); IRC §267(b)(2); IRC §404(a)(5); Form 1120 Schedule M-1 instructions","rat":"Add back federal income tax ($147,000).","ex":["Adds back Dana's bonus but forgets to add back the $147,000 of federal income tax expense.","Ignores the related-party rule and treats all bonuses as deductible in 2026. Dana is a 60% shareholder, so the deduction waits until payment.","Add back federal income tax ($147,000). The production employees' bonuses ($70,000) were paid within 2.5 months and are deductible in 2026. Dana owns more than 50% of the stock, so her $30,000 bonus is deductible only when she includes it in income (2027): add it back. Total $877,000.","Adds back all bonuses. The 2.5-month rule keeps the production employees' $70,000 deductible in 2026."],"wt":["calc","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"A","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"An accrual-basis corporation cannot deduct an accrued amount owed to a cash-basis related person, such as a more-than-50% shareholder, until the year the person reports it, even if paid within 2.5 months.","tb":[["Recipient","Ownership of Ridgeline stock","Bonus accrued","Date paid"],["Production employees (group)","None","$70,000","March 1, 2027"],["Dana Ortiz, vice president","60%","$30,000","March 1, 2027"]],"from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Book income 700,000 + federal income tax 147,000 = 847,000","Production bonuses 70,000: paid Mar 1, 2027 (within 2.5 months), deductible 2026, no adjustment","Dana (60% shareholder, cash method): deduction waits until paid in 2027, add back 30,000","847,000 + 30,000 = 877,000"],"gd":[["What is book income plus the federal tax add-back?","700,000 + 147,000 = 847,000."],["Which bonuses are deductible in 2026?","Production bonuses paid within 2.5 months, 70,000, need no adjustment."],["What about the bonus to a more-than-50% shareholder who uses the cash method?","The deduction waits until paid, so 30,000 is added back, giving 847,000 + 30,000 = 877,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-A-1006","t":"Nimbus Analytics, Inc., a calendar-year C corporation, began business on October 1, 2026. Its adjusted trial balance shows that it expensed $60,000 of start-up costs and $20,000 of organizational costs in 2026, all incurred before it began business. It made no special elections. What amount should be added back on its 2026 Schedule M-1 for these costs?","mc":{"s":"Nimbus Analytics, Inc., a calendar-year C corporation, began business on October 1, 2026. Its adjusted trial balance shows that it expensed $60,000 of start-up costs and $20,000 of organizational costs in 2026, all incurred before it began business. It made no special elections. What amount should be added back on its 2026 Schedule M-1 for these costs?","d":"","o":["$68,833","$73,750","$74,000","$78,667"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §195(b)(1); IRC §248(a); Form 1120 Schedule M-1 instructions","rat":"Start-up costs of $60,000 exceed $50,000 by $10,000, which wipes out the $5,000 immediate deduction; only amortization of $60,000 x 3/180 = $1,000 is allowed.","ex":["Gives start-up costs the full $5,000 immediate deduction. The $5,000 is reduced dollar for dollar once start-up costs exceed $50,000, and here it is eliminated.","Start-up costs of $60,000 exceed $50,000 by $10,000, which wipes out the $5,000 immediate deduction; only amortization of $60,000 x 3/180 = $1,000 is allowed. Organizational costs of $20,000 allow $5,000 plus $15,000 x 3/180 = $250. Deduction $6,250, so add back $73,750.","Allows the $5,000 for organizational costs but skips the amortization of the remaining $15,000 and gives no amortization credit for it.","Amortizes all $80,000 over 180 months and never applies the $5,000 immediate deduction for organizational costs."],"wt":["limit","","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"A","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Start-up and organizational costs each get a $5,000 immediate deduction that phases out dollar for dollar above $50,000; the rest is amortized over 180 months from the month business begins.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Start-up 60,000 > 50,000 by 10,000, so immediate deduction = 5,000 - 10,000 -> 0; amortize 60,000 x 3/180 = 1,000","Organizational 20,000: immediate 5,000; remaining 15,000 x 3/180 = 250; total 5,250","Deduction 1,000 + 5,250 = 6,250; add back 80,000 - 6,250 = 73,750"],"gd":[["What happens to start-up costs over 50,000?","The excess 10,000 eliminates the 5,000 immediate deduction, so only amortization of 60,000 x 3/180 = 1,000 is allowed."],["What is allowed for organizational costs?","5,000 immediate plus 15,000 x 3/180 = 250, so 5,250."],["What is added back?","Allowed 1,000 + 5,250 = 6,250, so 80,000 - 6,250 = 73,750."]]}}],"2":[{"i":"REG-V-B-0001","t":"Elmwood Corp., a domestic C corporation, has taxable income of $500,000 for its 2026 calendar year. What is its federal income tax before credits?","mc":{"s":"Elmwood Corp., a domestic C corporation, has taxable income of $500,000 for its 2026 calendar year. What is its federal income tax before credits?","d":"","o":["$84,000","$105,000","$170,000","$175,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §11(b); Form 1120 instructions","rat":"Domestic C corporations pay a flat 21% on taxable income: 21% x $500,000 = $105,000.","ex":["Applies a 20% deduction for qualified business income. Corporations do not receive that deduction.","Domestic C corporations pay a flat 21% on taxable income: 21% x $500,000 = $105,000.","Uses the 34% top rate that applied before 2018.","Uses the 35% rate that applied to taxable income over $10 million before 2018."],"wt":["concept","","limit","limit"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Domestic C corporations pay a flat 21% federal tax on taxable income.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Flat rate 21%","21% x 500,000 = 105,000"],"gd":[["What rate applies to a domestic C corporation?","A flat 21%."],["What is the tax?","21% x 500,000 = 105,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0002","t":"Bristol Holdings, Inc., a calendar-year C corporation, received $100,000 of dividends in 2026 from a domestic corporation in which it owns 25% of the stock. The stock was held for more than a year, and taxable income before the dividends-received deduction is well above $300,000. What is the dividends-received deduction?","mc":{"s":"Bristol Holdings, Inc., a calendar-year C corporation, received $100,000 of dividends in 2026 from a domestic corporation in which it owns 25% of the stock. The stock was held for more than a year, and taxable income before the dividends-received deduction is well above $300,000. What is the dividends-received deduction?","d":"","o":["$50,000","$65,000","$80,000","$100,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §243(a); IRC §243(c); Form 1120 Schedule C","rat":"A corporation owning at least 20% but less than 80% of the payor deducts 65% of the dividend: $65,000.","ex":["Uses the 50% rate for less-than-20% ownership. At 25% ownership the 65% rate applies.","A corporation owning at least 20% but less than 80% of the payor deducts 65% of the dividend: $65,000.","Uses the 80% rate that applied to 20%-owned payors before 2018; the current rate is 65%.","The full deduction applies only to qualifying dividends within an affiliated group (80% or more ownership)."],"wt":["limit","","limit","limit"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The dividends-received deduction is 65% for 20% to under 80% ownership.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Ownership 25% is at least 20% and under 80%: rate 65%","65% x 100,000 = 65,000"],"gd":[["What ownership percentage does Bristol hold and which range is it in?","25%, which is at least 20% and under 80%."],["What dividends-received rate applies?","65%."],["What is the deduction?","65% x 100,000 = 65,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0003","t":"In 2026, Whitcomb Optics, Inc. received $200,000 of dividends from a domestic corporation in which it owns 10% of the stock. Whitcomb's taxable income computed before the dividends-received deduction is $150,000, and it has no net operating loss. Using the 50% dividends-received deduction rate, what is its dividends-received deduction?","mc":{"s":"In 2026, Whitcomb Optics, Inc. received $200,000 of dividends from a domestic corporation in which it owns 10% of the stock. Whitcomb's taxable income computed before the dividends-received deduction is $150,000, and it has no net operating loss. Using the 50% dividends-received deduction rate, what is its dividends-received deduction?","d":"","o":["$50,000","$75,000","$97,500","$100,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §246(b); IRC §243(a)(1); Form 1120 Schedule C; IRC §246(b)(3)","rat":"The deduction is the lesser of 50% of the dividends ($100,000) or 50% of taxable income before the deduction ($75,000).","ex":["This is the $50,000 of taxable income left after the full $100,000 deduction. It confuses the resulting taxable income with the deduction itself.","The deduction is the lesser of 50% of the dividends ($100,000) or 50% of taxable income before the deduction ($75,000). The $75,000 limit applies because the full deduction would not create a loss.","Applies the 65% rate to taxable income. The 65% rate applies only to 20%-owned payors, and Whitcomb owns 10%.","Ignores the taxable-income limit. The deduction cannot exceed 50% of taxable income computed before the deduction when no loss results."],"wt":["calc","","limit","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The DRD is the lesser of the percentage of dividends or the percentage of taxable income before the DRD, unless it creates a loss.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["50% x dividends 200,000 = 100,000","50% x taxable income before DRD 150,000 = 75,000","Full 100,000 would not create a loss (150,000 - 100,000 = 50,000), so limit applies: 75,000"],"gd":[["What are the two amounts compared for the dividends-received deduction at 50%?","50% of dividends, 50% x 200,000 = 100,000, and 50% of taxable income before the deduction, 50% x 150,000 = 75,000."],["Would the full deduction create a loss?","No, 150,000 - 100,000 = 50,000, so the income limit applies and the deduction is 75,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0004","t":"Lindqvist Capital, Inc. bought common shares of a domestic corporation 10 days before the ex-dividend date and sold them 20 days after that date. It held the shares for 30 days in total and received a $40,000 dividend. Which statement is correct about its dividends-received deduction?","mc":{"s":"Lindqvist Capital, Inc. bought common shares of a domestic corporation 10 days before the ex-dividend date and sold them 20 days after that date. It held the shares for 30 days in total and received a $40,000 dividend. Which statement is correct about its dividends-received deduction?","d":"","o":["No deduction is allowed, because the shares were not held for more than 45 days during the 91-day period around the ex-dividend date","A 50% deduction is allowed, because the shares were held for more than 30 days","A 65% deduction is allowed, because any holding period suffices for dividends from domestic corporations","The deduction is allowed but reduced in proportion to the days held"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §246(c)(1); Form 1120 Schedule C instructions","rat":"The stock must be held more than 45 days within the 91-day period beginning 45 days before the ex-dividend date.","ex":["The stock must be held more than 45 days within the 91-day period beginning 45 days before the ex-dividend date. Thirty days does not qualify.","There is no 30-day rule for common stock; the required holding period is more than 45 days within the 91-day window.","The dividends-received deduction is denied unless the holding requirement is met.","The deduction is not prorated; it is either allowed in full at the applicable rate or denied."],"wt":["","limit","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The DRD requires holding the stock more than 45 days within the 91-day period around the ex-dividend date.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["What holding period does the deduction require?","More than 45 days within the 91-day period around the ex-dividend date."],["Does 30 days satisfy that?","No, so no deduction is allowed."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0005","t":"Cascade Provisions, Inc. had these 2026 results: gross receipts from sales $2,400,000; cost of goods sold $1,300,000; salaries $500,000; depreciation $120,000; dividends of $40,000 from a domestic corporation in which it owns 10%; interest of $10,000 on state bonds; and cash contributions to charity of $25,000. Under the 2026 rule, a corporation deducts only the part of its contributions that exceeds 1% of taxable income, and the deduction cannot exceed 10% of taxable income, both measured before the charitable deduction and the dividends-received deduction. Using the 50% dividends-received deduction rate, what is Cascade's federal income tax liability?","mc":{"s":"Cascade Provisions, Inc. had these 2026 results: gross receipts from sales $2,400,000; cost of goods sold $1,300,000; salaries $500,000; depreciation $120,000; dividends of $40,000 from a domestic corporation in which it owns 10%; interest of $10,000 on state bonds; and cash contributions to charity of $25,000. Under the 2026 rule, a corporation deducts only the part of its contributions that exceeds 1% of taxable income, and the deduction cannot exceed 10% of taxable income, both measured before the charitable deduction and the dividends-received deduction. Using the 50% dividends-received deduction rate, what is Cascade's federal income tax liability?","d":"","o":["$99,750","$100,842","$102,942","$105,042"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §11(b); IRC §243(a); IRC §170(b)(2); IRC §103(a); Public Law P.L. 119-21 §70426; IRC §170(d)(2)","rat":"Taxable income before charity and DRD is $520,000 (state bond interest is excluded).","ex":["Deducts the whole $25,000, ignoring the 1% floor, which disallows the first $5,200.","Taxable income before charity and DRD is $520,000 (state bond interest is excluded). The floor is 1% x $520,000 = $5,200, so the deduction is $25,000 - $5,200 = $19,800 (under the $52,000 ceiling). DRD is 50% x $40,000 = $20,000. Taxable income is $480,200; tax at 21% is $100,842.","Includes the $10,000 of state bond interest in taxable income. It is excluded.","Omits the dividends-received deduction of $20,000."],"wt":["calc","","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Corporate taxable income excludes state bond interest, applies the 1% charity floor and the DRD, then the 21% rate.","from":"2026-07-01","calc":1,"steps":["Taxable income before charity and DRD = 2,400,000 - 1,300,000 - 500,000 - 120,000 + 40,000 = 520,000","Floor 1% x 520,000 = 5,200; ceiling 10% x 520,000 = 52,000; deduction = 25,000 - 5,200 = 19,800","DRD 50% x 40,000 = 20,000","Taxable income 520,000 - 19,800 - 20,000 = 480,200; tax 21% x 480,200 = 100,842"],"gd":[["What is taxable income before charity and DRD?","2,400,000 - 1,300,000 - 500,000 - 120,000 + 40,000 = 520,000, with state bond interest excluded."],["What are the charitable deduction and the DRD?","25,000 - 1% x 520,000 = 25,000 - 5,200 = 19,800, and 50% x 40,000 = 20,000."],["What are taxable income and tax?","520,000 - 19,800 - 20,000 = 480,200, and 21% x 480,200 = 100,842."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0006","t":"Foxglove Industries, Inc. is an accrual-basis, calendar-year corporation. Its board of directors authorized a $20,000 gift to a public charity on December 15, 2026, and the gift was paid on March 10, 2027. Foxglove makes the election available to accrual-basis corporations to treat the gift as paid when authorized. In which year may Foxglove deduct the gift, subject to the percentage limit?","mc":{"s":"Foxglove Industries, Inc. is an accrual-basis, calendar-year corporation. Its board of directors authorized a $20,000 gift to a public charity on December 15, 2026, and the gift was paid on March 10, 2027. Foxglove makes the election available to accrual-basis corporations to treat the gift as paid when authorized. In which year may Foxglove deduct the gift, subject to the percentage limit?","d":"","o":["2027, because contributions are deductible only when paid, whatever the corporation's method","2026 only if the board approved the gift before December 1 of that year","2026 or 2027, at the corporation's choice when it files the return","2026, because the board authorized it during the year and it was paid within 3.5 months"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §170(a)(2); Form 1120 instructions (line 19)","rat":"An accrual-basis corporation may treat a gift authorized by the board during the tax year as paid in that year if actually paid by the 15th day of the fourth month after year-end.","ex":["That is the general cash-basis result. The 3.5-month election is available to accrual-basis corporations.","There is no date within the year that matters; authorization by year-end is enough.","The election has been made, so the gift is treated as paid in 2026. It is not left to be deducted in either year at the filing date.","An accrual-basis corporation may treat a gift authorized by the board during the tax year as paid in that year if actually paid by the 15th day of the fourth month after year-end."],"wt":["concept","trap","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"An accrual-basis corporation may treat a board-authorized gift as paid in the year authorized if paid within 3.5 months after year-end.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["What election lets an accrual-basis corporation deduct a gift in the authorizing year?","Treat a board-authorized gift as paid in that year."],["What payment deadline applies?","The 15th day of the fourth month after year end, so a March 10 payment qualifies and the deduction falls in 2026."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0007","t":"Reviewing Redwood Dynamics, Inc.'s 2026 Schedule C, a CPA sees the dividends-received deductions shown. Which item was claimed at the wrong percentage? Assume the deductions are not limited by taxable income and all holding periods are met.","mc":{"s":"Reviewing Redwood Dynamics, Inc.'s 2026 Schedule C, a CPA sees the dividends-received deductions shown. Which item was claimed at the wrong percentage? Assume the deductions are not limited by taxable income and all holding periods are met.","d":"","o":["The dividend from Delta Corp., in which Redwood owns 25%, should be deducted at 65%, not 50%","The dividend from Alpha Corp., in which Redwood owns 5%, should be deducted at 65%, not 50%","The dividend from Beta Corp., in which Redwood owns 90%, should be deducted at 65%, not 100%","The dividend from Gamma Corp., in which Redwood owns 15%, should be deducted at 100%, not 50%"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §243(a); IRC §243(c); IRC §243(b); Form 1120 Schedule C","rat":"Ownership of 20% or more (but under 80%) qualifies for 65%.","ex":["Ownership of 20% or more (but under 80%) qualifies for 65%. The draft used only 50%, which understates the deduction by $6,000.","Ownership under 20% qualifies only for 50%, so the draft is right.","Beta is a member of Redwood's affiliated group (80% or more), so the 100% rate on qualifying dividends is right.","Ownership of 15% qualifies for 50%; 100% is limited to affiliated group members."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The DRD is 65% for 20% to under 80% ownership, 50% below 20%, and 100% only for qualifying affiliated group dividends.","tb":[["Payor (Redwood's ownership)","Dividend","Rate claimed"],["Alpha Corp. (5%)","$10,000","50%"],["Beta Corp. (90%, affiliated group member)","$30,000","100%"],["Delta Corp. (25%)","$40,000","50%"],["Gamma Corp. (15%)","$20,000","50%"]],"from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["What DRD rate applies by ownership: under 20%, 20% to under 80%, 80% or more?","50%, 65% and 100%."],["Which claimed rate is off?","Delta at 25% ownership should be 65%, not 50%."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0008","t":"Sterling Bakeshop, Inc. incurred $12,000 of organizational costs to form the corporation and began business on July 1, 2026. It elected to deduct organizational costs and amortize the remainder over 180 months from the month business began. What is the total organizational cost deduction for 2026?","mc":{"s":"Sterling Bakeshop, Inc. incurred $12,000 of organizational costs to form the corporation and began business on July 1, 2026. It elected to deduct organizational costs and amortize the remainder over 180 months from the month business began. What is the total organizational cost deduction for 2026?","d":"","o":["$400","$5,000","$5,233","$12,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §248(a); Form 1120 instructions","rat":"Up to $5,000 is deducted immediately (the full $5,000 because total costs are under $50,000).","ex":["Amortizes the entire $12,000 and skips the $5,000 immediate deduction.","Omits amortization of the remaining $7,000 for the six months business was open.","Up to $5,000 is deducted immediately (the full $5,000 because total costs are under $50,000). The remaining $7,000 amortizes over 180 months: $7,000 / 180 x 6 months = $233. Total $5,233.","Deducts all costs. Only $5,000 may be deducted at once; the rest is amortized."],"wt":["calc","calc","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Corporate organizational costs allow a $5,000 immediate deduction and amortization of the rest over 180 months from the month business begins.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Immediate deduction 5,000","Remaining 12,000 - 5,000 = 7,000","Amortization 7,000 / 180 x 6 = 233","5,000 + 233 = 5,233"],"gd":[["What is the immediate deduction?","5,000, since total costs are under 50,000."],["What remains to amortize and for how many months in 2026?","12,000 - 5,000 = 7,000 over 180 months, July to December is 6 months."],["What is the total?","7,000 / 180 x 6 = 233, so 5,000 + 233 = 5,233."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0009","t":"Palisade Tools, Inc. is a calendar-year corporation that expects to owe more than $500. On what date is its first required estimated tax installment for 2026 due?","mc":{"s":"Palisade Tools, Inc. is a calendar-year corporation that expects to owe more than $500. On what date is its first required estimated tax installment for 2026 due?","d":"","o":["January 15, 2026","March 15, 2026","June 15, 2026","April 15, 2026"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §6655(c); IRC §6655(d); Form 1120-W","rat":"Corporate installments are due on the 15th day of the fourth, sixth, ninth and twelfth months of the tax year; for a calendar-year corporation the first is April 15.","ex":["That is the individual final estimated payment date for the prior year, not a corporate date.","March 15 is the S corporation and partnership return due date, not a corporate installment date.","June 15 is the second corporate installment, not the first.","Corporate installments are due on the 15th day of the fourth, sixth, ninth and twelfth months of the tax year; for a calendar-year corporation the first is April 15."],"wt":["limit","limit","limit",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A calendar-year corporation's estimated tax installments are due on the 15th of the fourth, sixth, ninth and twelfth months.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["On which months of the tax year are corporate installments due?","The fourth, sixth, ninth and twelfth months, on the 15th."],["What is the first date for a calendar-year corporation?","April 15."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0010","t":"Norland Shipping, Inc. is a calendar-year C corporation. When is its 2026 Form 1120 due, and what is the extended due date if it files a timely extension request?","mc":{"s":"Norland Shipping, Inc. is a calendar-year C corporation. When is its 2026 Form 1120 due, and what is the extended due date if it files a timely extension request?","d":"","o":["April 15, 2027, extended to October 15, 2027","March 15, 2027, extended to September 15, 2027","April 15, 2027, extended to July 15, 2027","March 15, 2027, extended to October 15, 2027"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §6072(a); Form 1120 instructions; Form 7004","rat":"A calendar-year corporation files by the 15th day of the fourth month after year-end, and the automatic extension is six months.","ex":["A calendar-year corporation files by the 15th day of the fourth month after year-end, and the automatic extension is six months.","Those are the S corporation and partnership dates.","The automatic extension for corporations is six months, not three.","March 15 is the S corporation and partnership return date; the extension is right but the original due date is not."],"wt":["","limit","limit","limit"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A calendar-year C corporation's Form 1120 is due April 15 with an automatic six-month extension to October 15.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["When is a calendar-year corporation's return due?","The 15th day of the fourth month, April 15, 2027."],["How long is the automatic extension?","Six months, to October 15, 2027."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0011","t":"Harbor Lantern, Inc. had a $500,000 net operating loss in 2025. In 2026 its taxable income before any net operating loss deduction is $400,000. Assuming no other loss carryovers, what is its 2026 taxable income?","mc":{"s":"Harbor Lantern, Inc. had a $500,000 net operating loss in 2025. In 2026 its taxable income before any net operating loss deduction is $400,000. Assuming no other loss carryovers, what is its 2026 taxable income?","d":"","o":["$0","$80,000","$100,000","$200,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §172(a)(2); IRC §172(b)(1); Form 1120 instructions (line 29a)","rat":"A post-2017 net operating loss is deductible only up to 80% of taxable income: $320,000.","ex":["Deducts 100% of taxable income. The 80% limit applies to losses arising after 2017.","A post-2017 net operating loss is deductible only up to 80% of taxable income: $320,000. Taxable income is $400,000 - $320,000 = $80,000, and $180,000 of NOL carries forward.","Subtracts income from the loss, which is not how the deduction is computed.","Uses a 50% limit; the limit is 80%."],"wt":["concept","","calc","limit"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A post-2017 NOL deduction is limited to 80% of taxable income, with the rest carried forward.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Deduction limit 80% x 400,000 = 320,000","NOL available 500,000 exceeds the limit","Taxable income = 400,000 - 320,000 = 80,000; NOL carryover 180,000"],"gd":[["What limit applies to a post-2017 NOL deduction?","80% of taxable income."],["What is it here?","80% x 400,000 = 320,000."],["What is taxable income?","400,000 - 320,000 = 80,000, with 180,000 of NOL carried forward."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0012","t":"Corvina Marketing, Inc., a C corporation, incurred a net operating loss in its 2026 tax year that is not a farming loss or an insurance company loss. Which statement describes the carryover?","mc":{"s":"Corvina Marketing, Inc., a C corporation, incurred a net operating loss in its 2026 tax year that is not a farming loss or an insurance company loss. Which statement describes the carryover?","d":"","o":["It may be carried back two years and forward 20 years","It may be carried back five years and forward indefinitely","It may not be carried back and expires after 20 years of carryforward","It may not be carried back and may be carried forward indefinitely, subject to an 80% taxable income limit"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §172(b)(1); IRC §172(a)(2)","rat":"For losses arising in years beginning after 2020, there is no carryback and the carryforward has no expiration date, limited to 80% of taxable income.","ex":["That was the pre-2018 rule.","A five-year carryback applied only temporarily to losses arising in 2018 through 2020.","The 20-year limit applied to pre-2018 losses.","For losses arising in years beginning after 2020, there is no carryback and the carryforward has no expiration date, limited to 80% of taxable income."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A post-2020 NOL has no carryback and an indefinite carryforward, limited to 80% of taxable income.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["May a post-2020 NOL of a C corporation be carried back?","No."],["How is the carryforward treated?","Indefinitely, subject to the 80% taxable income limit."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0013","t":"Quarry Ridge Corp. sold stock at a $40,000 capital loss in 2026 and had $15,000 of capital gains in the year. What is the treatment of the $25,000 net capital loss?","mc":{"s":"Quarry Ridge Corp. sold stock at a $40,000 capital loss in 2026 and had $15,000 of capital gains in the year. What is the treatment of the $25,000 net capital loss?","d":"","o":["It is not deductible in 2026, and is carried back three years and forward five years as a short-term capital loss","It offsets ordinary income up to $3,000 and the remainder carries forward indefinitely","It is fully deductible against ordinary income in 2026","It is carried forward indefinitely and may not be carried back"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §1211(a); IRC §1212(a)","rat":"A corporation deducts capital losses only against capital gains.","ex":["A corporation deducts capital losses only against capital gains. The net loss is carried back three years and forward five years, treated as short-term.","The $3,000 ordinary income offset is for individuals, not corporations.","Corporate capital losses may not offset ordinary income.","A corporation's net capital loss has a three-year carryback and a five-year carryforward."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A corporation deducts capital losses only against capital gains, with a three-year carryback and five-year carryforward as short-term.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Against what can a corporation deduct capital losses?","Only capital gains."],["What happens to the 25,000 net capital loss?","Carried back three years and forward five years as short-term."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0014","t":"In 2026, Dunmore Cabinets, Inc. had $300,000 of ordinary taxable income before capital transactions, a $20,000 long-term capital gain, and a $50,000 short-term capital loss. It has no prior loss carryovers. What is its 2026 federal income tax liability?","mc":{"s":"In 2026, Dunmore Cabinets, Inc. had $300,000 of ordinary taxable income before capital transactions, a $20,000 long-term capital gain, and a $50,000 short-term capital loss. It has no prior loss carryovers. What is its 2026 federal income tax liability?","d":"","o":["$52,500","$56,700","$63,000","$67,200"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §1211(a); IRC §11(b)","rat":"The capital loss offsets only the $20,000 capital gain.","ex":["Subtracts the whole $50,000 capital loss from ordinary income and ignores the $20,000 gain.","Deducts the whole capital loss. The excess over gains is not deductible against ordinary income.","The capital loss offsets only the $20,000 capital gain. The $30,000 net loss is not deductible now. Taxable income is $300,000, and tax is 21% x $300,000 = $63,000.","Includes the capital gain without offsetting the capital loss."],"wt":["calc","calc","","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A corporation's capital loss offsets only capital gains, not ordinary income, and taxable income is taxed at 21%.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Capital gain 20,000 - capital loss 50,000 = net loss 30,000 (not deductible)","Taxable income = 300,000 + 20,000 - 20,000 = 300,000","Tax = 21% x 300,000 = 63,000"],"gd":[["What can the 50,000 capital loss offset?","Only the 20,000 capital gain, leaving a 30,000 net loss that is not deductible now."],["What is taxable income?","300,000 + 20,000 - 20,000 = 300,000."],["What is the tax?","21% x 300,000 = 63,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0015","t":"Ivory Coast Imports, Inc. had a taxable loss of $200,000 in 2024, which is a net operating loss. Its taxable income before any net operating loss deduction was $150,000 in 2025 and $300,000 in 2026. It has no other carryovers. What is the taxable income for 2026?","mc":{"s":"Ivory Coast Imports, Inc. had a taxable loss of $200,000 in 2024, which is a net operating loss. Its taxable income before any net operating loss deduction was $150,000 in 2025 and $300,000 in 2026. It has no other carryovers. What is the taxable income for 2026?","d":"","o":["$60,000","$100,000","$220,000","$300,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §172(a)(2); IRC §172(b)(2)","rat":"In 2025 the deduction is limited to 80% x $150,000 = $120,000, leaving $80,000 of NOL.","ex":["Applies the 80% limit to 2026 income as though no NOL had been used in 2025.","Deducts the full $200,000 in 2026, ignoring the $120,000 already used in 2025.","In 2025 the deduction is limited to 80% x $150,000 = $120,000, leaving $80,000 of NOL. In 2026 the $80,000 is fully deductible (limit $240,000), so taxable income is $300,000 - $80,000 = $220,000.","Assumes the entire NOL was used in 2025 or ignores the loss carryover."],"wt":["calc","calc","","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The 80% NOL limit applies each year, so a partly used NOL carryover is deducted in later years against the remaining income.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["2025 deduction = min(200,000, 80% x 150,000 = 120,000) = 120,000","Remaining NOL = 80,000","2026 deduction = min(80,000, 240,000) = 80,000","Taxable income 2026 = 300,000 - 80,000 = 220,000"],"gd":[["What is the 2025 NOL deduction?","Limited to 80% x 150,000 = 120,000, leaving 200,000 - 120,000 = 80,000."],["What is the 2026 deduction?","The full 80,000, below the 240,000 limit."],["What is 2026 taxable income?","300,000 - 80,000 = 220,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0016","t":"Bayshore Analytics, Inc.'s draft 2026 Form 1120 shows taxable income before the net operating loss deduction of $400,000, a net operating loss carryforward from 2025 of $500,000, an NOL deduction of $400,000, and taxable income of $0. Bayshore has no other loss carryforwards. What correction is required?","mc":{"s":"Bayshore Analytics, Inc.'s draft 2026 Form 1120 shows taxable income before the net operating loss deduction of $400,000, a net operating loss carryforward from 2025 of $500,000, an NOL deduction of $400,000, and taxable income of $0. Bayshore has no other loss carryforwards. What correction is required?","d":"","o":["Deny the deduction unless the loss is first carried back two years, so taxable income is $400,000","Limit the deduction to $200,000, so taxable income is $200,000, and carry the remaining $300,000 forward","Limit the deduction to $320,000, and let the remaining $180,000 expire after 20 years","Limit the deduction to $320,000, so taxable income is $80,000, and carry the remaining $180,000 forward"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §172(a)(2); IRC §172(b)(1)(A); Form 1120 instructions (line 29a)","rat":"The deduction is capped at 80% of taxable income ($320,000).","ex":["Losses from years after 2020 cannot be carried back.","Uses a 50% limit; the limit is 80%.","Post-2017 NOLs carry forward indefinitely; the 20-year limit applied to earlier losses.","The deduction is capped at 80% of taxable income ($320,000). The unused NOL carries forward without expiration."],"wt":["concept","limit","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The NOL deduction is capped at 80% of taxable income and unused NOL carries forward indefinitely without carryback.","tb":[["Draft Form 1120 (2026)","Amount"],["Taxable income before NOL deduction","$400,000"],["NOL carryforward from 2025","$500,000"],["NOL deduction claimed","$400,000"],["Taxable income","$0"]],"from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Limit = 80% x 400,000 = 320,000","Taxable income = 400,000 - 320,000 = 80,000","Carryforward = 500,000 - 320,000 = 180,000"],"gd":[["What caps the NOL deduction?","80% of taxable income before the NOL."],["What is the cap and taxable income?","80% x 400,000 = 320,000, so 400,000 - 320,000 = 80,000."],["What carries forward?","500,000 - 320,000 = 180,000 with no expiration."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0017","t":"Maple Crest Corp. has a $60,000 net capital loss in 2026 and no capital gains. In 2023, 2024 and 2025 it had net capital gains of $20,000, $25,000 and $40,000. In 2027 it expects a $30,000 capital gain. Which statement about the loss is correct?","mc":{"s":"Maple Crest Corp. has a $60,000 net capital loss in 2026 and no capital gains. In 2023, 2024 and 2025 it had net capital gains of $20,000, $25,000 and $40,000. In 2027 it expects a $30,000 capital gain. Which statement about the loss is correct?","d":"","o":["The loss is carried back to 2023 first, then to 2024 and 2025, as a short-term loss; any remainder carries forward five years","The loss is deductible in 2026 against ordinary income up to $3,000, and the rest carries forward indefinitely","The loss is carried forward only, so it offsets the $30,000 gain in 2027 and the rest expires later","The loss is carried back to 2025 first, then to 2024 and 2023, to the extent of gains in those years"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §1212(a)(1)","rat":"A corporate net capital loss is carried back to the third preceding year first, then the second and first, and any excess is carried forward five years.","ex":["A corporate net capital loss is carried back to the third preceding year first, then the second and first, and any excess is carried forward five years.","The $3,000 offset is available to individuals only.","A corporation must first carry the loss back three years; it may carry forward only what remains.","The carryback goes to the earliest year first (the third preceding year), then progresses toward the loss year."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A corporate net capital loss carries back to the third preceding year first, then the second and first, with the rest carried forward five years.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Which year is used first for a corporate capital loss carryback?","The third preceding year, then the second and first."],["What happens to the excess?","It carries forward five years, treated as short-term."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0018","t":"In 2026, Juniper Hardware, Inc. has net income tax before credits of $200,000, general business credits of $250,000, and no alternative minimum tax or other minimum tax liability (tentative minimum tax is zero). The limitation is net income tax minus 25% of net regular tax over $25,000. What general business credit may Juniper claim in 2026?","mc":{"s":"In 2026, Juniper Hardware, Inc. has net income tax before credits of $200,000, general business credits of $250,000, and no alternative minimum tax or other minimum tax liability (tentative minimum tax is zero). The limitation is net income tax minus 25% of net regular tax over $25,000. What general business credit may Juniper claim in 2026?","d":"","o":["$150,000","$156,250","$200,000","$250,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §38(c)(1); IRC §38(c)(1)(B); Form 3800","rat":"The credit cannot exceed net income tax minus 25% of the tax over $25,000: $200,000 - 25% x $175,000 = $156,250.","ex":["Takes 25% of the entire tax instead of the tax over $25,000.","The credit cannot exceed net income tax minus 25% of the tax over $25,000: $200,000 - 25% x $175,000 = $156,250. The other $93,750 is carried over.","Allows the credits to reduce tax to zero. The 25% rule requires some tax to remain.","Claims all credits without applying the limitation."],"wt":["calc","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The general business credit cannot exceed net income tax minus 25% of net regular tax over $25,000.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Tax over $25,000 = 200,000 - 25,000 = 175,000","25% x 175,000 = 43,750","Limit = 200,000 - 43,750 = 156,250","Credit allowed = min(250,000, 156,250) = 156,250"],"gd":[["What is the tax over 25,000?","200,000 - 25,000 = 175,000."],["What is 25% of it and the limit?","43,750, so the limit is 200,000 - 43,750 = 156,250."],["What is allowed?","The lesser of 250,000 and 156,250, so 156,250, with 93,750 carried over."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0019","t":"Otter Point Corp. cannot use all of its general business credit in 2026 because of the annual limitation. How are unused general business credits treated?","mc":{"s":"Otter Point Corp. cannot use all of its general business credit in 2026 because of the annual limitation. How are unused general business credits treated?","d":"","o":["Carried back two years and carried forward 20 years","Carried forward only, for up to five years","Refunded in cash to the corporation in the following year","Carried back one year and carried forward up to 20 years"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §39(a); Form 3800","rat":"Unused general business credits are carried back one year and then forward up to 20 years.","ex":["There is no two-year carryback for the general business credit.","The carryforward is 20 years, and a one-year carryback applies first.","General business credits are not refundable.","Unused general business credits are carried back one year and then forward up to 20 years."],"wt":["limit","limit","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Unused general business credits carry back one year and forward up to 20 years, and are not refundable.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Where do unused general business credits go?","To another year rather than being refunded."],["How many years back and forward?","Back one year and forward up to 20 years."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0020","t":"Crestline Motors, Inc. had 2026 worldwide taxable income of $1,000,000, of which $300,000 was from foreign sources, all in a single category. It paid $80,000 of foreign income taxes. U.S. tax before credits is 21% of taxable income. What foreign tax credit may it claim?","mc":{"s":"Crestline Motors, Inc. had 2026 worldwide taxable income of $1,000,000, of which $300,000 was from foreign sources, all in a single category. It paid $80,000 of foreign income taxes. U.S. tax before credits is 21% of taxable income. What foreign tax credit may it claim?","d":"","o":["$63,000","$80,000","$147,000","$210,000"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §901; IRC §904(a); Form 1118","rat":"The credit is limited to U.S.","ex":["The credit is limited to U.S. tax multiplied by foreign-source income divided by worldwide income: $210,000 x 30% = $63,000, which is less than the $80,000 paid.","Claims all foreign taxes paid, ignoring the limitation.","Uses the domestic-income ratio (70%) instead of the foreign-source ratio.","Uses the entire U.S. tax, ignoring the ratio of foreign to worldwide income."],"wt":["","concept","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The foreign tax credit is limited to U.S. tax times foreign-source income over worldwide income.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["U.S. tax before credit = 21% x 1,000,000 = 210,000","Limit = 210,000 x 300,000 / 1,000,000 = 63,000","Credit = min(80,000, 63,000) = 63,000"],"gd":[["What is U.S. tax before credit?","21% x 1,000,000 = 210,000."],["What is the foreign tax credit limit?","210,000 x 300,000 / 1,000,000 = 63,000."],["Which is smaller than the 80,000 paid?","63,000, so that is the credit."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0021","t":"Which credit is one of the components of the current-year general business credit?","mc":{"s":"Which credit is one of the components of the current-year general business credit?","d":"","o":["The research credit","The foreign tax credit","The credit for prior-year alternative minimum tax paid by an individual","The credit for federal income tax withheld on wages"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §38(b); Form 3800","rat":"The research credit is among the credits combined in the general business credit.","ex":["The research credit is among the credits combined in the general business credit.","The foreign tax credit is a separate credit with its own limitation and is not part of the general business credit.","That credit belongs to individuals and is unrelated to business credits.","Withholding is a payment credit and is not a business credit."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The research credit is part of the general business credit; the foreign tax credit and withholding are separate.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["What is the general business credit?","A combination of several business credits."],["Which listed credit is a component?","The research credit; the foreign tax credit, prior-year AMT credit and wage withholding credit are separate."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0022","t":"Draft 2026 Form 1120 workpapers for Linden Rail Supply, Inc. claim the full available general business credit. Linden has no minimum tax liability, and the one-year carryback should be ignored. Which correction is required?","mc":{"s":"Draft 2026 Form 1120 workpapers for Linden Rail Supply, Inc. claim the full available general business credit. Linden has no minimum tax liability, and the one-year carryback should be ignored. Which correction is required?","d":"","o":["Limit the credit to $150,000, so tax due is $50,000, and carry the unused $100,000 forward","Limit the credit to $200,000, so tax due is $0, and carry the unused $50,000 forward","Limit the credit to $156,250 and refund the unused $93,750 in cash","Limit the credit to $156,250, so tax due is $43,750, and carry the unused $93,750 forward"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §38(c)(1); IRC §38(c)(1)(B); IRC §39(a); Form 3800","rat":"The limit is $200,000 - 25% x ($200,000 - $25,000) = $156,250.","ex":["Takes 25% of the entire tax rather than only the tax over $25,000.","This treats the credit as able to eliminate the tax. The 25% rule leaves tax payable for a corporation with tax over $25,000.","General business credits are not refundable; unused amounts are carried to other years.","The limit is $200,000 - 25% x ($200,000 - $25,000) = $156,250. Some tax must remain, and unused credits carry forward."],"wt":["calc","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The general business credit limit leaves some tax payable, and unused credits carry to other years rather than being refunded.","tb":[["Draft Form 3800 workpaper (2026)","Amount"],["Net income tax before general business credit","$200,000"],["General business credits available","$250,000"],["Credit claimed in draft","$250,000"],["Tax due in draft","$0"]],"from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Tax over 25,000 = 175,000","25% x 175,000 = 43,750","Limit = 200,000 - 43,750 = 156,250","Tax due = 200,000 - 156,250 = 43,750; carryover 250,000 - 156,250 = 93,750"],"gd":[["What is the limit on the general business credit?","Net income tax less 25% of the tax over 25,000."],["What is the limit here?","200,000 - 25% x 175,000 = 156,250."],["What remains payable and carried forward?","200,000 - 156,250 = 43,750 tax due, and 250,000 - 156,250 = 93,750 carried forward."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0051","t":"Two corporations each receive $200,000 of dividends from a 10%-owned domestic corporation and qualify for the 50% dividends-received deduction. Lark Co. has taxable income of $150,000 before the deduction. Moss Co. has taxable income of $60,000 before the deduction. Which statement is correct?","mc":{"s":"Two corporations each receive $200,000 of dividends from a 10%-owned domestic corporation and qualify for the 50% dividends-received deduction. Lark Co. has taxable income of $150,000 before the deduction. Moss Co. has taxable income of $60,000 before the deduction. Which statement is correct?","d":"","o":["Both deduct $100,000, because the taxable-income limit does not apply to dividends of 10%-owned corporations","Lark deducts $100,000 and Moss deducts $30,000","Lark's deduction is limited to $75,000, and Moss deducts the full $100,000, which creates a net operating loss","Both are limited to 50% of taxable income, so Lark deducts $75,000 and Moss deducts $30,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §246(b)(1); IRC §246(b)(2); IRC §243(a)(1); IRC §246(b)(3)","rat":"The taxable-income limit does not apply if the full deduction would create or increase a net operating loss.","ex":["The limit applies unless the full deduction would create a loss.","Lark is limited to 50% of taxable income, and Moss is not limited because the full deduction creates a loss.","The taxable-income limit does not apply if the full deduction would create or increase a net operating loss. It does not for Lark but does for Moss.","The loss exception allows Moss the full deduction."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The DRD taxable-income limit does not apply if the full deduction would create or increase an NOL.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Lark: 50% x 150,000 = 75,000; full 100,000 would leave 50,000 of income, so no loss and the limit applies","Moss: 50% x 200,000 = 100,000 exceeds taxable income 60,000, so it creates a loss and the limit does not apply"],"gd":[["When does the taxable-income limit not apply to the DRD?","When the full deduction would create or increase an NOL."],["What is Lark's outcome?","Full 100,000 would leave 50,000 of income, so the 75,000 limit applies."],["What is Moss's outcome?","100,000 exceeds its 60,000 income, so it creates a loss and Moss takes the full 100,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-1001","t":"Corvid Systems, Inc. is a calendar-year C corporation with a $1,500,000 net operating loss from 2024 and no other carryovers. Its taxable income before the net operating loss deduction is $1,000,000 for 2026 and $600,000 for 2027. Assume a 21% rate. By how much does the 80% taxable-income limitation increase Corvid's combined 2026 and 2027 federal income tax, compared with a rule that lets the loss offset 100% of taxable income?","mc":{"s":"Corvid Systems, Inc. is a calendar-year C corporation with a $1,500,000 net operating loss from 2024 and no other carryovers. Its taxable income before the net operating loss deduction is $1,000,000 for 2026 and $600,000 for 2027. Assume a 21% rate. By how much does the 80% taxable-income limitation increase Corvid's combined 2026 and 2027 federal income tax, compared with a rule that lets the loss offset 100% of taxable income?","d":"","o":["$4,200","$42,000","$46,200","$67,200"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §172(a)(2)(B)(ii); IRC §11(b)","rat":"With the limit: 2026 deduction $800,000, tax $42,000; 2027 deduction $480,000, tax $25,200; total $67,200.","ex":["Counts only the 2027 difference ($25,200 - $21,000) and ignores the extra $42,000 of tax in 2026.","Counts only 2026. The 2027 tax also differs because less of the loss is used in 2026 but more remains, and the 80% cap applies again in 2027.","With the limit: 2026 deduction $800,000, tax $42,000; 2027 deduction $480,000, tax $25,200; total $67,200. Without it: 2026 tax $0, 2027 deduction $500,000, tax $21,000. The limit adds $46,200.","Reports total tax with the limit ($67,200) instead of the increase over the no-limit result."],"wt":["calc","calc","","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A post-2017 net operating loss carries forward indefinitely but can offset only 80% of each year's taxable income.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["With the limit: 2026 NOL 80% x 1,000,000 = 800,000, TI 200,000, tax 42,000; carryover 700,000","2027 NOL min(700,000, 80% x 600,000 = 480,000) = 480,000, TI 120,000, tax 25,200; total 67,200","No limit: 2026 TI 0; carryover 500,000; 2027 TI 100,000, tax 21,000","67,200 - 21,000 = 46,200"],"gd":[["What is the tax with the 80% limit?","2026: deduction 800,000, income 200,000, tax 42,000; 2027: deduction 480,000, income 120,000, tax 25,200; total 67,200."],["What is the tax with no limit?","2026 income 0, then a 500,000 deduction in 2027 leaves 100,000, tax 21,000."],["What is the increase?","67,200 - 21,000 = 46,200."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-1006","t":"Tavern Ridge Foods, Inc., a C corporation, paid its sole shareholder-employee, Mark Lee, $300,000 of salary in 2026 and deducted it. The IRS concludes that $200,000 is reasonable compensation and recharacterizes the other $100,000 as a dividend. Assume the corporation's tax rate is 21%, Mark's rate on ordinary income is 35% and on qualified dividends 20%, and ignore payroll taxes, penalties and interest. What is the net change in combined federal income tax for the corporation and Mark?","mc":{"s":"Tavern Ridge Foods, Inc., a C corporation, paid its sole shareholder-employee, Mark Lee, $300,000 of salary in 2026 and deducted it. The IRS concludes that $200,000 is reasonable compensation and recharacterizes the other $100,000 as a dividend. Assume the corporation's tax rate is 21%, Mark's rate on ordinary income is 35% and on qualified dividends 20%, and ignore payroll taxes, penalties and interest. What is the net change in combined federal income tax for the corporation and Mark?","d":"","o":["An increase of $6,000","An increase of $21,000","An increase of $36,000","A decrease of $15,000"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §162(a)(1); Reg. §1.162-7; IRC §301(c)(1); IRC §11(b)","rat":"The corporation loses a $100,000 deduction: +$21,000.","ex":["The corporation loses a $100,000 deduction: +$21,000. Mark's $100,000 moves from 35% wages to 20% dividends: -$15,000. Net increase $6,000.","Counts only the corporation's extra tax. Mark's tax falls because the recharacterized amount is taxed as a dividend at the lower 20% rate.","Adds Mark's rate reduction to the corporate tax. His lower dividend rate reduces the combined increase; it does not add to it.","Counts only Mark's savings. The corporation's lost deduction raises its tax by more than his savings."],"wt":["","calc","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Unreasonable compensation is nondeductible to the corporation and taxed to the shareholder as a dividend, so corporate tax rises while the shareholder's rate falls.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Corporation: 21% x 100,000 = 21,000 more tax","Mark: (35% - 20%) x 100,000 = 15,000 less tax","21,000 - 15,000 = 6,000 net increase"],"gd":[["What does the corporation lose?","A 100,000 deduction, so 21% x 100,000 = 21,000 more tax."],["What does Mark save?","100,000 moves from 35% to 20%, 15% x 100,000 = 15,000 less tax."],["What is the net change?","21,000 - 15,000 = a 6,000 increase."]]}}],"3":[{"i":"REG-V-B-0023","t":"Sparrow Outdoor Gear, Inc. sells camping equipment (tangible goods) into State Q. Which of Sparrow's activities in State Q goes beyond the protection of the federal statute limiting state income taxation of interstate sales and can create income tax nexus?","mc":{"s":"Sparrow Outdoor Gear, Inc. sells camping equipment (tangible goods) into State Q. Which of Sparrow's activities in State Q goes beyond the protection of the federal statute limiting state income taxation of interstate sales and can create income tax nexus?","d":"","o":["Employees repair defective products at customers' sites in State Q","Sales representatives solicit orders that are approved and shipped from outside State Q","Sales representatives distribute free product samples and catalogs to prospective customers","Sparrow mails promotional email offers to residents of State Q"],"a":1,"cite":"P.L. 86-272 (15 U.S.C. §381); Wisconsin Dept. of Revenue v. William Wrigley, Jr., Co.","rat":"Repair and maintenance services exceed mere solicitation of orders, so the protection does not apply and nexus can exist.","ex":["Repair and maintenance services exceed mere solicitation of orders, so the protection does not apply and nexus can exist.","Solicitation of orders for tangible goods, with approval and shipment from outside the state, is the activity protected from state income tax.","Delivering samples and catalogs is part of solicitation and remains protected.","Advertising by mail or email is solicitation and remains protected."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Repairing products at customers' sites exceeds protected solicitation of orders and can create state income tax nexus.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["What does the federal statute protect for sellers of tangible goods?","Mere solicitation of orders that are approved and shipped from outside the state."],["Which listed activity goes beyond solicitation?","Employees repairing products at customers' sites, which can create nexus."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0024","t":"Mira Learning Co., an online course seller with no offices, employees or property in State R, made $350,000 of sales to State R customers in 2026. State R requires remote sellers with more than $100,000 of sales into the state to collect sales tax. Which statement is correct?","mc":{"s":"Mira Learning Co., an online course seller with no offices, employees or property in State R, made $350,000 of sales to State R customers in 2026. State R requires remote sellers with more than $100,000 of sales into the state to collect sales tax. Which statement is correct?","d":"","o":["Mira has no nexus, because sales tax collection requires a physical presence in the state","Mira has nexus only if it also has employees or property in State R","Mira has nexus only after it has made sales in State R for three consecutive years","Mira has economic nexus and must collect State R sales tax, even without a physical presence"],"a":4,"cite":"South Dakota v. Wayfair, Inc. (2018); State remote-seller statutes","rat":"Under the Wayfair decision, a state may impose collection duties on remote sellers that exceed a reasonable economic threshold.","ex":["That was the rule before the Wayfair decision; physical presence is no longer required.","A physical presence is not required when an economic threshold is exceeded.","There is no three-year requirement; the state's threshold applies to the current or prior year.","Under the Wayfair decision, a state may impose collection duties on remote sellers that exceed a reasonable economic threshold."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Economic nexus under Wayfair lets a state require collection from remote sellers above a sales threshold without physical presence.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["What did Wayfair allow states to require?","Tax collection by remote sellers above a reasonable economic threshold."],["Does Mira pass the threshold and does it need a physical presence?","350,000 exceeds 100,000, and no physical presence is needed, so it has economic nexus."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0025","t":"Greenfield Tooling, Inc. operates in several states. It also earns interest on a reserve fund that is unrelated to its operations. Which statement describes the standard state treatment of these two income types?","mc":{"s":"Greenfield Tooling, Inc. operates in several states. It also earns interest on a reserve fund that is unrelated to its operations. Which statement describes the standard state treatment of these two income types?","d":"","o":["Both are allocated entirely to the state in which the corporation is incorporated","Both are apportioned among the states using the same three-factor formula","Business income is apportioned by formula; nonbusiness income such as this interest is allocated to a specific state","Business income is allocated to a specific state; nonbusiness income is apportioned by formula"],"a":3,"cite":"UDITPA apportionment and allocation provisions; Multistate Tax Commission guidance","rat":"Apportionment divides business income using factors, while allocation assigns nonbusiness income (interest, rents, gains) to a single state, often the commercial domicile.","ex":["Business income of a multistate company is divided by formula, not assigned to one state.","Nonbusiness income is generally allocated, not apportioned.","Apportionment divides business income using factors, while allocation assigns nonbusiness income (interest, rents, gains) to a single state, often the commercial domicile.","The reverse of the standard treatment."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Multistate business income is apportioned by formula, while nonbusiness income is allocated to a specific state.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["How is business income divided among states?","Apportioned by a formula of factors."],["How is nonbusiness income such as unrelated interest assigned?","Allocated to a specific state, often the commercial domicile."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0026","t":"Tern Logistics, Inc. has $800,000 of business income. State S apportions business income using only a sales factor. Tern's total sales are $10,000,000 including $2,000,000 sold to State S customers. Its property factor for State S is 25% and its payroll factor is 40%. What business income is apportioned to State S?","mc":{"s":"Tern Logistics, Inc. has $800,000 of business income. State S apportions business income using only a sales factor. Tern's total sales are $10,000,000 including $2,000,000 sold to State S customers. Its property factor for State S is 25% and its payroll factor is 40%. What business income is apportioned to State S?","d":"","o":["$160,000","$200,000","$226,667","$320,000"],"a":1,"cite":"UDITPA apportionment and allocation provisions; State apportionment statutes","rat":"A single sales factor uses only sales: $2,000,000 / $10,000,000 = 20%.","ex":["A single sales factor uses only sales: $2,000,000 / $10,000,000 = 20%. State S apportioned income is 20% x $800,000 = $160,000.","Uses only the property factor.","Averages the three factors equally, though State S uses only sales.","Uses only the payroll factor."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Under a single sales factor, apportioned income equals the state's sales share times business income.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Sales factor = 2,000,000 / 10,000,000 = 20%","Apportioned income = 20% x 800,000 = 160,000"],"gd":[["Which factor does a single sales factor state use?","Sales only."],["What is the factor?","2,000,000 / 10,000,000 = 20%."],["What is apportioned income?","20% x 800,000 = 160,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0027","t":"Ashby Forge, Inc. has $800,000 of business income. State T uses a three-factor formula that weights sales twice as much as property or payroll (sales weighted double). Ashby's State T factors are: sales 20%, property 25% and payroll 40%. What business income is apportioned to State T?","mc":{"s":"Ashby Forge, Inc. has $800,000 of business income. State T uses a three-factor formula that weights sales twice as much as property or payroll (sales weighted double). Ashby's State T factors are: sales 20%, property 25% and payroll 40%. What business income is apportioned to State T?","d":"","o":["$160,000","$170,000","$210,000","$226,667"],"a":3,"cite":"UDITPA apportionment and allocation provisions; State apportionment statutes","rat":"The weighted average is (2 x 20% + 25% + 40%) / 4 = 26.25%.","ex":["Uses only the sales factor.","Divides by four without doubling the sales factor.","The weighted average is (2 x 20% + 25% + 40%) / 4 = 26.25%. Apportioned income is 26.25% x $800,000 = $210,000.","Averages the factors equally, ignoring the double weight on sales."],"wt":["concept","calc","","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A double-weighted sales formula averages 2 x sales plus property and payroll over four, then multiplies by business income.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Weighted sum = 2 x 20% + 25% + 40% = 105%","Divide by 4 = 26.25%","800,000 x 26.25% = 210,000"],"gd":[["How is a double-weighted sales formula computed?","Sales counts twice, so divide the weighted sum by 4."],["What is the weighted average?","(2 x 20% + 25% + 40%) / 4 = 26.25%."],["What is apportioned income?","26.25% x 800,000 = 210,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0028","t":"Delmar Textiles, Inc. reported the following for State U: business income of $600,000, of which 25% is apportioned to State U under the state's formula, and $40,000 of rental income from a nonbusiness building located in State U, which is allocated entirely to State U. What is Delmar's State U taxable income before any state adjustments?","mc":{"s":"Delmar Textiles, Inc. reported the following for State U: business income of $600,000, of which 25% is apportioned to State U under the state's formula, and $40,000 of rental income from a nonbusiness building located in State U, which is allocated entirely to State U. What is Delmar's State U taxable income before any state adjustments?","d":"","o":["$150,000","$160,000","$190,000","$640,000"],"a":3,"cite":"UDITPA apportionment and allocation provisions; State apportionment statutes","rat":"Business income apportioned is 25% x $600,000 = $150,000.","ex":["Omits the allocated nonbusiness rental income.","Apportions the nonbusiness rent by formula. Nonbusiness income is allocated to a specific state, not apportioned.","Business income apportioned is 25% x $600,000 = $150,000. Add the allocated nonbusiness rent ($40,000): $190,000.","Assigns all income to State U with no apportionment of business income."],"wt":["calc","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"State taxable income equals formula-apportioned business income plus nonbusiness income allocated to that state.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Apportioned business income = 25% x 600,000 = 150,000","Allocated nonbusiness rent = 40,000","Total = 190,000"],"gd":[["How much business income is apportioned to State U?","25% x 600,000 = 150,000."],["How is the rent treated?","Allocated entirely to State U, 40,000."],["What is the total?","150,000 + 40,000 = 190,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0029","t":"Kessler Optics, Inc. has $500,000 of taxable income and operates in both State A and State B. Its factors in each state are: sales 10%, property 40% and payroll 40%. State A imposes a 5% tax using a single sales factor. State B imposes a 4% tax using an equally weighted three-factor formula. Which statement compares the two taxes correctly?","mc":{"s":"Kessler Optics, Inc. has $500,000 of taxable income and operates in both State A and State B. Its factors in each state are: sales 10%, property 40% and payroll 40%. State A imposes a 5% tax using a single sales factor. State B imposes a 4% tax using an equally weighted three-factor formula. Which statement compares the two taxes correctly?","d":"","o":["State B's tax exceeds State A's by $3,500","State A's tax exceeds State B's by $3,500 because its rate is higher","The two taxes are equal because both states use the same taxable income","State B's tax exceeds State A's by $1,000"],"a":1,"cite":"UDITPA apportionment and allocation provisions; State apportionment statutes","rat":"State A: $500,000 x 10% x 5% = $2,500.","ex":["State A: $500,000 x 10% x 5% = $2,500. State B: factor (10% + 40% + 40%) / 3 = 30%, so $500,000 x 30% x 4% = $6,000. The difference is $3,500.","A higher rate does not offset State A's much smaller factor: State A's tax is $2,500 and State B's is $6,000.","The apportionment formulas and rates differ, so the taxes differ.","Uses a 10% factor for both states; State B's equally weighted factor is 30%."],"wt":["","concept","concept","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"State tax depends on each state's apportionment formula and rate applied to the same taxable income.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["State A tax = 500,000 x 10% x 5% = 2,500","State B factor = (10% + 40% + 40%) / 3 = 30%","State B tax = 500,000 x 30% x 4% = 6,000","Difference = 3,500"],"gd":[["What is State A's tax with a single sales factor?","500,000 x 10% x 5% = 2,500."],["What is State B's factor and tax?","(10% + 40% + 40%) / 3 = 30%, so 500,000 x 30% x 4% = 6,000."],["What is the difference?","6,000 - 2,500 = 3,500."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0030","t":"For federal income tax purposes, how does a C corporation treat the state income taxes it pays in 2026?","mc":{"s":"For federal income tax purposes, how does a C corporation treat the state income taxes it pays in 2026?","d":"","o":["It may not deduct them, because state taxes are not business expenses","It deducts them only up to the individual cap on state and local taxes","It claims a credit against federal tax equal to the state taxes paid","It deducts them as ordinary business expenses, and the individual state and local tax cap does not apply"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §164(a); IRC §162(a); IRC §164(b)(6)","rat":"State and local income taxes are deductible by a corporation as a business expense.","ex":["State income taxes are deductible taxes paid in carrying on a trade or business.","The cap applies only to individuals' itemized deductions.","State taxes are deducted, not credited.","State and local income taxes are deductible by a corporation as a business expense. The cap on state and local tax deductions applies only to individuals."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A C corporation deducts state income taxes as a business expense, and the individual SALT cap does not apply.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["May a corporation deduct state income taxes?","Yes, as ordinary business expenses."],["Does the individual state and local tax cap apply?","No, it applies only to individuals."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-1005","t":"Larkspur Equipment, Inc. manufactures in State H and ships tangible goods to customers in three states. Its business income is $400,000, and its 2026 sales are: State H, $200,000; State J, $300,000; State K, $500,000. In State J, Larkspur only solicits orders that are approved and shipped from State H, and it has no other contact there. Larkspur is taxable in State K. State H apportions business income with a sales factor only and has a throwback rule for sales into states where the seller is not taxable. How much of Larkspur's business income is apportioned to State H?","mc":{"s":"Larkspur Equipment, Inc. manufactures in State H and ships tangible goods to customers in three states. Its business income is $400,000, and its 2026 sales are: State H, $200,000; State J, $300,000; State K, $500,000. In State J, Larkspur only solicits orders that are approved and shipped from State H, and it has no other contact there. Larkspur is taxable in State K. State H apportions business income with a sales factor only and has a throwback rule for sales into states where the seller is not taxable. How much of Larkspur's business income is apportioned to State H?","d":"","o":["$80,000","$120,000","$200,000","$280,000"],"a":3,"cite":"Other P.L. 86-272 (15 U.S.C. §381); Other UDITPA §16(b) (throwback rule)","rat":"P.L.","ex":["Ignores the throwback rule. Only State H's own sales are counted, giving 20%.","Counts only the State J sales. State H's own $200,000 of sales stay in the numerator too.","P.L. 86-272 protects Larkspur from State J's income tax, so the $300,000 of State J sales are thrown back to State H. State H's factor is ($200,000 + $300,000) / $1,000,000 = 50%, so $200,000.","Throws back the State K sales. Larkspur is taxable in State K, so those sales are not thrown back."],"wt":["concept","calc","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Solicitation-only sales are protected from a state's income tax, so a throwback state adds those sales to its own sales factor.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Total sales 1,000,000","Sales into State J are not taxable there (solicitation only), so thrown back: H numerator 200,000 + 300,000 = 500,000","Factor 500,000 / 1,000,000 = 50%; 400,000 x 50% = 200,000"],"gd":[["Does the federal statute protect the solicitation-only state?","Yes, so Larkspur is not taxable in State J."],["What does the throwback rule do to those sales?","It moves State J's 300,000 to State H, so the numerator is 200,000 + 300,000 = 500,000."],["What is apportioned to State H?","500,000 / 1,000,000 = 50%, so 400,000 x 50% = 200,000."]]}}],"4":[{"i":"REG-V-B-0031","t":"Adam Lindner transferred land (adjusted basis $40,000, fair market value $100,000) to a new corporation in exchange for stock worth $90,000 and $10,000 in cash. Adam controls the corporation immediately after the exchange. What gain does Adam recognize?","mc":{"s":"Adam Lindner transferred land (adjusted basis $40,000, fair market value $100,000) to a new corporation in exchange for stock worth $90,000 and $10,000 in cash. Adam controls the corporation immediately after the exchange. What gain does Adam recognize?","d":"","o":["$0","$10,000","$50,000","$60,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §351(a); IRC §351(b)","rat":"Gain is recognized to the extent of boot received: the lesser of the realized gain ($60,000) and the cash ($10,000) is $10,000.","ex":["Assumes full nonrecognition; receipt of cash (boot) triggers gain up to the boot.","Gain is recognized to the extent of boot received: the lesser of the realized gain ($60,000) and the cash ($10,000) is $10,000.","Compares only the stock received with basis, ignoring the cash and the limit on recognized gain.","Recognizes the entire realized gain, though a transfer to a controlled corporation defers gain not matched by boot."],"wt":["concept","","calc","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"On a transfer to a controlled corporation, gain is recognized only to the extent of boot received, up to realized gain.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Realized gain = (90,000 + 10,000) - 40,000 = 60,000","Boot = 10,000","Recognized gain = lesser of 60,000 and 10,000 = 10,000"],"gd":[["What is the realized gain?","(90,000 + 10,000) - 40,000 = 60,000."],["What is the boot?","Cash of 10,000."],["What is recognized?","The lesser of 60,000 and 10,000, so 10,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0032","t":"Using the same facts (Adam transfers land with adjusted basis $40,000 and value $100,000 for stock worth $90,000 and $10,000 in cash, and recognizes $10,000 of gain), what is Adam's basis in the stock?","mc":{"s":"Using the same facts (Adam transfers land with adjusted basis $40,000 and value $100,000 for stock worth $90,000 and $10,000 in cash, and recognizes $10,000 of gain), what is Adam's basis in the stock?","d":"","o":["$30,000","$40,000","$50,000","$90,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §358(a)","rat":"Stock basis equals property basis ($40,000), minus boot received ($10,000), plus gain recognized ($10,000) = $40,000.","ex":["Subtracts the cash but forgets to add the $10,000 of recognized gain.","Stock basis equals property basis ($40,000), minus boot received ($10,000), plus gain recognized ($10,000) = $40,000.","Adds the recognized gain without subtracting the boot received.","Uses the fair market value of the stock. A nonrecognition exchange gives a substituted basis, not fair market value."],"wt":["calc","","calc","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Stock basis equals property basis minus boot received plus gain recognized.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Basis of property 40,000","Less cash received (10,000)","Plus gain recognized 10,000","Stock basis = 40,000"],"gd":[["What is the property basis and how does cash received change it?","40,000 less cash received 10,000."],["What does recognized gain do?","Adds 10,000."],["What is the stock basis?","40,000 - 10,000 + 10,000 = 40,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0033","t":"Beth Amaral transferred business assets with an adjusted basis of $20,000 and a fair market value of $90,000 to a new corporation solely for stock. The corporation also assumed $35,000 of liabilities on the assets, incurred for business purposes. Beth controls the corporation. What gain must Beth recognize?","mc":{"s":"Beth Amaral transferred business assets with an adjusted basis of $20,000 and a fair market value of $90,000 to a new corporation solely for stock. The corporation also assumed $35,000 of liabilities on the assets, incurred for business purposes. Beth controls the corporation. What gain must Beth recognize?","d":"","o":["$0","$15,000","$35,000","$70,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §357(c)(1); IRC §351","rat":"When liabilities assumed exceed the total adjusted basis of the transferred property, the excess is gain: $35,000 - $20,000 = $15,000.","ex":["Treats liability assumption as never creating gain. It does when liabilities exceed basis.","When liabilities assumed exceed the total adjusted basis of the transferred property, the excess is gain: $35,000 - $20,000 = $15,000.","Recognizes the entire liability, but only the excess over basis is gain.","Recognizes the full realized gain, ignoring that the transfer is otherwise tax-free."],"wt":["concept","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Liabilities assumed in a controlled corporation transfer create gain only to the extent they exceed the transferred property's basis.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Liabilities assumed 35,000","Adjusted basis of assets 20,000","Excess = 35,000 - 20,000 = 15,000 gain"],"gd":[["When do liabilities assumed create gain in a controlled exchange?","When they exceed the total adjusted basis of the property transferred."],["What is the excess here?","35,000 - 20,000 = 15,000 gain."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0034","t":"Which transfer qualifies as a nonrecognition exchange under the rules for transfers to a controlled corporation?","mc":{"s":"Which transfer qualifies as a nonrecognition exchange under the rules for transfers to a controlled corporation?","d":"","o":["Ann transfers land for 70% of the stock, and Carl receives 30% of the stock for legal services","Ann and Carl transfer property for all of the stock, and under a binding prior agreement Carl immediately sells half of his shares to an unrelated buyer","Ann transfers land for 60% of a new corporation's stock, and Carl transfers cash for the other 40%","Ann transfers land for 75% of the stock, and the other 25% is issued to her friend for no consideration"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §351(a); IRC §368(c)","rat":"The transferors of property or cash together hold 100% of the stock immediately after the exchange, meeting the 80% control test.","ex":["Stock received for services does not count toward control, so the property transferor holds only 70%.","The prearranged sale reduces the transferors' control below 80% immediately after the exchange.","The transferors of property or cash together hold 100% of the stock immediately after the exchange, meeting the 80% control test.","The transferor holds only 75%, below the required 80% control."],"wt":["concept","concept","","limit"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A transfer to a controlled corporation needs the property transferors to hold at least 80% of the stock immediately afterward; services stock does not count.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["What control test applies?","Transferors of property or cash together hold at least 80% of the stock immediately after the exchange."],["Which arrangement meets it?","The one where both transferors give property or cash for 100% of the stock; stock for services, a binding prior sale and stock for no consideration break it."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0035","t":"Ivy and Noah transferred property with a total adjusted basis of $40,000 and a fair market value of $100,000 to a new corporation solely for all of its stock, and control is met. The corporation's draft opening balance sheet records the property at $100,000. What correction is required to the corporation's basis in the property?","mc":{"s":"Ivy and Noah transferred property with a total adjusted basis of $40,000 and a fair market value of $100,000 to a new corporation solely for all of its stock, and control is met. The corporation's draft opening balance sheet records the property at $100,000. What correction is required to the corporation's basis in the property?","d":"","o":["The corporation's basis is the transferors' $40,000 adjusted basis, since no gain was recognized","The corporation's basis is $100,000, so no correction is needed","The corporation's basis is $60,000, the gain that was not recognized","The corporation's basis is $70,000, the average of the two amounts"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §362(a)","rat":"In a tax-free transfer to a controlled corporation, the corporation takes a carryover basis, increased by any gain recognized.","ex":["In a tax-free transfer to a controlled corporation, the corporation takes a carryover basis, increased by any gain recognized. No gain was recognized here.","Fair market value basis applies only when gain is fully recognized.","The unrecognized gain is not a basis amount; the corporation carries over the transferors' basis.","There is no averaging rule; the corporation uses the transferors' adjusted basis."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"In a tax-free transfer to a controlled corporation, the corporation takes carryover basis, increased by any gain recognized.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Carryover basis = transferors' adjusted basis 40,000","No gain recognized, so no increase"],"gd":[["What basis does the corporation take in a tax-free controlled transfer?","The transferors' carryover basis, plus any gain recognized."],["What is it here?","40,000, since no gain was recognized."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0036","t":"Dana Oyelaran received stock worth $30,000 from a newly formed corporation in exchange for services she performed to organize it. She transferred no property. What is the tax treatment to Dana?","mc":{"s":"Dana Oyelaran received stock worth $30,000 from a newly formed corporation in exchange for services she performed to organize it. She transferred no property. What is the tax treatment to Dana?","d":"","o":["She recognizes no income and takes no basis in the stock","She recognizes $30,000 of long-term capital gain and takes no basis","She recognizes no income until she sells the stock","She recognizes $30,000 of ordinary income and takes a $30,000 basis in the stock"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §61; IRC §83; IRC §351(d)(1)","rat":"Stock received for services is compensation, taxed at fair market value, and the stock takes that value as its basis.","ex":["The nonrecognition rule does not apply to stock issued for services.","Compensation for services is ordinary income, not capital gain.","Stock received for services is included in income when received (if vested).","Stock received for services is compensation, taxed at fair market value, and the stock takes that value as its basis."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Stock received for services is ordinary compensation income at FMV, and that value becomes the stock's basis.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["How is stock received for services treated?","As compensation at fair market value."],["What are the income and the basis?","30,000 of ordinary income and a 30,000 basis."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0037","t":"Poplar Consulting, Inc. has an accumulated earnings and profits deficit of $200,000 at the start of 2026 and current earnings and profits of $50,000 for 2026. During 2026 it distributed $80,000 in cash to Erin Kowal, its only shareholder, whose stock basis is $100,000. How much of the distribution is a dividend?","mc":{"s":"Poplar Consulting, Inc. has an accumulated earnings and profits deficit of $200,000 at the start of 2026 and current earnings and profits of $50,000 for 2026. During 2026 it distributed $80,000 in cash to Erin Kowal, its only shareholder, whose stock basis is $100,000. How much of the distribution is a dividend?","d":"","o":["$0","$30,000","$50,000","$80,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §316(a)(2); IRC §301(c)","rat":"A distribution is a dividend to the extent of current earnings and profits, even when accumulated earnings and profits are negative: $50,000.","ex":["Nets current earnings against the deficit. Current earnings and profits are tested separately for the year.","Uses the amount in excess of current earnings and profits, which is the return of capital, not the dividend.","A distribution is a dividend to the extent of current earnings and profits, even when accumulated earnings and profits are negative: $50,000.","Treats the entire distribution as a dividend; the excess over earnings and profits is return of capital."],"wt":["concept","calc","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A distribution is a dividend to the extent of current earnings and profits, even if accumulated E&P is negative.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Dividend = current E&P = 50,000","Return of capital = 80,000 - 50,000 = 30,000 (reduces basis to 70,000)"],"gd":[["When is a distribution a dividend if accumulated E&P is negative?","To the extent of current E&P."],["What is the dividend and the remainder?","50,000, with 80,000 - 50,000 = 30,000 as a return of capital."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0038","t":"In 2026, Stone Harbor Corp. distributed $90,000 in cash to its only shareholder, Vic Adler. The corporation's total current and accumulated earnings and profits are $20,000. Vic's stock basis is $50,000 and he has held the stock for many years. How much capital gain does Vic recognize?","mc":{"s":"In 2026, Stone Harbor Corp. distributed $90,000 in cash to its only shareholder, Vic Adler. The corporation's total current and accumulated earnings and profits are $20,000. Vic's stock basis is $50,000 and he has held the stock for many years. How much capital gain does Vic recognize?","d":"","o":["$0","$20,000","$40,000","$70,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §301(c)(3); IRC §316","rat":"The first $20,000 is a dividend, the next $50,000 reduces basis to zero, and the remaining $20,000 is capital gain.","ex":["Assumes the entire distribution is taxed as a dividend or return of capital, with no excess over basis.","The first $20,000 is a dividend, the next $50,000 reduces basis to zero, and the remaining $20,000 is capital gain.","Ignores the $20,000 dividend and treats everything over basis as gain.","Treats everything over the earnings and profits as gain, ignoring the return of basis."],"wt":["concept","","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A distribution is dividend to the extent of E&P, then reduces basis, then is capital gain.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Dividend = 20,000","Return of basis = 50,000","Capital gain = 90,000 - 20,000 - 50,000 = 20,000"],"gd":[["How is the first 20,000 treated?","As a dividend up to total E&P."],["What reduces basis?","The next 50,000."],["What is the capital gain?","90,000 - 20,000 - 50,000 = 20,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0039","t":"Slate Ridge Corp. distributed land with an adjusted basis of $60,000 and a fair market value of $150,000 to a shareholder as a dividend. The corporation has ample earnings and profits. What gain does the corporation recognize on the distribution?","mc":{"s":"Slate Ridge Corp. distributed land with an adjusted basis of $60,000 and a fair market value of $150,000 to a shareholder as a dividend. The corporation has ample earnings and profits. What gain does the corporation recognize on the distribution?","d":"","o":["$0","$60,000","$90,000","$150,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §311(b)","rat":"A corporation recognizes gain on a distribution of appreciated property as if it had sold the property at fair market value: $90,000.","ex":["Treats the distribution as nontaxable to the corporation. Gain is recognized on appreciated property distributed to shareholders.","Uses the corporation's adjusted basis as the gain.","A corporation recognizes gain on a distribution of appreciated property as if it had sold the property at fair market value: $90,000.","Uses the full fair market value, ignoring the corporation's $60,000 basis."],"wt":["concept","calc","","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A corporation recognizes gain on distributing appreciated property as if sold at FMV, measured against its adjusted basis.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Fair market value 150,000 - adjusted basis 60,000 = 90,000"],"gd":[["Does a corporation recognize gain on distributing appreciated property?","Yes, as if it sold at fair market value."],["What is the gain?","150,000 - 60,000 = 90,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0040","t":"Using the same facts (Slate Ridge distributes land with basis $60,000 and value $150,000 to a shareholder as a dividend), what is the shareholder's basis in the land?","mc":{"s":"Using the same facts (Slate Ridge distributes land with basis $60,000 and value $150,000 to a shareholder as a dividend), what is the shareholder's basis in the land?","d":"","o":["$0","$60,000","$90,000","$150,000"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §301(d)","rat":"A shareholder's basis in distributed property is its fair market value on the distribution date.","ex":["A dividend of property does not give a zero basis.","Carries over the corporation's basis. The shareholder takes fair market value.","Uses the gain the corporation recognized as basis.","A shareholder's basis in distributed property is its fair market value on the distribution date."],"wt":["concept","concept","calc",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A shareholder's basis in property received in a distribution is its FMV on the distribution date.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Shareholder basis = fair market value = 150,000"],"gd":[["What is a shareholder's basis in distributed property?","Its fair market value on the distribution date."],["What is it here?","150,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0041","t":"Quarrytown Corp. had taxable income of $400,000 for 2026 after a $30,000 dividends-received deduction. It also received $15,000 of tax-exempt interest, paid $84,000 of federal income tax, paid $5,000 of nondeductible penalties, and had $12,000 of business meals of which 50% was disallowed. What is its current earnings and profits for 2026?","mc":{"s":"Quarrytown Corp. had taxable income of $400,000 for 2026 after a $30,000 dividends-received deduction. It also received $15,000 of tax-exempt interest, paid $84,000 of federal income tax, paid $5,000 of nondeductible penalties, and had $12,000 of business meals of which 50% was disallowed. What is its current earnings and profits for 2026?","d":"","o":["$320,000","$350,000","$356,000","$434,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §312; Reg. §1.312-6; Reg. §1.312-7","rat":"Start with taxable income; add tax-exempt interest and the dividends-received deduction; subtract federal income tax, penalties and the disallowed meals: $350,000.","ex":["Omits the add-back of the $30,000 dividends-received deduction.","Start with taxable income; add tax-exempt interest and the dividends-received deduction; subtract federal income tax, penalties and the disallowed meals: $350,000.","Fails to reduce earnings and profits for the $6,000 of disallowed meals, which are real economic costs.","Does not reduce earnings and profits by the federal income tax paid."],"wt":["calc","","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Current E&P adds back the DRD and tax-exempt interest and subtracts federal tax, penalties and disallowed meals.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Taxable income 400,000","Add tax-exempt interest 15,000 and DRD 30,000","Subtract federal tax 84,000, penalties 5,000, disallowed meals 6,000","400,000 + 15,000 + 30,000 - 84,000 - 5,000 - 6,000 = 350,000"],"gd":[["What items are added to taxable income to reach E&P?","Tax-exempt interest 15,000 and the dividends-received deduction 30,000."],["What items are subtracted?","Federal tax 84,000, penalties 5,000 and disallowed meals 6,000."],["What is E&P?","400,000 + 15,000 + 30,000 - 84,000 - 5,000 - 6,000 = 350,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0042","t":"For 2026, Wren Valley, Inc. distributed $90,000 in cash to its only shareholder, Owen Pruitt. The corporation's current and accumulated earnings and profits total $20,000, and Owen's stock basis is $50,000. Owen's draft return reports the entire $90,000 as a qualified dividend. Which correction is needed?","mc":{"s":"For 2026, Wren Valley, Inc. distributed $90,000 in cash to its only shareholder, Owen Pruitt. The corporation's current and accumulated earnings and profits total $20,000, and Owen's stock basis is $50,000. Owen's draft return reports the entire $90,000 as a qualified dividend. Which correction is needed?","d":"","o":["Report a $20,000 dividend and $70,000 of return of capital, with no gain","Report $40,000 of dividend and $50,000 of return of capital","Report a $20,000 dividend, apply $50,000 to reduce basis to $0, and report $20,000 of capital gain","Report no dividend, because the distribution is a return of capital to the extent of basis"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §301(c); IRC §316(a)","rat":"The distribution is a dividend to the extent of earnings and profits, then a nontaxable reduction of basis, then gain.","ex":["Return of capital cannot exceed basis; the amount over basis is gain.","Overstates the dividend; only the $20,000 of earnings and profits is a dividend.","The distribution is a dividend to the extent of earnings and profits, then a nontaxable reduction of basis, then gain.","Earnings and profits are exhausted first as dividends before basis is reduced."],"wt":["concept","calc","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A distribution is a dividend to the extent of E&P, then a return of basis, then capital gain.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Dividend = 20,000","Return of basis = 50,000","Capital gain = 90,000 - 20,000 - 50,000 = 20,000"],"gd":[["What is the order for a distribution over E&P?","Dividend to the extent of E&P, then reduction of basis, then capital gain."],["What are the amounts here?","Dividend 20,000, basis reduction 50,000, gain 90,000 - 20,000 - 50,000 = 20,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0043","t":"Northgate Fixtures, Inc. paid $12,000 of the personal vacation expenses of Jill Marlow, its sole shareholder, and recorded the payment as a business travel expense. Jill did not repay the corporation. What is the likely tax treatment?","mc":{"s":"Northgate Fixtures, Inc. paid $12,000 of the personal vacation expenses of Jill Marlow, its sole shareholder, and recorded the payment as a business travel expense. Jill did not repay the corporation. What is the likely tax treatment?","d":"","o":["A constructive dividend to Jill to the extent of earnings and profits, and no deduction for the corporation","A deductible travel expense for the corporation and no income to Jill","A loan from the corporation that is taxed only if repaid","Compensation to Jill that is deductible by the corporation and taxable to Jill"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §301; IRC §162(a); IRC §262","rat":"A corporate payment of a shareholder's personal expenses is treated as a distribution, not as a deductible expense.","ex":["A corporate payment of a shareholder's personal expenses is treated as a distribution, not as a deductible expense.","Personal expenses of a shareholder are not deductible business expenses.","Without an obligation to repay, the payment is a distribution.","Compensation would be deductible, but only if the payment was made as pay for services, which the facts do not support."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A corporation's payment of a shareholder's personal expenses is a constructive dividend, not a deductible business expense.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["How is a corporate payment of a shareholder's personal expense treated?","As a distribution, not a business expense."],["What follows for Jill and the corporation?","A constructive dividend to the extent of E&P, with no corporate deduction."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0044","t":"Meridian Wire Corp. adopted a plan of complete liquidation in 2026 and distributed $250,000 in cash to Kara Ellis, its only shareholder, in exchange for all her stock. Kara's stock basis is $80,000, and she has held the stock for five years. What is her gain or loss?","mc":{"s":"Meridian Wire Corp. adopted a plan of complete liquidation in 2026 and distributed $250,000 in cash to Kara Ellis, its only shareholder, in exchange for all her stock. Kara's stock basis is $80,000, and she has held the stock for five years. What is her gain or loss?","d":"","o":["$0","$80,000","$170,000","$250,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §331(a); IRC §1222","rat":"In a complete liquidation the shareholder is treated as selling the stock: amount received $250,000 minus basis $80,000 = $170,000 long-term capital gain.","ex":["Assumes a liquidation is tax-free. Only certain subsidiary liquidations are.","Uses the shareholder's basis as the gain.","In a complete liquidation the shareholder is treated as selling the stock: amount received $250,000 minus basis $80,000 = $170,000 long-term capital gain.","Treats the full distribution as gain without subtracting basis."],"wt":["concept","calc","","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"In a complete liquidation the shareholder is treated as selling stock, with gain equal to amount received minus basis.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Amount realized 250,000 - basis 80,000 = 170,000 long-term capital gain"],"gd":[["How is a shareholder treated in a complete liquidation?","As selling the stock."],["What is the gain?","250,000 - 80,000 = 170,000 long-term capital gain."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0045","t":"In a complete liquidation, Vale Machinery Corp. distributed land with an adjusted basis of $100,000 and a fair market value of $400,000 to its individual shareholders. What is the effect on the corporation and what is the shareholders' basis in the land?","mc":{"s":"In a complete liquidation, Vale Machinery Corp. distributed land with an adjusted basis of $100,000 and a fair market value of $400,000 to its individual shareholders. What is the effect on the corporation and what is the shareholders' basis in the land?","d":"","o":["The corporation recognizes no gain, and the shareholders take a $100,000 basis","The corporation recognizes a $300,000 gain, and the shareholders take a $100,000 basis","The corporation recognizes a $400,000 gain, and the shareholders take a $400,000 basis","The corporation recognizes a $300,000 gain, and the shareholders take a $400,000 basis"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §336(a); IRC §334(a)","rat":"The corporation recognizes gain as if it sold the land at fair market value, and shareholders take fair market value as their basis.","ex":["That is the result for a qualifying subsidiary liquidation into a corporate parent, not for individual shareholders.","After the corporation recognizes gain, the shareholders' basis is fair market value.","The gain is fair market value less the corporation's $100,000 basis, not the full value.","The corporation recognizes gain as if it sold the land at fair market value, and shareholders take fair market value as their basis."],"wt":["concept","concept","calc",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"In a taxable liquidation the corporation recognizes gain as if selling at FMV, and shareholders take FMV basis.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Corporate gain = 400,000 - 100,000 = 300,000","Shareholder basis = fair market value = 400,000"],"gd":[["What does the corporation recognize on distributing land in a complete liquidation?","Gain as if sold at fair market value, 400,000 - 100,000 = 300,000."],["What basis do shareholders take?","Fair market value, 400,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0046","t":"Crown Mills, Inc. owns 100% of Crown Assembly, Inc., and completely liquidates the subsidiary into the parent in 2026. Assembly's assets have an adjusted basis of $200,000 and a fair market value of $900,000; the parent's basis in the subsidiary stock is $500,000. What is the parent's basis in the assets it receives?","mc":{"s":"Crown Mills, Inc. owns 100% of Crown Assembly, Inc., and completely liquidates the subsidiary into the parent in 2026. Assembly's assets have an adjusted basis of $200,000 and a fair market value of $900,000; the parent's basis in the subsidiary stock is $500,000. What is the parent's basis in the assets it receives?","d":"","o":["$200,000","$500,000","$700,000","$900,000"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §332; IRC §334(b); IRC §337","rat":"In a qualifying liquidation of an 80%-owned subsidiary the parent takes a carryover basis in the assets: $200,000.","ex":["In a qualifying liquidation of an 80%-owned subsidiary the parent takes a carryover basis in the assets: $200,000.","Uses the parent's basis in the subsidiary stock, which disappears in the liquidation.","Uses the built-in gain in the assets; it is not a basis amount.","Uses fair market value, which applies in taxable liquidations to non-corporate shareholders."],"wt":["","concept","calc","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"In a qualifying liquidation of an 80% subsidiary into its parent, the parent takes a carryover basis in the assets.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Parent owns at least 80%, so no gain to parent or subsidiary","Parent takes subsidiary's asset basis: 200,000"],"gd":[["Does the parent own at least 80% of the subsidiary?","Yes, 100%."],["What follows for gain and basis?","No gain, and the parent takes the subsidiary's carryover basis of 200,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0047","t":"Tilden Hardware, Inc. is 90% owned by Parent Corp. and 10% owned by an individual, Ray. Tilden completely liquidates and distributes appreciated land to each owner in proportion to ownership. Which statement is correct?","mc":{"s":"Tilden Hardware, Inc. is 90% owned by Parent Corp. and 10% owned by an individual, Ray. Tilden completely liquidates and distributes appreciated land to each owner in proportion to ownership. Which statement is correct?","d":"","o":["Nobody recognizes gain, because the subsidiary was liquidated into a corporate parent that owns most of the stock","Tilden recognizes gain on all of the land, and both Parent and Ray recognize gain or loss on their stock","Parent recognizes no gain; Tilden recognizes gain on the land distributed to Ray, who recognizes gain or loss on his stock","Parent recognizes gain on its share of the land, and Ray recognizes nothing until he sells the land he received"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §332; IRC §336; IRC §337; IRC §331","rat":"The 80% parent qualifies for tax-free treatment on its share, while the distribution to the minority holder is taxable at both the corporate and shareholder level.","ex":["The nonrecognition rule covers only the corporate parent's share; Ray's distribution is taxable.","Distributions to the 80%-or-more corporate owner are tax-free at both levels.","The 80% parent qualifies for tax-free treatment on its share, while the distribution to the minority holder is taxable at both the corporate and shareholder level.","Parent qualifies for nonrecognition, and Ray is taxed on liquidation."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A subsidiary liquidation is tax-free only for the 80% corporate parent's share; the minority holder's share is taxable at both levels.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["How is the 90% parent's share treated in the liquidation?","Tax-free as an 80% parent liquidation."],["How is the 10% owner's share treated?","Taxable to the subsidiary on the land distributed to Ray and to Ray on his stock."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0048","t":"Beacon Systems, Inc. wants to file a consolidated return with its subsidiary Beacon Retail, Inc. What ownership test must be met?","mc":{"s":"Beacon Systems, Inc. wants to file a consolidated return with its subsidiary Beacon Retail, Inc. What ownership test must be met?","d":"","o":["The parent must own stock with at least 80% of the total voting power and at least 80% of the total value of the subsidiary","The parent must own more than 50% of the voting power of the subsidiary","The parent must own at least 80% of the subsidiary's voting power, regardless of value","The parent must own at least 20% of the subsidiary and control the board"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §1504(a); Form 851","rat":"An affiliated group requires a common parent owning at least 80% of both vote and value of each includible subsidiary.","ex":["An affiliated group requires a common parent owning at least 80% of both vote and value of each includible subsidiary.","A majority vote is not enough; 80% of vote and value is required.","Both voting power and value must reach 80%.","A 20% owner is not an affiliated group member."],"wt":["","limit","concept","limit"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A consolidated return requires the parent to own at least 80% of both total voting power and value of the subsidiary.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["What ownership does a common parent need for an affiliated group?","At least 80% of both total voting power and total value of each includible subsidiary."],["Do majority-vote or 20% tests suffice?","No."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0049","t":"Bison Freight, Inc. is the common parent of a consolidated group. During 2026 its wholly owned subsidiary, Bison Local, Inc., paid it a $100,000 dividend. What is the effect on the group's 2026 consolidated taxable income?","mc":{"s":"Bison Freight, Inc. is the common parent of a consolidated group. During 2026 its wholly owned subsidiary, Bison Local, Inc., paid it a $100,000 dividend. What is the effect on the group's 2026 consolidated taxable income?","d":"","o":["The parent includes the $100,000 as income and then deducts 65% of it as a dividend","The parent includes the full $100,000 as income, because dividends from a subsidiary are taxable to it","The parent excludes the dividend and increases its stock basis in the subsidiary by that amount","The dividend is excluded from consolidated taxable income, and the parent reduces its basis in the subsidiary"],"a":4,"cite":"Reg. §1.1502-13(f)(2); Reg. §1.1502-32","rat":"Intercompany distributions within a consolidated group are excluded from income and reduce the parent's basis in the subsidiary stock.","ex":["The 65% rate applies to 20% to 80% owned payors and is not needed within a consolidated group.","Intercompany dividends in a consolidated group are eliminated from income.","The distribution reduces stock basis; it does not increase it.","Intercompany distributions within a consolidated group are excluded from income and reduce the parent's basis in the subsidiary stock."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Intercompany dividends in a consolidated group are excluded from income and reduce the parent's basis in the subsidiary stock.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["How are intercompany distributions treated in a consolidated group?","Excluded from income."],["What happens to the parent's basis in the subsidiary?","It is reduced by the distribution."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0050","t":"Mesa Ridge Corp. began 2026 with an accumulated earnings and profits deficit of $100,000 and had current earnings and profits of $30,000 for 2026, earned evenly. It distributed $20,000 on July 1 to one shareholder and $20,000 on December 1 to another. How much of the December 1 distribution is a dividend?","mc":{"s":"Mesa Ridge Corp. began 2026 with an accumulated earnings and profits deficit of $100,000 and had current earnings and profits of $30,000 for 2026, earned evenly. It distributed $20,000 on July 1 to one shareholder and $20,000 on December 1 to another. How much of the December 1 distribution is a dividend?","d":"","o":["$0","$15,000","$20,000","$30,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §316(a)(2); Reg. §1.316-2","rat":"Current earnings and profits are allocated pro rata among the year's distributions: $30,000 x $20,000 / $40,000 = $15,000 per distribution.","ex":["Nets the current earnings against the accumulated deficit.","Current earnings and profits are allocated pro rata among the year's distributions: $30,000 x $20,000 / $40,000 = $15,000 per distribution. The accumulated deficit does not reduce current earnings and profits.","Treats the whole distribution as a dividend.","Assigns all current earnings and profits to the December distribution."],"wt":["concept","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Current E&P is allocated pro rata among distributions during the year and is not reduced by an accumulated deficit.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Total distributions 40,000","Current E&P allocated 30,000 x (20,000 / 40,000) = 15,000 to each distribution"],"gd":[["How is current E&P allocated among distributions?","Pro rata by amount."],["How much goes to each distribution?","30,000 x 20,000 / 40,000 = 15,000."],["Does the accumulated deficit reduce it?","No, so the December distribution has 15,000 of dividend."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-0052","t":"Wells Corridor Corp. completely liquidated in 2026, and Jon Ames, its only shareholder, received $250,000 in cash. Jon's stock basis is $80,000 and he has held the stock for five years. His draft return reports the $170,000 as an ordinary dividend. Which correction is needed?","mc":{"s":"Wells Corridor Corp. completely liquidated in 2026, and Jon Ames, its only shareholder, received $250,000 in cash. Jon's stock basis is $80,000 and he has held the stock for five years. His draft return reports the $170,000 as an ordinary dividend. Which correction is needed?","d":"","o":["Report a $250,000 long-term capital gain, with no basis offset","Report a $170,000 long-term capital gain, because a complete liquidation is treated as a sale of the stock","Report a $170,000 dividend, because liquidating distributions are dividends to the extent of value over basis","Report no gain, because liquidating distributions are a tax-free return of basis until basis is recovered, and the excess is deferred"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §331(a); IRC §1222(3)","rat":"In a complete liquidation the shareholder computes gain or loss on the exchange of stock, which here is long-term capital gain.","ex":["Gain is measured after subtracting the $80,000 basis.","In a complete liquidation the shareholder computes gain or loss on the exchange of stock, which here is long-term capital gain.","Liquidating distributions are exchanges, not dividends.","The full gain is recognized in the liquidation year."],"wt":["calc","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A complete liquidation is treated as a sale of stock, so the shareholder reports capital gain after subtracting basis.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Amount received 250,000 - basis 80,000 = 170,000 long-term capital gain"],"gd":[["How is a shareholder treated in a complete liquidation?","As exchanging stock, computing gain or loss."],["What is the gain and its character?","250,000 - 80,000 = 170,000 long-term capital gain, not a dividend."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-1002","t":"Rita Calloway transfers land (adjusted basis $40,000, fair market value $200,000) to a new corporation for 50% of its stock. Sam Ives transfers $200,000 in cash for the other 50%. Under a binding contract signed before the transfers, Sam sells half of his shares (25% of the corporation) to Tom Reed, an unrelated buyer, on the day after formation. Compared with the result if there were no such contract, which statement is correct?","mc":{"s":"Rita Calloway transfers land (adjusted basis $40,000, fair market value $200,000) to a new corporation for 50% of its stock. Sam Ives transfers $200,000 in cash for the other 50%. Under a binding contract signed before the transfers, Sam sells half of his shares (25% of the corporation) to Tom Reed, an unrelated buyer, on the day after formation. Compared with the result if there were no such contract, which statement is correct?","d":"","o":["Rita recognizes $160,000 of gain, and her stock basis and the corporation's basis in the land are both $200,000","Rita recognizes no gain, and her stock basis and the corporation's basis in the land are both $40,000, because Sam's later sale does not affect control","Rita recognizes $160,000 of gain and has a $200,000 stock basis, but the corporation keeps a $40,000 carryover basis in the land","Rita recognizes $160,000 of gain, but her stock basis stays $40,000 and the corporation takes a $200,000 basis in the land"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §351(a); IRC §368(c); IRC §1012; IRC §362(a); Rev. Rul. 70-140","rat":"The pre-arranged sale leaves the transferors with only 75% (Rita 50% + Sam 25%), below the 80% control test, so section 351 does not apply.","ex":["The pre-arranged sale leaves the transferors with only 75% (Rita 50% + Sam 25%), below the 80% control test, so section 351 does not apply. The exchange is taxable: Rita's gain is $200,000 - $40,000 = $160,000, and both bases equal the $200,000 value.","Ignores the binding contract. A pre-arranged sale is counted, so the transferors do not have 80% control immediately after the exchange.","Splits the treatment. If the exchange is taxable, the corporation's basis is cost ($200,000); carryover basis applies only under section 351.","Mixes the results. In a taxable exchange Rita's stock basis is its cost ($200,000), not a carryover of her land basis."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Section 351 needs the transferors to control 80% of the stock immediately after the exchange, and a binding pre-arranged sale of some of the stock can break that control.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Control after: Rita 50% + Sam 50% x 50% = 25% -> 75% < 80%, so section 351 fails","Rita: amount realized 200,000 - basis 40,000 = 160,000 gain; stock basis and corporation land basis = 200,000"],"gd":[["What control test applies to the exchange?","Transferors must hold at least 80% immediately after, and a binding prior sale counts against them."],["What do the transferors hold after Sam's sale?","Rita 50% plus Sam 25% = 75%, below 80%, so section 351 fails."],["What follows?","Rita's gain is 200,000 - 40,000 = 160,000 and both bases equal the 200,000 value."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-1003","t":"Corvina Tools, Inc. has 100 shares of voting common stock outstanding and substantial earnings and profits. Ann Reyes owns 60 shares, and her brother Ben owns the other 40. Ann is considering two alternative plans: under Plan 1 the corporation redeems 25 of her shares for cash, and under Plan 2 it redeems 20 of her shares for cash. Which statement is correct?","mc":{"s":"Corvina Tools, Inc. has 100 shares of voting common stock outstanding and substantial earnings and profits. Ann Reyes owns 60 shares, and her brother Ben owns the other 40. Ann is considering two alternative plans: under Plan 1 the corporation redeems 25 of her shares for cash, and under Plan 2 it redeems 20 of her shares for cash. Which statement is correct?","d":"","o":["Both plans are treated as exchanges because any redemption from a majority shareholder reduces her interest","Neither plan is an exchange, because Ann is treated as owning Ben's shares through family attribution","Only Plan 2 is an exchange, because she still owns 50% of the voting power afterward","Plan 1 is treated as an exchange because it is substantially disproportionate, and Plan 2 is a dividend to the extent of earnings and profits"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §302(b)(2); IRC §318(a)(1); IRC §301(c)(1)","rat":"Ben, as a brother, is not attributed to Ann.","ex":["There is no such rule. A redemption is an exchange only if a test such as the substantially disproportionate test is met.","Attribution runs among spouses, children, grandchildren and parents, not siblings. Ben's shares are not treated as Ann's.","Owning 50% afterward fails the substantially disproportionate test, which requires her ownership to fall below 50% of the voting power and below 80% of her prior percentage.","Ben, as a brother, is not attributed to Ann. After Plan 1 she owns 35 of 75 shares (46.7%), under 50% and under 80% of her prior 60% (48%). After Plan 2 she owns 40 of 80 (50%), which fails both tests, so it is a dividend."],"wt":["concept","concept","limit",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A redemption is an exchange only if a test such as the 80%/50% substantially disproportionate test is met after family attribution, which does not include siblings; otherwise it is a dividend.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Plan 1: 60 - 25 = 35 of 100 - 25 = 75 shares = 46.7%; 80% x 60% = 48%; 46.7% < 48% and < 50%, so it qualifies","Plan 2: 60 - 20 = 40 of 80 shares = 50%; not below 50% and above 48%, so it fails and is a section 301 distribution","Brother is not a section 318(a)(1) family member"],"gd":[["Is a brother's stock attributed to Ann?","No, family attribution does not include siblings."],["How do the plans fare on the substantially-disproportionate test?","Plan 1 leaves 35 of 75 shares, 46.7%, below 50% and below 80% of her prior 60%, which is 48%."],["What about Plan 2?","40 of 80 shares is 50%, which fails, so it is a dividend to the extent of E&P."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-1004","t":"Halvern Industries, Inc., a C corporation owned entirely by Grace Lund, holds operating assets with an adjusted basis of $200,000 and a fair market value of $800,000, and it has no liabilities. Grace's stock basis is $150,000. A buyer offers $800,000 either for the assets (Plan 1, after which Halvern liquidates and distributes all remaining cash to Grace) or for Grace's stock (Plan 2). Assume the corporation's tax rate is 21%, Grace's long-term capital gain rate is 20%, and no other tax applies. By how much is the combined federal income tax under Plan 1 higher than under Plan 2?","mc":{"s":"Halvern Industries, Inc., a C corporation owned entirely by Grace Lund, holds operating assets with an adjusted basis of $200,000 and a fair market value of $800,000, and it has no liabilities. Grace's stock basis is $150,000. A buyer offers $800,000 either for the assets (Plan 1, after which Halvern liquidates and distributes all remaining cash to Grace) or for Grace's stock (Plan 2). Assume the corporation's tax rate is 21%, Grace's long-term capital gain rate is 20%, and no other tax applies. By how much is the combined federal income tax under Plan 1 higher than under Plan 2?","d":"","o":["$100,800","$104,800","$126,000","$230,800"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §1001; IRC §331(a); IRC §336(a); IRC §11(b)","rat":"Plan 1: corporate tax $126,000; Grace receives $674,000 and has a $524,000 gain taxed at 20% ($104,800); total $230,800.","ex":["Plan 1: corporate tax $126,000; Grace receives $674,000 and has a $524,000 gain taxed at 20% ($104,800); total $230,800. Plan 2: $650,000 x 20% = $130,000. Difference $100,800.","Counts only the shareholder-level tax under Plan 1 ($104,800) and ignores the corporate tax and Plan 2.","Equals the corporate tax alone. It results from taking Grace's Plan 1 gain on the full $650,000 (ignoring that the corporate tax reduces what she receives), so the two shareholder-level taxes cancel.","Reports the total Plan 1 tax ($230,800) rather than the difference from Plan 2."],"wt":["","calc","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"An asset sale followed by liquidation taxes the gain at both the corporate and shareholder levels, while a stock sale taxes it once at the shareholder level.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Plan 1 corporate tax: 21% x (800,000 - 200,000) = 126,000; distribution 800,000 - 126,000 = 674,000","Grace: 674,000 - 150,000 = 524,000 x 20% = 104,800; total Plan 1 = 230,800","Plan 2: (800,000 - 150,000) x 20% = 130,000","230,800 - 130,000 = 100,800"],"gd":[["What is the corporate tax under Plan 1?","21% x (800,000 - 200,000) = 126,000, leaving 674,000 to distribute."],["What is Grace's tax and the total under Plan 1?","(674,000 - 150,000) x 20% = 104,800, total 230,800."],["What is Plan 2 and the difference?","(800,000 - 150,000) x 20% = 130,000, so 230,800 - 130,000 = 100,800."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-B-1007","t":"Parent Corp. and its wholly owned subsidiaries Sub A and Sub B file a consolidated return on a calendar year. In 2026, Sub A sold land (adjusted basis $40,000) to Sub B for $100,000. In 2027, Sub B sold the land to an outside buyer for $75,000. Which statement correctly describes the group's recognized gain or loss?","mc":{"s":"Parent Corp. and its wholly owned subsidiaries Sub A and Sub B file a consolidated return on a calendar year. In 2026, Sub A sold land (adjusted basis $40,000) to Sub B for $100,000. In 2027, Sub B sold the land to an outside buyer for $75,000. Which statement correctly describes the group's recognized gain or loss?","d":"","o":["A $60,000 gain in 2026 and a $25,000 loss in 2027","No gain in 2026, and a $25,000 loss in 2027","A $60,000 gain in 2026, and a $35,000 gain in 2027","No gain in 2026, and a $35,000 net gain in 2027"],"a":4,"cite":"Reg. §1.1502-13(c); Reg. §1.1502-13(d); IRC §1012","rat":"Sub A's $60,000 intercompany gain is deferred and is taken into account in 2027 when the land leaves the group, together with Sub B's $25,000 loss ($100,000 basis - $75,000).","ex":["This is the separate-return result. In a consolidated return, Sub A's gain on the intercompany sale is deferred until the land is sold outside the group.","Reports only Sub B's loss. Sub A's deferred $60,000 gain is taken into account at the same time as Sub B's outside sale.","Double counts. Sub A's gain is not recognized in 2026, and the group's total gain over both years equals only $35,000.","Sub A's $60,000 intercompany gain is deferred and is taken into account in 2027 when the land leaves the group, together with Sub B's $25,000 loss ($100,000 basis - $75,000). The group reports $35,000 of net gain, its actual economic gain."],"wt":["concept","calc","calc",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"B","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"In a consolidated return an intercompany gain is deferred and matched with the buyer's outside sale, so the group reports its single economic result when the property leaves the group.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Sub A intercompany gain: 100,000 - 40,000 = 60,000, deferred in 2026","Sub B outside sale in 2027: 75,000 - 100,000 = -25,000","2027 group result: 60,000 - 25,000 = 35,000 net gain"],"gd":[["What is Sub A's intercompany gain in 2026 and how is it treated?","100,000 - 40,000 = 60,000, deferred."],["What is Sub B's outside sale result?","75,000 - 100,000 = a 25,000 loss."],["What does the group report in 2027?","60,000 - 25,000 = 35,000 net gain, and none in 2026."]]}}],"5":[{"i":"REG-V-C-0001","t":"Which of the following may be a shareholder of an S corporation?","mc":{"s":"Which of the following may be a shareholder of an S corporation?","d":"","o":["Elm Partners, a general partnership","A nonresident alien individual","The estate of a deceased shareholder","A domestic C corporation"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §1361(b)(1)(B); IRC §1361(c)(2); Form 2553 instructions","rat":"A domestic estate is a permitted S corporation shareholder.","ex":["Partnerships are not permitted shareholders.","Nonresident aliens are not permitted shareholders.","A domestic estate is a permitted S corporation shareholder.","Corporations are not permitted shareholders of an S corporation."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Permitted S corporation shareholders include individuals who are citizens or residents, estates and certain trusts, but not partnerships, corporations or nonresident aliens.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Who may hold S corporation stock?","Individuals who are U.S. citizens or residents, estates, and certain trusts."],["Which listed holder is permitted?","The estate of a deceased shareholder; partnerships, nonresident aliens and C corporations are not."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0002","t":"Briar Creek, Inc. wants to elect S status. It has 95 unrelated individual shareholders, plus 20 more shareholders who are all lineal descendants of one common ancestor (or their spouses or former spouses). All are U.S. residents. Does Briar Creek meet the shareholder-count limit?","mc":{"s":"Briar Creek, Inc. wants to elect S status. It has 95 unrelated individual shareholders, plus 20 more shareholders who are all lineal descendants of one common ancestor (or their spouses or former spouses). All are U.S. residents. Does Briar Creek meet the shareholder-count limit?","d":"","o":["Yes, because the family members are treated as one shareholder, so it has 96 shareholders","No, because it has 115 shareholders, which exceeds the limit of 100","No, because family members may be aggregated only if they file a written election","Yes, because the limit applies only to shareholders with voting stock"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §1361(b)(1)(A); IRC §1361(c)(1)","rat":"All members of a family (common ancestor and lineal descendants and their spouses) are treated as one shareholder for the 100-shareholder limit.","ex":["All members of a family (common ancestor and lineal descendants and their spouses) are treated as one shareholder for the 100-shareholder limit.","The family aggregation rule reduces the count to 96.","Family members are aggregated automatically; no election is needed.","The limit counts shareholders, not voting shareholders."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Family members are automatically treated as one shareholder for the 100-shareholder S corporation limit.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["95 unrelated shareholders","20 family members count as 1","Total 96, not over 100"],"gd":[["How are family members counted toward the 100 limit?","All lineal descendants of a common ancestor and their spouses count as one shareholder."],["What is the total count?","95 + 1 = 96, not over 100."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0003","t":"Ferro Components, Inc., an S corporation, has two arrangements under consideration. Which one would create a second class of stock and terminate the S election?","mc":{"s":"Ferro Components, Inc., an S corporation, has two arrangements under consideration. Which one would create a second class of stock and terminate the S election?","d":"","o":["Nonvoting common shares with the same distribution and liquidation rights as voting common shares","A one-year disproportionate distribution that the governing documents do not authorize","A shareholder loan that meets the straight-debt safe harbor","Class B shares that entitle the holder to twice the per-share liquidation proceeds of Class A shares"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §1361(b)(1)(D); Reg. §1.1361-1(l)","rat":"Shares with different rights to distribution or liquidation proceeds create a second class of stock.","ex":["Voting differences alone are allowed.","Disproportionate distributions do not create a second class if the governing provisions give identical rights.","Straight debt meeting the safe harbor is not treated as a second class of stock.","Shares with different rights to distribution or liquidation proceeds create a second class of stock."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Shares with different distribution or liquidation rights create a second class of stock, while voting differences alone do not.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["What creates a second class of stock?","Different rights to distributions or liquidation proceeds."],["Which arrangement gives one class twice the liquidation proceeds?","The Class B shares with double per-share liquidation proceeds; voting differences, unauthorized one-time disproportion and safe-harbor debt do not."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0004","t":"Which corporation is ineligible to elect S status?","mc":{"s":"Which corporation is ineligible to elect S status?","d":"","o":["A bakery corporation with 12 individual shareholders","A bank that uses the direct charge-off method for bad debts","A domestic insurance company taxed under the rules for insurance companies","A domestic corporation with accumulated earnings and profits from earlier C years"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §1361(b)(2)","rat":"Insurance companies taxed under subchapter L are ineligible corporations.","ex":["An ordinary domestic operating corporation with eligible shareholders is eligible.","Only banks that use the reserve method are ineligible.","Insurance companies taxed under subchapter L are ineligible corporations.","Accumulated earnings and profits do not bar an election; they only matter for later distributions and passive income rules."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Insurance companies taxed under subchapter L are ineligible for S status, while accumulated E&P does not bar the election.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Which types of corporations cannot elect S status?","Ineligible corporations such as insurance companies taxed under subchapter L."],["Which listed corporation is that?","The domestic insurance company taxed under insurance rules."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0005","t":"Lakeshore Ceramics, Inc. was formed on February 1, 2026. It issued stock, acquired assets and began business on that day. It wants S status to apply from its first day. By what date must Form 2553 be filed, with all shareholders consenting?","mc":{"s":"Lakeshore Ceramics, Inc. was formed on February 1, 2026. It issued stock, acquired assets and began business on that day. It wants S status to apply from its first day. By what date must Form 2553 be filed, with all shareholders consenting?","d":"","o":["April 15, 2026, the 15th day of the third month","March 15, 2026, the 15th day of the third month of a calendar year","December 31, 2026, the last day of the tax year","May 15, 2026, the 15th day of the fourth month of the tax year"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §1362(b); Form 2553 instructions; Rev. Proc. 2013-30; IRC §1362(b)(1)(B)","rat":"The election must be made no later than the 15th day of the third month of the tax year to be effective for that year.","ex":["The election must be made no later than the 15th day of the third month of the tax year to be effective for that year.","March 15 applies to a corporation with a January 1 start; here the tax year began February 1.","Filing after the 15th day of the third month makes the election effective for the following year.","That is the 15th day of the fourth month, which is one month too late."],"wt":["","limit","limit","limit"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Form 2553 must be filed by the 15th day of the third month of the tax year to be effective for that year.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["By when must the election be made to apply to the first year?","The 15th day of the third month of the tax year."],["What date is that for a February 1 start?","April 15, 2026."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0006","t":"Ridgecrest Homes, Inc., an S corporation, has 1,000 shares outstanding: 500 voting and 500 nonvoting. Carla holds 300 voting and 100 nonvoting shares. Dev holds 100 voting and 300 nonvoting shares. Others hold the remaining 200 shares. Who can revoke the S election without the consent of anyone else?","mc":{"s":"Ridgecrest Homes, Inc., an S corporation, has 1,000 shares outstanding: 500 voting and 500 nonvoting. Carla holds 300 voting and 100 nonvoting shares. Dev holds 100 voting and 300 nonvoting shares. Others hold the remaining 200 shares. Who can revoke the S election without the consent of anyone else?","d":"","o":["Carla alone, because she holds 60% of the voting shares","Dev alone, because he holds 60% of the nonvoting shares","Any single shareholder may revoke, since the election requires unanimous consent to make but not to revoke","Carla and Dev together, who hold 80% of all shares"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §1362(d)(1); Reg. §1.1362-6","rat":"A revocation requires consent of shareholders holding more than half of all shares, voting and nonvoting.","ex":["Revocation is measured on all shares, including nonvoting ones. Carla holds only 40% of all shares.","Dev also holds only 40% of all shares.","Revocation requires the consent of holders of more than half the shares.","A revocation requires consent of shareholders holding more than half of all shares, voting and nonvoting. Together they hold 80% of all shares."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Revoking an S election requires consent of holders of more than half of all shares, voting and nonvoting.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Carla 300 + 100 = 400 of 1,000 shares = 40%","Dev 100 + 300 = 400 of 1,000 shares = 40%","Together 800 = 80% > 50%"],"gd":[["What consent is needed to revoke an S election?","Shareholders holding more than half of all shares, voting and nonvoting."],["How many shares do Carla and Dev each hold and together?","400 of 1,000 each, so 800 or 80% together."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0007","t":"Cedar Hollow, Inc. is an S corporation that was a C corporation earlier and still has accumulated earnings and profits. Passive investment income exceeded 25% of gross receipts in each of 2024, 2025 and 2026. When does its S status terminate?","mc":{"s":"Cedar Hollow, Inc. is an S corporation that was a C corporation earlier and still has accumulated earnings and profits. Passive investment income exceeded 25% of gross receipts in each of 2024, 2025 and 2026. When does its S status terminate?","d":"","o":["On December 31, 2024, after the first year of excess passive income","On the date passive income first exceeded 25%, in 2026","On January 1, 2027, the first day of the tax year after the third consecutive year","It does not terminate; the corporation pays only a corporate-level tax each year"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §1362(d)(3); IRC §1375","rat":"The election terminates on the first day of the first tax year beginning after the third consecutive tax year in which the corporation had accumulated earnings and profits and excess passive income.","ex":["Three consecutive years are required.","Termination is effective on the first day of the next tax year, not the date the threshold is crossed.","The election terminates on the first day of the first tax year beginning after the third consecutive tax year in which the corporation had accumulated earnings and profits and excess passive income.","The corporate-level tax applies each year, but three consecutive years also terminate the election."],"wt":["limit","limit","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"An S election terminates on the first day of the year after three consecutive years of excess passive income with accumulated E&P.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["What conditions trigger termination for passive income?","Accumulated E&P and excess passive income in three consecutive years."],["When does termination take effect?","The first day of the first tax year after the third year, January 1, 2027."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0008","t":"On September 10, 2026, Sofia Reyes, a shareholder of a calendar-year S corporation, sold 20% of the stock to a partnership. Before then the corporation met all requirements. From what date is the corporation a C corporation?","mc":{"s":"On September 10, 2026, Sofia Reyes, a shareholder of a calendar-year S corporation, sold 20% of the stock to a partnership. Before then the corporation met all requirements. From what date is the corporation a C corporation?","d":"","o":["September 10, 2026, the date of the terminating event","January 1, 2026, the start of the year","January 1, 2027, the start of the next year","December 31, 2026, the end of the year"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §1362(d)(2); IRC §1362(e)","rat":"Ceasing to be a small business corporation terminates the election on the day the event occurs.","ex":["Ceasing to be a small business corporation terminates the election on the day the event occurs. The year splits into S and C short years.","Termination is not retroactive; it is effective on the day of the event.","Termination for ineligibility is effective on the date of the event, not the next year.","The termination date is the event date, not year-end."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"An S election terminates on the day the corporation ceases to qualify, splitting the year into S and C short years.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["What effect does a partnership becoming a shareholder have?","The corporation ceases to be a small business corporation."],["When does the election terminate?","On the day of the event, September 10, 2026, splitting the year into S and C short years."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0009","t":"An S corporation election terminated effective in 2026, so 2026 is the corporation's first C corporation year. Absent consent from the IRS, what is the earliest year for which it may elect S status again?","mc":{"s":"An S corporation election terminated effective in 2026, so 2026 is the corporation's first C corporation year. Absent consent from the IRS, what is the earliest year for which it may elect S status again?","d":"","o":["2027, the next year","2029, the third year after termination","2036, ten years after termination","2031, the fifth taxable year after the first C corporation year"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §1362(g)","rat":"A corporation may not re-elect S status before its fifth taxable year after the first year the termination took effect, without IRS consent.","ex":["A waiting period applies after termination.","The waiting period is five taxable years, not three.","There is no ten-year rule; the wait is five taxable years.","A corporation may not re-elect S status before its fifth taxable year after the first year the termination took effect, without IRS consent."],"wt":["limit","limit","limit",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A corporation whose S election terminated may not re-elect before its fifth taxable year after the first C year without IRS consent.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Is immediate re-election allowed after termination?","No, a waiting period applies without IRS consent."],["Which year is the earliest?","The fifth taxable year after the first C corporation year, 2031."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0010","t":"Marlin Yard Supply, Inc. filed Form 2553 in 2026, listing the shareholders shown. All shareholders consented and the corporation is otherwise eligible. Which shareholder makes the corporation ineligible?","mc":{"s":"Marlin Yard Supply, Inc. filed Form 2553 in 2026, listing the shareholders shown. All shareholders consented and the corporation is otherwise eligible. Which shareholder makes the corporation ineligible?","d":"","o":["Ada Kim, a U.S. citizen individual","The Ada Kim Revocable Trust, a grantor trust deemed owned by Ada","Northridge Holdings LLC, a limited liability company taxed as a partnership","Ben Ortiz, a U.S. resident alien individual"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §1361(b)(1)(B); IRC §1361(c)(2)(A)(i); Form 2553","rat":"An entity taxed as a partnership cannot be an S corporation shareholder, so the election is invalid.","ex":["U.S. citizen individuals are permitted shareholders.","A grantor trust deemed owned by a U.S. individual is a permitted shareholder.","An entity taxed as a partnership cannot be an S corporation shareholder, so the election is invalid.","A resident alien is a permitted shareholder; only nonresident aliens are excluded."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Entities taxed as partnerships cannot be S corporation shareholders, while citizens, grantor trusts and resident aliens can.","tb":[["Shareholder","Description"],["Ada Kim","U.S. citizen individual"],["Ada Kim Revocable Trust","Grantor trust owned by Ada"],["Ben Ortiz","U.S. resident alien individual"],["Northridge Holdings LLC","LLC taxed as a partnership"]],"from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Which entities can hold S stock?","U.S. citizen or resident individuals and grantor trusts, but not partnerships."],["Which shareholder is disqualifying?","The LLC taxed as a partnership."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0044","t":"Orchard Bay Supply, Inc. was formed and began business on January 1, 2026, and intended to be an S corporation from that date. Its shareholders signed Form 2553, but it was not filed until July 10, 2026. The corporation otherwise met every S requirement. Which statement is correct?","mc":{"s":"Orchard Bay Supply, Inc. was formed and began business on January 1, 2026, and intended to be an S corporation from that date. Its shareholders signed Form 2553, but it was not filed until July 10, 2026. The corporation otherwise met every S requirement. Which statement is correct?","d":"","o":["The election is effective January 1, 2026, because Form 2553 may be filed at any time during the tax year and is still timely","The election is permanently invalid, because a late Form 2553 cannot be corrected by the IRS under any procedure","The election is effective for 2026 but only for the portion of the year after the July 10 filing date","The election is not effective for 2026 unless the IRS grants late-election relief, which requires reasonable cause and a timely request"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §1362(b)(5); Rev. Proc. 2013-30","rat":"An election filed after the 15th day of the third month is late.","ex":["The election must be filed by the 15th day of the third month to be effective for that year.","Relief provisions allow a late election to be treated as timely.","An election is effective for the whole year or not at all for that year, not from the filing date.","An election filed after the 15th day of the third month is late. Rev. Proc. 2013-30 offers relief within 3 years and 75 days of the intended effective date when reasonable cause exists."],"wt":["limit","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A Form 2553 filed after the 15th day of the third month is late and needs IRS late-election relief for that year.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["When is a Form 2553 timely for an election effective from the start of the year?","By the 15th day of the third month."],["Is a July 10 filing timely and what is needed?","No, it is late, so the election needs late-election relief with reasonable cause and a timely request."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-1003","t":"Three trusts hold shares of Marlin Works, Inc., which wants to elect S status effective January 1, 2026. Trust 1 is a revocable trust wholly owned by its grantor, a U.S. citizen. Trust 2 is an irrevocable trust with one current income beneficiary, a U.S. citizen who must receive all income currently, and principal may be paid only to her. Trust 3 is an irrevocable trust that accumulates income for the benefit of three U.S. citizen children. Which statement is correct?","mc":{"s":"Three trusts hold shares of Marlin Works, Inc., which wants to elect S status effective January 1, 2026. Trust 1 is a revocable trust wholly owned by its grantor, a U.S. citizen. Trust 2 is an irrevocable trust with one current income beneficiary, a U.S. citizen who must receive all income currently, and principal may be paid only to her. Trust 3 is an irrevocable trust that accumulates income for the benefit of three U.S. citizen children. Which statement is correct?","d":"","o":["All three trusts are eligible without any election, because trusts may hold S corporation stock","Trust 1 needs an ESBT election, Trust 2 can qualify only as an ESBT, and Trust 3 can qualify through a QSST election","Trust 3 qualifies only through a QSST election because it has several beneficiaries, and Trust 2 can qualify only as an ESBT","Trust 1 is eligible without any trust election, Trust 2 can qualify through a QSST or an ESBT election, and Trust 3 can qualify only through an ESBT election"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §1361(c)(2)(A)(i); IRC §1361(d); IRC §1361(e)","rat":"A grantor trust owned by a U.S.","ex":["Only grantor trusts (and a few testamentary or voting trusts) are eligible by themselves. Other trusts need a QSST or ESBT election.","Reverses the rules. A QSST allows only one income beneficiary, so it cannot fit Trust 3, and a wholly owned grantor trust needs no election.","The QSST is for a single income beneficiary and the ESBT is the choice for several beneficiaries, so this swaps them.","A grantor trust owned by a U.S. citizen is a permitted shareholder. Trust 2 meets the QSST requirements (one income beneficiary, all income distributed) and may also elect ESBT. Trust 3 has several beneficiaries and accumulates income, so only an ESBT election works."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Only grantor trusts are S shareholders on their own; other trusts need a QSST (one income beneficiary, all income distributed) or an ESBT (multiple beneficiaries, accumulation allowed) election.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Which trust is a permitted shareholder without an election?","A grantor trust wholly owned by a U.S. citizen."],["Which election fits a trust with one income beneficiary and all income distributed currently?","It meets QSST requirements and may also choose ESBT."],["What about the accumulation trust with several children?","It fits only an ESBT election."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-1004","t":"Quill Works, Inc. was a calendar-year S corporation owned entirely by Hazel Robb. Its S election terminated effective June 1, 2026, when it created a second class of stock. At the end of the last S day (May 31, 2026), its accumulated adjustments account was $40,000 and Hazel's stock basis was $60,000. The corporation has $30,000 of earnings and profits. On December 20, 2026, it distributed $50,000 in cash to Hazel. Which statement is correct?","mc":{"s":"Quill Works, Inc. was a calendar-year S corporation owned entirely by Hazel Robb. Its S election terminated effective June 1, 2026, when it created a second class of stock. At the end of the last S day (May 31, 2026), its accumulated adjustments account was $40,000 and Hazel's stock basis was $60,000. The corporation has $30,000 of earnings and profits. On December 20, 2026, it distributed $50,000 in cash to Hazel. Which statement is correct?","d":"","o":["The entire $50,000 is a dividend, because the corporation is now a C corporation","$40,000 is tax-free and reduces her basis to $20,000, and $10,000 is a dividend","The entire $50,000 is tax-free, because it does not exceed her $60,000 stock basis","$30,000 is a dividend and $20,000 reduces basis, because distributions come from earnings and profits first"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §1371(e)(1); IRC §1377(b); IRC §301(c)","rat":"The distribution falls within the post-termination transition period (one year after the last S day, to May 31, 2027).","ex":["Ignores the post-termination transition period, during which distributions of money are tax-free to the extent of the accumulated adjustments account.","The distribution falls within the post-termination transition period (one year after the last S day, to May 31, 2027). Money is tax-free to the extent of the accumulated adjustments account ($40,000), and the remaining $10,000 is a dividend from earnings and profits.","Only the accumulated adjustments account amount is tax-free in the transition period. The excess is taxed as a dividend to the extent of earnings and profits.","Reverses the order. In the post-termination transition period the accumulated adjustments account is applied first, then earnings and profits."],"wt":["concept","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"For one year after an S election ends, cash distributions are tax-free up to the accumulated adjustments account and only then are dividends from earnings and profits.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Last S day 5/31/2026; the transition period runs to the later of 5/31/2027 or the due date of the last S return, so 12/20/2026 is inside it","AAA first: 40,000 tax-free, basis 60,000 - 40,000 = 20,000","Remaining 50,000 - 40,000 = 10,000 <= earnings and profits 30,000, so a 10,000 dividend"],"gd":[["Is the December distribution inside the post-termination transition period?","Yes, it runs one year past the last S day, May 31, 2027."],["How much is tax-free from AAA and what is basis afterward?","40,000 tax-free, so basis is 60,000 - 40,000 = 20,000."],["What is left and how is it taxed?","50,000 - 40,000 = 10,000, a dividend within E&P of 30,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-1006","t":"Calico Products, Inc. is a calendar-year S corporation with three shareholders: Ava Tran (45% of the shares, voting), Ben Ruiz (30%, nonvoting) and Cara Diaz (25%, voting). On March 10, 2026, Ava alone signed and filed a statement revoking the S election, with no effective date specified. On March 20, 2026, Ava and Cara together signed and filed a new revocation statement, again with no effective date. Which statement is correct?","mc":{"s":"Calico Products, Inc. is a calendar-year S corporation with three shareholders: Ava Tran (45% of the shares, voting), Ben Ruiz (30%, nonvoting) and Cara Diaz (25%, voting). On March 10, 2026, Ava alone signed and filed a statement revoking the S election, with no effective date specified. On March 20, 2026, Ava and Cara together signed and filed a new revocation statement, again with no effective date. Which statement is correct?","d":"","o":["The March 10 revocation is valid and takes effect January 1, 2026, because a revocation made in the first 2.5 months applies to the whole year","The March 20 revocation is valid and takes effect March 20, 2026, so the year splits into S and C short years","Neither revocation is valid, because a revocation requires the consent of every shareholder, including the nonvoting shareholder","The March 20 revocation is valid and takes effect January 1, 2027, so the corporation remains an S corporation for all of 2026"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §1362(d)(1); Reg. §1.1362-6(a)(3)","rat":"A revocation needs consent of shareholders holding more than half of all shares, voting and nonvoting.","ex":["The timing rule is right for a valid revocation, but Ava's 45% is not more than half of all shares, so the March 10 statement is invalid.","With no effective date specified, a revocation filed after the 15th day of the third month takes effect on the first day of the next year. It does not split the year.","Only shareholders holding more than half of the shares (voting and nonvoting) must consent, not all of them.","A revocation needs consent of shareholders holding more than half of all shares, voting and nonvoting. Ava alone holds 45%, so March 10 fails; Ava and Cara hold 70%. A valid revocation filed after March 15 (the 15th day of the third month) is effective the next January 1."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"An S revocation needs consent of holders of more than half of all shares, and one filed after the 15th day of the third month takes effect the next January 1.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["March 10 statement: Ava 45% is not more than 50%, invalid","March 20 statement: Ava + Cara = 45% + 25% = 70% > 50%, valid; filed after March 15, so effective January 1, 2027"],"gd":[["What consent does a revocation need?","Holders of more than half of all shares, voting and nonvoting."],["Which statement meets that?","Ava alone has 45%, so it fails; Ava and Cara have 45% + 25% = 70%, so the second is valid."],["When is a valid revocation filed after March 15 effective?","The next January 1, so January 1, 2027."]]}}],"6":[{"i":"REG-V-C-0011","t":"Tamarack Design, Inc., an S corporation, had 2026 revenue of $900,000, cost of goods sold of $400,000, wages of $200,000 and regular depreciation of $50,000. It also had a $30,000 immediate expensing deduction, a $10,000 charitable contribution, $6,000 of interest income, $4,000 of tax-exempt interest and a $20,000 long-term capital gain. What is its ordinary business income?","mc":{"s":"Tamarack Design, Inc., an S corporation, had 2026 revenue of $900,000, cost of goods sold of $400,000, wages of $200,000 and regular depreciation of $50,000. It also had a $30,000 immediate expensing deduction, a $10,000 charitable contribution, $6,000 of interest income, $4,000 of tax-exempt interest and a $20,000 long-term capital gain. What is its ordinary business income?","d":"","o":["$220,000","$240,000","$250,000","$276,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §1363(b); IRC §1366(a)(1); Form 1120-S instructions","rat":"Revenue less COGS, wages and depreciation is $250,000.","ex":["Deducts the immediate expensing amount in ordinary income; it is separately stated.","Deducts the charitable contribution in ordinary income; it is a separately stated item.","Revenue less COGS, wages and depreciation is $250,000. The immediate expensing deduction, charitable gift, interest, tax-exempt interest and capital gain are separately stated.","Includes interest income and the capital gain, which are separately stated to shareholders."],"wt":["calc","calc","","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"S corporation ordinary income excludes separately stated items such as section 179, charity, interest and capital gains.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["900,000 - 400,000 - 200,000 - 50,000 = 250,000","Separately stated: expensing 30,000, charity 10,000, interest 6,000, tax-exempt interest 4,000, capital gain 20,000"],"gd":[["What is ordinary business income?","Revenue less COGS, wages and depreciation: 900,000 - 400,000 - 200,000 - 50,000 = 250,000."],["Which items are separately stated?","Immediate expensing, charity, interest, tax-exempt interest and capital gain."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0012","t":"Falcon Ridge, Inc., a calendar-year S corporation that has not elected to close its books, had $365,000 of ordinary business income for 2026. Wes Palmer owned 40% of the stock from January 1 through March 31 and sold all of it on March 31. How much ordinary business income is allocated to Wes?","mc":{"s":"Falcon Ridge, Inc., a calendar-year S corporation that has not elected to close its books, had $365,000 of ordinary business income for 2026. Wes Palmer owned 40% of the stock from January 1 through March 31 and sold all of it on March 31. How much ordinary business income is allocated to Wes?","d":"","o":["$36,000","$73,000","$90,000","$146,000"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §1377(a)(1); Reg §1.1377-1(a)(2)(ii)","rat":"Income is allocated per share per day.","ex":["Income is allocated per share per day. Wes owned 40% for 90 days (January 1 through March 31, the day of sale counting as a day of ownership): $365,000 x 90/365 x 40% = $36,000.","Uses a half-year holding period instead of 90 days.","Prorates by days but does not apply Wes's 40% ownership.","Allocates 40% of the full year, ignoring that Wes owned the stock for only 90 days."],"wt":["","calc","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"S corporation income is allocated per share per day of ownership, including the sale date.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Daily income = 365,000 / 365 = 1,000","90 days x 1,000 = 90,000","40% x 90,000 = 36,000"],"gd":[["How is S corporation income allocated to a shareholder who sells?","Per share per day of ownership."],["What is daily income and the days owned?","365,000 / 365 = 1,000 and 90 days from January 1 to March 31."],["What is Wes's share?","40% x 90,000 = 36,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0013","t":"Which deduction may an S corporation claim in computing its ordinary business income?","mc":{"s":"Which deduction may an S corporation claim in computing its ordinary business income?","d":"","o":["Wages paid to employees","The dividends-received deduction","A net operating loss deduction","The federal income tax on its ordinary income"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §1363(b); Form 1120-S instructions","rat":"An S corporation deducts ordinary and necessary business expenses such as wages.","ex":["An S corporation deducts ordinary and necessary business expenses such as wages.","S corporations cannot claim the corporate dividends-received deduction; dividends pass through.","S corporation losses pass through to shareholders; the entity has no net operating loss deduction.","An S corporation generally pays no federal income tax on its ordinary income, so there is nothing to deduct."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"An S corporation deducts ordinary business expenses like wages, but not the DRD, an NOL or federal income tax.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["What can an S corporation deduct in computing ordinary income?","Ordinary and necessary business expenses."],["Which listed item is such an expense?","Wages; the DRD, NOL deduction and entity-level income tax are not allowed."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0014","t":"Which of these items must an S corporation state separately on Schedule K-1 rather than include in ordinary business income?","mc":{"s":"Which of these items must an S corporation state separately on Schedule K-1 rather than include in ordinary business income?","d":"","o":["Wages paid to employees","Cost of goods sold","Regular depreciation on business equipment","Charitable contributions made by the corporation"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §1366(a)(1)(A); Form 1120-S instructions","rat":"Charitable contributions are separately stated because shareholders' deductions depend on individual limits.","ex":["Wages are ordinary business deductions included in box 1.","Cost of goods sold is part of ordinary business income.","Ordinary depreciation is a business deduction included in box 1; only the expensing election is separately stated.","Charitable contributions are separately stated because shareholders' deductions depend on individual limits."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"An S corporation separately states items like charitable contributions that depend on each shareholder's own limits; wages, COGS and regular depreciation stay in box 1.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Which items are included in ordinary business income?","Ordinary operating items such as wages, COGS and regular depreciation."],["Which listed item is separately stated and why?","Charitable contributions, because each shareholder's deduction depends on individual limits."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0015","t":"Northfield Print, Inc., an S corporation, paid $12,000 of health insurance premiums in 2026 for Grace Tull, who owns 30% of the stock and works full time for the company. How is the payment treated?","mc":{"s":"Northfield Print, Inc., an S corporation, paid $12,000 of health insurance premiums in 2026 for Grace Tull, who owns 30% of the stock and works full time for the company. How is the payment treated?","d":"","o":["The corporation deducts it and Grace excludes it from income as an ordinary employee fringe benefit of the company","The corporation cannot deduct it, because it is a distribution to a shareholder rather than compensation","The corporation deducts it and includes it in Grace's wages; Grace may take the self-employed health insurance deduction","The payment is included in Grace's wages and is subject to FICA tax like other wages"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §1372; IRC §162(l); Notice 2008-1; Pub 15-B","rat":"Premiums for a more-than-2% shareholder-employee are wages for income tax purposes (not FICA), deductible by the corporation, and deductible by the shareholder on Schedule 1.","ex":["Fringe benefit exclusions do not apply to more-than-2% shareholders.","Premiums are deductible compensation-type expenses if included in wages.","Premiums for a more-than-2% shareholder-employee are wages for income tax purposes (not FICA), deductible by the corporation, and deductible by the shareholder on Schedule 1.","Premiums paid under a plan for a more-than-2% shareholder-employee are reported in box 1 wages but are not FICA wages, so the statement that they are subject to FICA like other wages is wrong."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Health premiums for a more-than-2% S shareholder-employee are included in wages but not FICA, and deductible by the corporation and shareholder.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["How are health premiums for a more-than-2% shareholder-employee treated?","As wages for income tax but not FICA, deductible by the corporation."],["What can Grace claim?","The self-employed health insurance deduction on Schedule 1."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0016","t":"A preparer classified the following 2026 items of Delta Ridge, Inc., an S corporation, on Form 1120-S and Schedule K. Which classification is incorrect?","mc":{"s":"A preparer classified the following 2026 items of Delta Ridge, Inc., an S corporation, on Form 1120-S and Schedule K. Which classification is incorrect?","d":"","o":["The $5,000 cash charitable contribution should be separately stated, not deducted in box 1","The $15,000 long-term capital gain should not be separately stated","The $3,000 of interest income on savings should be included in box 1","The $40,000 of regular depreciation should be separately stated"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §1366(a)(1); Form 1120-S instructions","rat":"Charitable contributions are separately stated items and should not reduce ordinary business income.","ex":["Charitable contributions are separately stated items and should not reduce ordinary business income.","Capital gains are separately stated on Schedule K; the draft is right.","Portfolio interest is separately stated; the draft is right.","Regular MACRS depreciation is an ordinary business deduction, so the draft is right."],"wt":["","trap","trap","trap"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Charitable contributions are separately stated, while regular depreciation stays in ordinary income and capital gains and interest are separately stated.","tb":[["Item","Amount","Draft classification"],["Cash charitable contribution","$5,000","Deducted in box 1 (ordinary income)"],["Long-term capital gain","$15,000","Separately stated (box 8a)"],["Interest income on savings","$3,000","Separately stated (box 4)"],["Regular MACRS depreciation","$40,000","Deducted in box 1 (ordinary income)"]],"from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Which items are separately stated?","Charitable contributions, capital gains and interest income."],["Which draft entry treats a separately stated item as ordinary?","The 5,000 charitable contribution deducted in box 1."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0017","t":"An automated check on Sedge Bay, Inc.'s 2026 Form 1120-S flags that shareholder box 1 amounts do not equal Schedule K line 1, which shows $200,000. Shareholders and ownership all year: Amy 50%, Ben 30%, Cal 20%. The Schedule K-1s show box 1 of $100,000 for Amy, $60,000 for Ben and $30,000 for Cal. Which correction resolves the discrepancy?","mc":{"s":"An automated check on Sedge Bay, Inc.'s 2026 Form 1120-S flags that shareholder box 1 amounts do not equal Schedule K line 1, which shows $200,000. Shareholders and ownership all year: Amy 50%, Ben 30%, Cal 20%. The Schedule K-1s show box 1 of $100,000 for Amy, $60,000 for Ben and $30,000 for Cal. Which correction resolves the discrepancy?","d":"","o":["Change Amy's box 1 amount to $110,000","Change Ben's box 1 amount to $70,000","Reduce Schedule K line 1 to $190,000","Change Cal's box 1 amount to $40,000"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §1366(a); IRC §1377(a); Form 1120-S instructions (Schedule K-1)","rat":"Cal owned 20% all year, and 20% of $200,000 is $40,000.","ex":["Amy's 50% share is $100,000, which is correct.","Ben's 30% share is $60,000, which is correct.","The Schedule K total is the source amount; the error is in the shareholder allocation.","Cal owned 20% all year, and 20% of $200,000 is $40,000. Amy and Ben are correct."],"wt":["trap","trap","trap",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"S corporation ordinary income is allocated to shareholders by ownership percentage, so each K-1 must sum to Schedule K.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Amy 50% x 200,000 = 100,000","Ben 30% x 200,000 = 60,000","Cal 20% x 200,000 = 40,000; K-1 shows 30,000, a 10,000 shortfall"],"gd":[["What is each shareholder's share of 200,000?","Amy 50% = 100,000, Ben 30% = 60,000 and Cal 20% = 40,000."],["Which K-1 does not match?","Cal's shows 30,000, a 10,000 shortfall, so it is changed to 40,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0018","t":"For 2026, Oakhaven Supply, Inc., an S corporation, began the year with an accumulated adjustments account (AAA) of $40,000. It had ordinary business income of $120,000, a long-term capital gain of $10,000, tax-exempt interest of $5,000, nondeductible expenses of $3,000, charitable contributions of $4,000 and cash distributions of $50,000. What is AAA at year-end?","mc":{"s":"For 2026, Oakhaven Supply, Inc., an S corporation, began the year with an accumulated adjustments account (AAA) of $40,000. It had ordinary business income of $120,000, a long-term capital gain of $10,000, tax-exempt interest of $5,000, nondeductible expenses of $3,000, charitable contributions of $4,000 and cash distributions of $50,000. What is AAA at year-end?","d":"","o":["$113,000","$118,000","$120,000","$163,000"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §1368(e)(1); Form 1120-S Schedule M-2","rat":"AAA increases by ordinary income and capital gain and decreases by nondeductible expenses, charitable gifts and distributions: $113,000.","ex":["AAA increases by ordinary income and capital gain and decreases by nondeductible expenses, charitable gifts and distributions: $113,000. Tax-exempt income affects a separate account.","Adds tax-exempt interest to AAA. That income adjusts the other adjustments account instead.","Omits the reductions for nondeductible expenses and charitable contributions.","Omits the reduction for the $50,000 of distributions."],"wt":["","concept","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"AAA increases with ordinary income and capital gains and decreases with nondeductible expenses, charity and distributions; tax-exempt income is excluded.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Beginning 40,000","Add ordinary income 120,000 and capital gain 10,000","Subtract nondeductible expenses 3,000, charitable contributions 4,000, distributions 50,000","40,000 + 130,000 - 57,000 = 113,000"],"gd":[["What increases AAA?","Ordinary income 120,000 and capital gain 10,000."],["What decreases it?","Nondeductible expenses 3,000, charity 4,000 and distributions 50,000."],["What is the balance?","40,000 + 130,000 - 57,000 = 113,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0019","t":"Riverbend Grocers, Inc., an S corporation, had an AAA of $20,000 at the beginning of 2026, and an ordinary business loss of $30,000 for the year. During the year it made a $25,000 cash distribution. The corporation has no accumulated earnings and profits. How much of the distribution is treated as coming from AAA?","mc":{"s":"Riverbend Grocers, Inc., an S corporation, had an AAA of $20,000 at the beginning of 2026, and an ordinary business loss of $30,000 for the year. During the year it made a $25,000 cash distribution. The corporation has no accumulated earnings and profits. How much of the distribution is treated as coming from AAA?","d":"","o":["$0","$5,000","$20,000","$25,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §1368(e)(1)(C); IRC §1368(b)","rat":"For distribution purposes AAA is figured without the net negative adjustment for the year, so the full beginning AAA of $20,000 is available.","ex":["Reduces AAA by the loss first. Distributions ignore the net negative adjustment for the year.","Uses the excess of the distribution over AAA, not the AAA amount.","For distribution purposes AAA is figured without the net negative adjustment for the year, so the full beginning AAA of $20,000 is available. The remaining $5,000 is applied against stock basis.","Treats the entire distribution as from AAA, which is only $20,000."],"wt":["concept","calc","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"For distributions, AAA is computed without regard to the net negative adjustment for the year.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["AAA available for distributions (ignoring net negative adjustment) = 20,000","Amount treated as from AAA = min(25,000, 20,000) = 20,000"],"gd":[["How is AAA figured for distributions?","Without the net negative adjustment for the year."],["How much is treated as from AAA?","The lesser of 25,000 and 20,000, so 20,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0020","t":"Highland Bakery, Inc. is an S corporation with AAA of $30,000 and accumulated earnings and profits of $40,000 from earlier C corporation years. It made one $50,000 distribution to Tess Wu, its only shareholder, whose stock basis is $100,000. How much is a taxable dividend?","mc":{"s":"Highland Bakery, Inc. is an S corporation with AAA of $30,000 and accumulated earnings and profits of $40,000 from earlier C corporation years. It made one $50,000 distribution to Tess Wu, its only shareholder, whose stock basis is $100,000. How much is a taxable dividend?","d":"","o":["$0","$20,000","$40,000","$50,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §1368(c); IRC §1368(e)","rat":"The first $30,000 comes from AAA and reduces basis.","ex":["Treats the whole distribution as return of basis. With accumulated earnings and profits, amounts beyond AAA are dividends.","The first $30,000 comes from AAA and reduces basis. The next $20,000 comes from accumulated earnings and profits and is a dividend.","Treats all accumulated earnings and profits as distributed; only the $20,000 that exceeds AAA is drawn.","Treats the entire distribution as a dividend and ignores AAA."],"wt":["concept","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"With accumulated E&P, S distributions come first from AAA, then from E&P as dividends.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["From AAA: 30,000 (tax-free, reduces basis)","From accumulated E&P: 50,000 - 30,000 = 20,000 (dividend)"],"gd":[["How much of the distribution comes from AAA?","30,000, tax-free and reducing basis."],["What comes next and how is it taxed?","50,000 - 30,000 = 20,000 from accumulated E&P as a dividend."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0021","t":"Which item does not adjust an S corporation's accumulated adjustments account?","mc":{"s":"Which item does not adjust an S corporation's accumulated adjustments account?","d":"","o":["Ordinary business income","Separately stated capital gains","Tax-exempt interest income","Nondeductible expenses that are not capital"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §1368(e)(1)(A); Form 1120-S Schedule M-2","rat":"Tax-exempt income and related expenses are tracked in the other adjustments account, not AAA.","ex":["Ordinary business income increases AAA, so it does adjust the account.","Separately stated capital gains increase AAA, so they do adjust the account.","Tax-exempt income and related expenses are tracked in the other adjustments account, not AAA.","Nondeductible expenses decrease AAA, so they do adjust the account."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Tax-exempt income adjusts the other adjustments account, not AAA.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Which items adjust AAA?","Ordinary income, capital gains and nondeductible expenses."],["Which item is tracked in a separate account?","Tax-exempt interest, in the other adjustments account."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0022","t":"In determining whether a distribution during 2026 comes from AAA, how is the balance at year-end treated?","mc":{"s":"In determining whether a distribution during 2026 comes from AAA, how is the balance at year-end treated?","d":"","o":["It is computed without regard to a net negative adjustment for the year, so current-year losses do not reduce it","It is reduced by all current-year losses and expenses before any distribution is tested against it","It is computed with regard to the distributions only, ignoring all income items for the year","It is set at $0 whenever the corporation has accumulated earnings and profits from C years"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §1368(e)(1)(C)","rat":"Under the special rule, AAA for distributions is determined without regard to a net negative adjustment (losses and expenses exceeding income).","ex":["Under the special rule, AAA for distributions is determined without regard to a net negative adjustment (losses and expenses exceeding income).","That would count the net negative adjustment; the special rule ignores it.","Income items increase AAA before distributions are tested.","AAA is measured regardless of accumulated earnings and profits."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"AAA for distributions is computed without regard to the year's net negative adjustment, so current losses do not reduce it.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Does the year's net loss reduce AAA for testing distributions?","No."],["What rule applies?","AAA for distributions is determined without regard to a net negative adjustment."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0023","t":"Draft Schedule M-2 for Bluff Point, Inc., an S corporation, is shown. The corporation had no other items. Which correction is needed?","mc":{"s":"Draft Schedule M-2 for Bluff Point, Inc., an S corporation, is shown. The corporation had no other items. Which correction is needed?","d":"","o":["Remove the $10,000 capital gain from AAA; ending AAA is $112,000","Do not reduce AAA for the $3,000 of nondeductible expenses; ending AAA is $125,000","Do not reduce AAA for distributions; ending AAA is $172,000","Remove the $5,000 of tax-exempt interest from AAA; ending AAA is $117,000"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §1368(e)(1); Form 1120-S Schedule M-2","rat":"Tax-exempt income does not adjust AAA.","ex":["Separately stated capital gains do increase AAA, so the draft is right on that item.","Nondeductible expenses reduce AAA, so the draft is right on that item.","Distributions reduce AAA, so the draft is right on that item.","Tax-exempt income does not adjust AAA. Correct ending AAA is $40,000 + $120,000 + $10,000 - $3,000 - $50,000 = $117,000."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Tax-exempt income does not adjust AAA, though ordinary income, gains, nondeductibles and distributions do.","tb":[["Draft Schedule M-2 (AAA)","Amount"],["Beginning balance","$40,000"],["Ordinary business income","$120,000"],["Net long-term capital gain","$10,000"],["Tax-exempt interest income","$5,000"],["Nondeductible expenses","($3,000)"],["Distributions","($50,000)"],["Ending balance","$122,000"]],"from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Correct = 40,000 + 120,000 + 10,000 - 3,000 - 50,000 = 117,000","Draft overstated by the 5,000 tax-exempt interest"],"gd":[["Does tax-exempt income adjust AAA?","No, it goes to the other adjustments account."],["What is the correct ending balance?","40,000 + 120,000 + 10,000 - 3,000 - 50,000 = 117,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0024","t":"Pine Terrace, Inc., an S corporation with no accumulated earnings and profits, distributed $45,000 in cash to Uma Ruiz, its only shareholder, whose stock basis was $30,000. The corporation's AAA before the distribution was $60,000. What gain does Uma recognize?","mc":{"s":"Pine Terrace, Inc., an S corporation with no accumulated earnings and profits, distributed $45,000 in cash to Uma Ruiz, its only shareholder, whose stock basis was $30,000. The corporation's AAA before the distribution was $60,000. What gain does Uma recognize?","d":"","o":["$0","$15,000","$30,000","$45,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §1368(b)","rat":"With no accumulated earnings and profits, distributions are tax-free to the extent of stock basis, then gain.","ex":["Treats the entire distribution as tax-free because AAA is large enough; the shareholder's basis is still the limit.","With no accumulated earnings and profits, distributions are tax-free to the extent of stock basis, then gain. The gain is $45,000 - $30,000 = $15,000 of capital gain; AAA does not override the basis limit.","Uses the basis as the gain; basis is what is tax-free.","Treats the whole distribution as gain, ignoring the $30,000 basis."],"wt":["concept","","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Without E&P, S distributions are tax-free up to stock basis and gain above it, regardless of AAA.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Tax-free to the extent of basis 30,000","Excess 45,000 - 30,000 = 15,000 capital gain"],"gd":[["Without accumulated E&P, how far is a distribution tax-free?","To the extent of stock basis, 30,000."],["What is the gain?","45,000 - 30,000 = 15,000 capital gain."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0025","t":"Two S corporations each have AAA of $60,000 and each distribute $80,000 in cash in 2026 to a sole shareholder whose stock basis is $200,000. Ash Grove, Inc. has accumulated earnings and profits of $25,000 from prior C corporation years. Birch Grove, Inc. has none. Which statement compares the shareholders' results correctly?","mc":{"s":"Two S corporations each have AAA of $60,000 and each distribute $80,000 in cash in 2026 to a sole shareholder whose stock basis is $200,000. Ash Grove, Inc. has accumulated earnings and profits of $25,000 from prior C corporation years. Birch Grove, Inc. has none. Which statement compares the shareholders' results correctly?","d":"","o":["Both shareholders have a $20,000 dividend","Ash Grove's shareholder has a $25,000 dividend","Ash Grove's shareholder has a $20,000 dividend; Birch Grove's shareholder has no taxable amount","Neither shareholder has a taxable amount, because basis exceeds the distribution"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §1368(b); IRC §1368(c)","rat":"For Ash Grove, $60,000 comes from AAA and $20,000 from accumulated earnings and profits (a dividend).","ex":["Birch Grove has no earnings and profits, so nothing is a dividend.","Only the $20,000 that exceeds AAA is drawn from earnings and profits.","For Ash Grove, $60,000 comes from AAA and $20,000 from accumulated earnings and profits (a dividend). For Birch Grove, the entire $80,000 reduces basis.","In a corporation with earnings and profits, the amount beyond AAA is a dividend regardless of basis."],"wt":["concept","calc","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Beyond AAA, an S corporation with accumulated E&P pays dividends from E&P; without E&P the excess reduces basis.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Ash Grove: AAA 60,000 tax-free; E&P 20,000 dividend","Birch Grove: 80,000 reduces basis; no dividend"],"gd":[["What happens for the corporation with accumulated E&P?","60,000 comes from AAA and the next 20,000 from E&P as a dividend."],["What happens for the corporation with none?","The entire 80,000 reduces basis with no dividend."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0026","t":"Cross Timber, Inc., an S corporation, has accumulated earnings and profits and wants its distributions to be treated as coming first from accumulated earnings and profits, before AAA. What must it do?","mc":{"s":"Cross Timber, Inc., an S corporation, has accumulated earnings and profits and wants its distributions to be treated as coming first from accumulated earnings and profits, before AAA. What must it do?","d":"","o":["Make an election with the consent of all affected shareholders","Nothing; the ordering is at the corporation's discretion each year","Obtain consent from holders of more than half of the shares","Convert to a C corporation, since S corporations cannot make this election"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §1368(e)(3)","rat":"A corporation may elect, with the consent of all affected shareholders, to distribute accumulated earnings and profits first.","ex":["A corporation may elect, with the consent of all affected shareholders, to distribute accumulated earnings and profits first.","The default order is AAA first, and a change requires an election.","All affected shareholders must consent, not just a majority.","S corporations may make the election while remaining S corporations."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"An S corporation may elect to distribute accumulated E&P first with consent of all affected shareholders.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Can an S corporation with E&P change the distribution order?","Yes, by election."],["Who must consent?","All affected shareholders."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-1001","t":"Elena Voss expects her business to earn $300,000 in 2026 before any payment to her. If it is a partnership in which she is the only general partner, she pays self-employment tax on the whole amount. If it is an S corporation, she takes $120,000 of reasonable salary and the rest as a distribution. Assume the 2026 Social Security wage base is $184,500, Social Security tax is 12.4% (combined employer and employee), Medicare tax is 2.9% with no wage cap, and self-employment income is 92.35% of net earnings. Ignore income tax, Additional Medicare Tax and any deduction for the tax. By how much do the combined Social Security and Medicare taxes under the partnership exceed those under the S corporation?","mc":{"s":"Elena Voss expects her business to earn $300,000 in 2026 before any payment to her. If it is a partnership in which she is the only general partner, she pays self-employment tax on the whole amount. If it is an S corporation, she takes $120,000 of reasonable salary and the rest as a distribution. Assume the 2026 Social Security wage base is $184,500, Social Security tax is 12.4% (combined employer and employee), Medicare tax is 2.9% with no wage cap, and self-employment income is 92.35% of net earnings. Ignore income tax, Additional Medicare Tax and any deduction for the tax. By how much do the combined Social Security and Medicare taxes under the partnership exceed those under the S corporation?","d":"","o":["$12,552","$13,218","$21,732","$24,029"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §1401(a); IRC §1401(b); IRC §1402(a)(12); IRC §3111; IRC §3121(a)(1)","rat":"Partnership: net earnings $300,000 x 92.35% = $277,050; Social Security 12.4% x $184,500 = $22,878; Medicare 2.9% x $277,050 = $8,034; total $30,912.","ex":["Partnership: net earnings $300,000 x 92.35% = $277,050; Social Security 12.4% x $184,500 = $22,878; Medicare 2.9% x $277,050 = $8,034; total $30,912. S corporation: 15.3% x $120,000 = $18,360. Difference $12,552.","Applies the taxes to the full $300,000 and skips the 92.35% factor for self-employment income.","Compares against only the employee half of the S corporation's payroll tax ($9,180). The corporation's half is also a real payroll tax on the salary.","Applies 15.3% to all self-employment income and ignores the Social Security wage base of $184,500."],"wt":["","calc","calc","limit"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A general partner pays self-employment tax on all net earnings (with the 92.35% factor and the Social Security cap), while an S corporation owner pays payroll tax only on reasonable salary.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Partnership: 300,000 x 0.9235 = 277,050; SS 12.4% x min(277,050, 184,500) = 22,878; Medicare 2.9% x 277,050 = 8,034.45; total 30,912.45","S corporation: 15.3% x 120,000 = 18,360","30,912.45 - 18,360 = 12,552.45"],"gd":[["What are the partnership Social Security and Medicare taxes?","Net earnings 300,000 x 0.9235 = 277,050; Social Security 12.4% x 184,500 = 22,878; Medicare 2.9% x 277,050 = 8,034; total 30,912."],["What is the S corporation tax on the 120,000 salary?","15.3% x 120,000 = 18,360."],["What is the difference?","30,912 - 18,360 = 12,552."]]}}],"7":[{"i":"REG-V-C-0027","t":"Nadia Osei bought her S corporation stock for $50,000 at the start of 2026. For 2026 her share of the corporation's items was ordinary income of $30,000, a long-term capital gain of $6,000, tax-exempt interest of $4,000 and nondeductible expenses of $2,000. She received cash distributions of $20,000. What is her stock basis at year-end?","mc":{"s":"Nadia Osei bought her S corporation stock for $50,000 at the start of 2026. For 2026 her share of the corporation's items was ordinary income of $30,000, a long-term capital gain of $6,000, tax-exempt interest of $4,000 and nondeductible expenses of $2,000. She received cash distributions of $20,000. What is her stock basis at year-end?","d":"","o":["$64,000","$68,000","$70,000","$88,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §1367(a); Form 7203","rat":"Basis increases for income items (ordinary, capital gain and tax-exempt) and decreases for distributions and nondeductible expenses: $68,000.","ex":["Leaves out the $4,000 of tax-exempt interest, which increases stock basis.","Basis increases for income items (ordinary, capital gain and tax-exempt) and decreases for distributions and nondeductible expenses: $68,000.","Does not reduce basis for the $2,000 of nondeductible expenses.","Does not reduce basis for the $20,000 of cash distributions."],"wt":["calc","","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"S stock basis increases for income including tax-exempt income and decreases for distributions and nondeductible expenses.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Beginning 50,000","Increases: 30,000 + 6,000 + 4,000 = 40,000","Decreases: distributions 20,000 + nondeductible expenses 2,000 = 22,000","50,000 + 40,000 - 22,000 = 68,000"],"gd":[["Which items increase basis?","Ordinary income 30,000, capital gain 6,000 and tax-exempt interest 4,000."],["Which decrease it?","Distributions 20,000 and nondeductible expenses 2,000."],["What is year-end basis?","50,000 + 40,000 - 22,000 = 68,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0028","t":"Leon Baptiste owns 40% of an S corporation. His stock basis is $25,000 at the start of 2026 and he has no loans to the corporation. His share of the 2026 ordinary business loss is $40,000, and the corporation had no other items. What loss may Leon deduct in 2026, ignoring at-risk and passive activity rules?","mc":{"s":"Leon Baptiste owns 40% of an S corporation. His stock basis is $25,000 at the start of 2026 and he has no loans to the corporation. His share of the 2026 ordinary business loss is $40,000, and the corporation had no other items. What loss may Leon deduct in 2026, ignoring at-risk and passive activity rules?","d":"","o":["$0","$15,000","$25,000","$40,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §1366(d)(1); Form 7203","rat":"A shareholder's deductible loss cannot exceed his stock and debt basis.","ex":["Disallows the entire loss; a loss is allowed up to basis.","Uses the amount of the suspended loss instead of the amount allowed.","A shareholder's deductible loss cannot exceed his stock and debt basis. With $25,000 of basis, $25,000 is deductible and $15,000 is suspended.","Deducts the full loss, ignoring the basis limit."],"wt":["concept","calc","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"An S shareholder's deductible loss is limited to stock plus debt basis, with the excess suspended.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Loss 40,000 versus basis 25,000","Deductible = 25,000; suspended = 15,000 carried forward"],"gd":[["What limits a shareholder's loss deduction?","Stock plus debt basis."],["What is deductible and what is suspended?","25,000 deductible and 40,000 - 25,000 = 15,000 suspended."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0029","t":"Priya Nair owns 50% of an S corporation. Her stock basis is $20,000 and she has lent the corporation $30,000 directly (debt basis $30,000). Her share of the 2026 ordinary loss is $45,000. What is her debt basis after the loss is deducted?","mc":{"s":"Priya Nair owns 50% of an S corporation. Her stock basis is $20,000 and she has lent the corporation $30,000 directly (debt basis $30,000). Her share of the 2026 ordinary loss is $45,000. What is her debt basis after the loss is deducted?","d":"","o":["$0","$5,000","$25,000","$30,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §1367(b)(2); Form 7203","rat":"The loss first reduces stock basis to $0 ($20,000), and the remaining $25,000 reduces debt basis to $5,000.","ex":["Assumes the entire $45,000 loss reduces debt basis. Stock basis absorbs the first $20,000.","The loss first reduces stock basis to $0 ($20,000), and the remaining $25,000 reduces debt basis to $5,000.","This is the $25,000 of loss charged to debt basis, not the debt basis that remains.","Leaves debt basis unchanged; the loss exceeds stock basis, so debt basis is reduced."],"wt":["calc","","calc","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"S losses reduce stock basis first, then debt basis for the excess.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Loss 45,000 - stock basis 20,000 = 25,000 charged to debt basis","Debt basis = 30,000 - 25,000 = 5,000"],"gd":[["Which basis is reduced first?","Stock basis, 20,000."],["How much reaches debt basis?","45,000 - 20,000 = 25,000."],["What is the debt basis afterward?","30,000 - 25,000 = 5,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0030","t":"Ivan Petrov made a $50,000 direct loan to his S corporation. Prior losses reduced his basis in the note to $30,000. In 2026 the corporation repaid $25,000 of the note (evidenced by a written note). What gain does Ivan recognize?","mc":{"s":"Ivan Petrov made a $50,000 direct loan to his S corporation. Prior losses reduced his basis in the note to $30,000. In 2026 the corporation repaid $25,000 of the note (evidenced by a written note). What gain does Ivan recognize?","d":"","o":["$0","$5,000","$10,000","$25,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §1367(b)(2); Reg. §1.1367-2(c)","rat":"The repayment is 50% of the note.","ex":["Treats the repayment as a tax-free return of capital; repayment of a note whose basis was reduced produces gain.","Subtracts the repayment from the remaining basis, which measures leftover basis rather than gain.","The repayment is 50% of the note. The basis in the repaid portion is 50% x $30,000 = $15,000, so the gain is $25,000 - $15,000 = $10,000.","Recognizes the whole repayment as gain, ignoring the basis in the repaid part."],"wt":["concept","calc","","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Repayment of a note whose basis was reduced produces gain in proportion to the repaid share of the note.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Portion repaid = 25,000 / 50,000 = 50%","Basis of repaid portion = 30,000 x 50% = 15,000","Gain = 25,000 - 15,000 = 10,000"],"gd":[["What portion of the note was repaid?","25,000 / 50,000 = 50%."],["What basis is in the repaid part?","30,000 x 50% = 15,000."],["What is the gain?","25,000 - 15,000 = 10,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0031","t":"Mona Reyes has a note from her S corporation with an original basis of $40,000, which prior losses reduced to $10,000. In 2026 her share of the corporation's net income is $50,000. Ignoring distributions, how much of the $50,000 increases her stock basis?","mc":{"s":"Mona Reyes has a note from her S corporation with an original basis of $40,000, which prior losses reduced to $10,000. In 2026 her share of the corporation's net income is $50,000. Ignoring distributions, how much of the $50,000 increases her stock basis?","d":"","o":["$0","$20,000","$30,000","$50,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §1367(b)(2)(B)","rat":"Net income first restores debt basis to its original $40,000 (a $30,000 restoration).","ex":["Assumes the income only restores debt basis.","Net income first restores debt basis to its original $40,000 (a $30,000 restoration). Only the remaining $20,000 increases stock basis.","This is the amount that restores debt basis, not the amount that increases stock basis.","Applies all the income to stock basis; debt basis must be restored first."],"wt":["concept","","calc","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Net S income first restores debt basis to its original amount before increasing stock basis.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Restoration of debt basis = 40,000 - 10,000 = 30,000","Remaining income = 50,000 - 30,000 = 20,000 increases stock basis"],"gd":[["How is net income applied after prior losses reduced debt basis?","First to restore debt basis to its original amount."],["How much is restored?","40,000 - 10,000 = 30,000."],["What increases stock basis?","50,000 - 30,000 = 20,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0032","t":"Which item decreases a shareholder's basis in S corporation stock?","mc":{"s":"Which item decreases a shareholder's basis in S corporation stock?","d":"","o":["The shareholder's share of tax-exempt interest","The shareholder's share of a separately stated capital gain","A cash contribution the shareholder makes to the corporation","The shareholder's share of nondeductible expenses, such as fines"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §1367(a)(2)","rat":"Nondeductible expenses that are not capital reduce stock basis.","ex":["Tax-exempt income increases stock basis.","Separately stated income items increase stock basis.","Capital contributions increase stock basis.","Nondeductible expenses that are not capital reduce stock basis."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Nondeductible expenses reduce S stock basis, while tax-exempt income, capital gains and contributions increase it.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Which items increase basis?","Income items including tax-exempt interest and capital gains, and contributions of capital."],["Which listed item decreases it?","The share of nondeductible expenses such as fines."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0033","t":"Sam Ortega owns 50% of an S corporation for the entire year. In 2026 the corporation donated land (adjusted basis $20,000, fair market value $60,000) to a qualified charity. By how much does the donation reduce Sam's stock basis?","mc":{"s":"Sam Ortega owns 50% of an S corporation for the entire year. In 2026 the corporation donated land (adjusted basis $20,000, fair market value $60,000) to a qualified charity. By how much does the donation reduce Sam's stock basis?","d":"","o":["$0","$10,000","$20,000","$30,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §1367(a)(2); IRC §1366(a)","rat":"Basis reduces by the shareholder's pro rata share of the property's adjusted basis: 50% x $20,000 = $10,000.","ex":["Assumes no reduction because the property was donated instead of paid in cash.","Basis reduces by the shareholder's pro rata share of the property's adjusted basis: 50% x $20,000 = $10,000.","Uses the whole adjusted basis; Sam is responsible only for his 50% share.","Uses his share of fair market value, which is what passes through for the charitable deduction, not the basis reduction."],"wt":["concept","","calc","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A property charitable donation by an S corporation reduces shareholder basis by the shareholder's share of the property's adjusted basis.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Share of adjusted basis = 50% x 20,000 = 10,000"],"gd":[["How much of the donated property's basis does the shareholder's stock basis absorb?","His pro rata share of the property's adjusted basis."],["What is the reduction?","50% x 20,000 = 10,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0034","t":"Two unrelated 50% shareholders of an S corporation each have stock basis of $10,000. Xavier lent the corporation $40,000 directly. Yara guaranteed a $40,000 bank loan to the corporation but paid nothing on it. Each shareholder's share of the 2026 loss is $60,000. How much more loss may Xavier deduct than Yara?","mc":{"s":"Two unrelated 50% shareholders of an S corporation each have stock basis of $10,000. Xavier lent the corporation $40,000 directly. Yara guaranteed a $40,000 bank loan to the corporation but paid nothing on it. Each shareholder's share of the 2026 loss is $60,000. How much more loss may Xavier deduct than Yara?","d":"","o":["$0","$10,000","$40,000","$50,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §1366(d); IRC §1367(b)(2); Reg. §1.1366-2(a)(2)","rat":"Xavier's basis is $10,000 stock plus $40,000 debt = $50,000, so he deducts $50,000.","ex":["Assumes a guarantee gives the same basis as a direct loan.","Compares Yara's disallowed loss with the debt basis; it does not give the difference in deductions.","Xavier's basis is $10,000 stock plus $40,000 debt = $50,000, so he deducts $50,000. Yara's guarantee creates no basis, so she deducts only $10,000. The difference is $40,000.","Adds the stock basis to the difference; the difference in deductible loss is only the extra debt basis."],"wt":["concept","calc","","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A shareholder loan to the corporation creates debt basis, but a mere guarantee does not.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Xavier: 10,000 + 40,000 = 50,000 deductible","Yara: 10,000 deductible (guarantee gives no basis)","Difference = 40,000"],"gd":[["What is Xavier's basis?","Stock 10,000 plus his direct loan 40,000 = 50,000."],["Does Yara's guarantee create basis?","No, so she has only 10,000."],["What is the difference in deductible loss?","50,000 - 10,000 = 40,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0035","t":"Draft basis worksheet for Petra Lund's S corporation stock for 2026 is shown. Petra's share of tax-exempt interest was $4,000 and it was omitted; all other items are correct. Which correction is needed?","mc":{"s":"Draft basis worksheet for Petra Lund's S corporation stock for 2026 is shown. Petra's share of tax-exempt interest was $4,000 and it was omitted; all other items are correct. Which correction is needed?","d":"","o":["Remove the $6,000 capital gain, since separately stated gains do not increase basis; ending basis is $58,000","Do not reduce basis for the $2,000 of nondeductible expenses; ending basis is $66,000","Add the $4,000 of tax-exempt interest; ending basis is $68,000","Do not reduce basis for distributions, because AAA covers them; ending basis is $84,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §1367(a); Form 7203","rat":"Tax-exempt income increases stock basis.","ex":["Separately stated capital gains do increase basis, so the draft is right on this item.","Nondeductible expenses reduce stock basis, so the draft is right on this item.","Tax-exempt income increases stock basis. Correct ending basis is $50,000 + $30,000 + $6,000 + $4,000 - $20,000 - $2,000 = $68,000.","Distributions reduce stock basis even when AAA covers them, so the draft is right on this item."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Tax-exempt interest increases S stock basis, so it must be added; the other worksheet items are correct.","tb":[["Draft stock basis worksheet","Amount"],["Beginning basis","$50,000"],["Ordinary business income","$30,000"],["Long-term capital gain","$6,000"],["Distributions","($20,000)"],["Nondeductible expenses","($2,000)"],["Ending basis","$64,000"]],"from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Correct = 50,000 + 30,000 + 6,000 + 4,000 - 20,000 - 2,000 = 68,000"],"gd":[["Does tax-exempt income affect stock basis?","Yes, it increases it."],["What is the correct ending basis?","50,000 + 30,000 + 6,000 + 4,000 - 20,000 - 2,000 = 68,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0036","t":"An S corporation shareholder has a $60,000 loss allocated to her. In which order are the limitations applied in determining how much loss she may deduct?","mc":{"s":"An S corporation shareholder has a $60,000 loss allocated to her. In which order are the limitations applied in determining how much loss she may deduct?","d":"","o":["Basis first, then at-risk, then the passive activity limits","At-risk first, then basis, then the passive activity limits","Passive activity first, then at-risk, then basis","Basis and passive activity together, and at-risk not at all"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §1366(d); IRC §465; IRC §469","rat":"The loss must first be within stock and debt basis, then at-risk, then passive loss limitations.","ex":["The loss must first be within stock and debt basis, then at-risk, then passive loss limitations.","Basis is the first limitation.","The passive rules are applied last, not first.","The at-risk limits apply to S corporation shareholders too."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"S loss limits apply in order: basis, then at-risk, then passive activity.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["What limit applies first to an S shareholder's loss?","Basis."],["What follows?","The at-risk rules and then the passive activity limits."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0037","t":"Wanda Kroll contributed equipment with an adjusted basis of $12,000 and a fair market value of $30,000 to her S corporation as a capital contribution without receiving additional stock. By how much does the contribution increase her stock basis?","mc":{"s":"Wanda Kroll contributed equipment with an adjusted basis of $12,000 and a fair market value of $30,000 to her S corporation as a capital contribution without receiving additional stock. By how much does the contribution increase her stock basis?","d":"","o":["$0","$12,000","$18,000","$30,000"],"a":2,"cite":"Form 7203 instructions (capital contributions); Reg §1.118-1","rat":"A contribution of property increases stock basis by the shareholder's adjusted basis in the property.","ex":["Assumes no increase because no stock was issued; contributions to capital increase basis.","A contribution of property increases stock basis by the shareholder's adjusted basis in the property.","Uses the built-in gain, which is not a basis amount.","Uses fair market value; the shareholder adds her adjusted basis."],"wt":["concept","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A capital contribution of property increases stock basis by the shareholder's adjusted basis in the property.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Stock basis increases by adjusted basis of contributed property = 12,000"],"gd":[["How does a property contribution affect stock basis?","It increases it by the shareholder's adjusted basis in the property."],["What is the increase?","12,000, not the value."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0043","t":"Gus Ramos begins 2026 with a stock basis of $5,000 and $12,000 of ordinary losses suspended from an earlier year. For 2026 his share of the S corporation's ordinary income is $30,000, and he receives a $10,000 distribution. He has no debt basis. What is his stock basis at year-end after the suspended loss is deducted?","mc":{"s":"Gus Ramos begins 2026 with a stock basis of $5,000 and $12,000 of ordinary losses suspended from an earlier year. For 2026 his share of the S corporation's ordinary income is $30,000, and he receives a $10,000 distribution. He has no debt basis. What is his stock basis at year-end after the suspended loss is deducted?","d":"","o":["$13,000","$23,000","$25,000","$35,000"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §1366(d)(2); IRC §1367(a)","rat":"Basis is increased by income ($35,000), then reduced by the distribution ($25,000), then by the previously suspended loss, which is now allowed: $13,000.","ex":["Basis is increased by income ($35,000), then reduced by the distribution ($25,000), then by the previously suspended loss, which is now allowed: $13,000.","Does not reduce basis for the distribution.","Does not reduce basis for the previously suspended loss that is now deductible.","Ignores both the distribution and the suspended loss."],"wt":["","calc","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"S basis increases by income, decreases by distributions, then by previously suspended losses now allowed.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["5,000 + 30,000 income = 35,000","Less distribution 10,000 = 25,000","Less suspended loss allowed 12,000 = 13,000"],"gd":[["What is basis after income?","5,000 + 30,000 = 35,000."],["What is basis after the distribution?","35,000 - 10,000 = 25,000."],["What does the suspended loss do?","Reduces basis by 12,000 to 13,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-1005","t":"Dax Moreno is the sole shareholder of a corporation that has a $100,000 operating loss in 2026, its first year, and no income. His stock basis is $60,000, he has made no loans to the corporation, and he materially participates and is at risk to the extent of his basis. Which statement compares the result if the corporation is an S corporation with the result if it is a C corporation?","mc":{"s":"Dax Moreno is the sole shareholder of a corporation that has a $100,000 operating loss in 2026, its first year, and no income. His stock basis is $60,000, he has made no loans to the corporation, and he materially participates and is at risk to the extent of his basis. Which statement compares the result if the corporation is an S corporation with the result if it is a C corporation?","d":"","o":["If S, Dax deducts $60,000 in 2026 and carries $40,000 forward at the shareholder level; if C, he deducts nothing and the corporation has a $100,000 net operating loss carryforward","If S, Dax deducts $100,000 in 2026 because he is at risk; if C, the loss offsets his other income only after he sells the stock","If S, Dax deducts nothing because losses are limited to distributions; if C, he deducts $60,000, the amount of his basis","If S, Dax deducts $60,000 and the other $40,000 is lost permanently; if C, the corporation carries the $100,000 back two years"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §1366(a),(d)(1),(d)(2); IRC §172(b)(1)","rat":"A shareholder's S corporation loss deduction is limited to stock and debt basis ($60,000); the excess is suspended.","ex":["A shareholder's S corporation loss deduction is limited to stock and debt basis ($60,000); the excess is suspended. A C corporation's loss stays in the corporation as a net operating loss carryforward.","The basis limit caps the S deduction at $60,000. A C corporation's loss never passes to the shareholder, even on a sale.","Reverses the entities. Basis limits S corporation losses, and a C corporation shareholder deducts none of the corporation's loss.","The disallowed S loss is carried forward indefinitely, not lost, and a 2026 C corporation net operating loss is carried forward only."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"S corporation losses pass through but are deductible only up to shareholder basis (excess is suspended); a C corporation's loss stays inside as a net operating loss carryforward.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["S: deduct min(100,000, basis 60,000) = 60,000; suspended 40,000 carried forward","C: no pass-through; corporate NOL 100,000 carried forward"],"gd":[["What limits Dax's loss if the corporation is an S corporation?","Stock and debt basis, so min(100,000, 60,000) = 60,000, with 40,000 suspended at his level."],["What happens if it is a C corporation?","No pass-through; the corporation carries a 100,000 NOL forward."]]}}],"8":[{"i":"REG-V-C-0038","t":"Harmon Steel, Inc. converted from a C corporation to an S corporation on January 1, 2023. In March 2026 it sold equipment whose gain was built in at the conversion date. The net recognized built-in gain is $200,000 and it does not exceed taxable income or the corporation's net unrealized built-in gain. What corporate-level tax applies to the sale?","mc":{"s":"Harmon Steel, Inc. converted from a C corporation to an S corporation on January 1, 2023. In March 2026 it sold equipment whose gain was built in at the conversion date. The net recognized built-in gain is $200,000 and it does not exceed taxable income or the corporation's net unrealized built-in gain. What corporate-level tax applies to the sale?","d":"","o":["$0","$42,000","$70,000","$200,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §1374(a); IRC §1374(b)(1)","rat":"The built-in gains tax is imposed at the top corporate rate (21%) on net recognized built-in gain during the five-year recognition period: $42,000.","ex":["Assumes S corporations never pay federal tax; the tax applies during the recognition period.","The built-in gains tax is imposed at the top corporate rate (21%) on net recognized built-in gain during the five-year recognition period: $42,000.","Uses the 35% rate that applied before 2018.","Uses the gain as the tax."],"wt":["concept","","limit","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Built-in gains tax applies to net recognized built-in gain during the five-year recognition period at the 21% corporate rate.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Sale is within five years after conversion","Tax = 21% x 200,000 = 42,000"],"gd":[["Is the sale within the built-in gains recognition period?","Yes, five years after the 2023 conversion."],["What is the rate and the tax?","21% x 200,000 = 42,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0039","t":"Willow Springs, Inc. converted from a C corporation to an S corporation effective January 1, 2020. In 2026 it sold an asset that was appreciated when it converted. Which statement is correct?","mc":{"s":"Willow Springs, Inc. converted from a C corporation to an S corporation effective January 1, 2020. In 2026 it sold an asset that was appreciated when it converted. Which statement is correct?","d":"","o":["The built-in gains tax applies, because it applies to any sale of an appreciated asset","The built-in gains tax applies at 21%, because the recognition period is ten years","The built-in gains tax applies only if the corporation elects it","No built-in gains tax applies, because the five-year recognition period ended in 2024"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §1374(d)(7)","rat":"The recognition period is five years; it ended after December 31, 2024, so the 2026 sale is outside the period.","ex":["The tax applies only during the recognition period.","The recognition period is five years, not ten.","The tax is automatic when the conditions are met; no election creates it.","The recognition period is five years; it ended after December 31, 2024, so the 2026 sale is outside the period."],"wt":["concept","limit","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Built-in gains tax applies only to sales within the five-year recognition period after conversion.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["How long is the built-in gains recognition period?","Five years."],["Has it ended for a January 1, 2020 conversion?","Yes, after 2024, so the 2026 sale is outside it."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0040","t":"Cliffside Rentals, Inc., an S corporation with accumulated earnings and profits, had 2026 gross receipts of $300,000, of which $120,000 was passive investment income (rents and royalties). Deductions allocable to the passive income were $20,000. Taxable income exceeds net passive income. What is the tax on excess net passive income at the 21% corporate rate?","mc":{"s":"Cliffside Rentals, Inc., an S corporation with accumulated earnings and profits, had 2026 gross receipts of $300,000, of which $120,000 was passive investment income (rents and royalties). Deductions allocable to the passive income were $20,000. Taxable income exceeds net passive income. What is the tax on excess net passive income at the 21% corporate rate?","d":"","o":["$7,875","$9,450","$21,000","$25,200"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §1375(a); IRC §1375(b)","rat":"Excess passive income is $120,000 - 25% x $300,000 = $45,000.","ex":["Excess passive income is $120,000 - 25% x $300,000 = $45,000. Excess net passive income is $100,000 x $45,000 / $120,000 = $37,500. Tax at 21% is $7,875.","Applies 21% to excess gross passive income without reducing for allocable deductions.","Applies 21% to the net passive income, ignoring the 25% threshold.","Applies 21% to all passive income."],"wt":["","calc","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Tax on excess net passive income is 21% of net passive income times excess gross passive income over gross passive income.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Net passive income = 120,000 - 20,000 = 100,000","Excess passive investment income = 120,000 - 75,000 = 45,000","Excess net passive income = 100,000 x 45,000 / 120,000 = 37,500","Tax = 21% x 37,500 = 7,875"],"gd":[["What is net passive income?","120,000 - 20,000 = 100,000."],["What is excess passive investment income over 25% of receipts?","120,000 - 75,000 = 45,000."],["What is excess net passive income and the tax?","100,000 x 45,000 / 120,000 = 37,500, and 21% x 37,500 = 7,875."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0041","t":"Sagebrush Design, Inc. is wholly owned by Lee Vargas, who works full time for the company. It paid Lee $20,000 of salary and $180,000 of distributions in 2026, when comparable services are worth $120,000. What is the likely tax risk?","mc":{"s":"Sagebrush Design, Inc. is wholly owned by Lee Vargas, who works full time for the company. It paid Lee $20,000 of salary and $180,000 of distributions in 2026, when comparable services are worth $120,000. What is the likely tax risk?","d":"","o":["The IRS may deny the corporation's S election because distributions were unequal","The distributions are taxed as dividends, because the corporation paid too little salary","The IRS may recharacterize part of the distributions as wages, subjecting them to employment taxes","There is no risk, because distributions from an S corporation are not subject to employment tax"],"a":3,"cite":"Rev. Rul. 74-44; IRC §3121(a)","rat":"An S corporation must pay a shareholder-employee reasonable compensation; part of distributions may be reclassified as wages.","ex":["Unequal treatment of one shareholder's pay does not create a second class of stock.","Reclassification would be to wages, not dividends, for a corporation without earnings and profits.","An S corporation must pay a shareholder-employee reasonable compensation; part of distributions may be reclassified as wages.","Distributions are not, but the IRS may recharacterize amounts that are really compensation."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"An S corporation must pay shareholder-employees reasonable compensation; the IRS may recharacterize distributions as wages subject to employment tax.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["What must an S corporation pay a working shareholder?","Reasonable compensation."],["What can the IRS do with a 20,000 salary against 120,000 of comparable pay?","Recharacterize part of the distributions as wages subject to employment taxes."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-0042","t":"Ken Alvarez expects $100,000 of pre-tax business profit in 2026 and will keep it in a corporation only until paid out to him in full. If the business is a C corporation, it pays 21% corporate tax and Ken pays 23.8% on the remaining amount as a dividend. If it is an S corporation, Ken pays 32% on the profit. Ignoring the qualified business income deduction and payroll taxes, how much more total tax does the C corporation structure produce?","mc":{"s":"Ken Alvarez expects $100,000 of pre-tax business profit in 2026 and will keep it in a corporation only until paid out to him in full. If the business is a C corporation, it pays 21% corporate tax and Ken pays 23.8% on the remaining amount as a dividend. If it is an S corporation, Ken pays 32% on the profit. Ignoring the qualified business income deduction and payroll taxes, how much more total tax does the C corporation structure produce?","d":"","o":["$7,802","$18,802","$21,000","$39,802"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §11(b); IRC §1(h); IRC §1411; IRC §1366","rat":"C corporation: $21,000 + 23.8% x $79,000 ($18,802) = $39,802.","ex":["C corporation: $21,000 + 23.8% x $79,000 ($18,802) = $39,802. S corporation: $32,000. The C corporation structure costs $7,802 more.","Uses only the shareholder-level dividend tax, ignoring the S corporation tax it replaces.","Uses only the corporate-level tax.","This is the total C corporation tax, not the difference from the S corporation tax."],"wt":["","calc","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A C corporation's profit is taxed at the corporate level and again as a dividend, so it costs more than pass-through S taxation.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["C corporation: corporate tax 21% x 100,000 = 21,000","Dividend = 79,000; shareholder tax 23.8% x 79,000 = 18,802","Total C = 39,802; S corporation = 32% x 100,000 = 32,000","Difference = 7,802"],"gd":[["What is the C corporation total?","Corporate tax 21% x 100,000 = 21,000, then dividend 79,000 taxed at 23.8% = 18,802, total 39,802."],["What is the S corporation tax?","32% x 100,000 = 32,000."],["What is the difference?","39,802 - 32,000 = 7,802."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-1002","t":"Two corporations each distribute land (adjusted basis $30,000, fair market value $100,000) to their sole shareholders in 2026. Ridge Corp. is an S corporation; its shareholder's stock basis is $50,000, its accumulated adjustments account is $50,000, and it has no earnings and profits. Cove Corp. is a C corporation with ample earnings and profits. Which statement compares the results correctly?","mc":{"s":"Two corporations each distribute land (adjusted basis $30,000, fair market value $100,000) to their sole shareholders in 2026. Ridge Corp. is an S corporation; its shareholder's stock basis is $50,000, its accumulated adjustments account is $50,000, and it has no earnings and profits. Cove Corp. is a C corporation with ample earnings and profits. Which statement compares the results correctly?","d":"","o":["Both corporations recognize $70,000 of gain; Ridge's shareholder reports that gain, has $20,000 of stock basis left and no dividend, and Cove's shareholder reports a $100,000 dividend","Only Cove recognizes gain; Ridge's shareholder has $0 of stock basis left and reports $50,000 of gain on the distribution in excess of basis","Both corporations recognize $70,000 of gain; Ridge's shareholder reports that gain and also a $100,000 dividend, and Cove's shareholder reports a $100,000 dividend","Neither corporation recognizes gain, because a distribution is not a sale, and both shareholders take a $30,000 basis in the land and report no income"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §311(b); IRC §1366(a); IRC §1367(a)(1); IRC §1368(b); IRC §301(b),(d)","rat":"Section 311(b) makes both corporations recognize the $70,000 built-in gain.","ex":["Section 311(b) makes both corporations recognize the $70,000 built-in gain. In the S corporation it passes through and raises basis to $120,000 before the $100,000 distribution reduces it to $20,000. The C corporation's shareholder has a dividend.","Treats the S corporation as if it did not recognize gain on distributing appreciated property. Section 311(b) applies to S corporations too, and the gain raises basis first.","Taxes the S shareholder twice. With no earnings and profits, the S distribution is tax-free to the extent of basis after the gain increases basis.","A corporation recognizes gain on distributing appreciated property, and the shareholder's basis in the land is its $100,000 fair market value."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Section 311(b) makes any corporation recognize gain on distributing appreciated property; an S corporation passes that gain through (raising basis first), while a C corporation's shareholder is taxed on a dividend.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Corporate gain: 100,000 - 30,000 = 70,000 in both entities","S shareholder basis: 50,000 + 70,000 = 120,000; distribution 100,000 tax-free; basis 20,000","C shareholder: 100,000 dividend (ample earnings and profits)"],"gd":[["What gain do both corporations recognize on distributing appreciated land?","100,000 - 30,000 = 70,000."],["How does it affect the S shareholder's basis?","50,000 + 70,000 = 120,000, then the 100,000 distribution is tax-free, leaving 20,000."],["How is the C shareholder treated?","A 100,000 dividend, given ample E&P."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-C-1007","t":"Wolf Creek, Inc. converted from a C corporation to an S corporation on January 1, 2024. In 2026 it sold equipment for a $150,000 gain that is entirely built in from the conversion date, and it had $50,000 of other ordinary business income that is not built in. Its net unrealized built-in gain at conversion was $400,000, and its taxable income for 2026 computed as if it were a C corporation would be $500,000. Assume a 21% rate. Wolf Creek has one shareholder. By how much do these items increase the shareholder's stock basis for 2026?","mc":{"s":"Wolf Creek, Inc. converted from a C corporation to an S corporation on January 1, 2024. In 2026 it sold equipment for a $150,000 gain that is entirely built in from the conversion date, and it had $50,000 of other ordinary business income that is not built in. Its net unrealized built-in gain at conversion was $400,000, and its taxable income for 2026 computed as if it were a C corporation would be $500,000. Assume a 21% rate. Wolf Creek has one shareholder. By how much do these items increase the shareholder's stock basis for 2026?","d":"","o":["$118,500","$158,000","$168,500","$200,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §1374(b)(1),(d)(7); IRC §1366(f)(2); IRC §1367(a)(1)","rat":"Built-in gains tax = 21% x lesser of the $150,000 recognized built-in gain, the $400,000 net unrealized built-in gain and the $500,000 taxable income = $31,500.","ex":["Leaves out the $50,000 of ordinary income, which also passes through and increases basis.","Applies the 21% tax to all income. The tax applies only to the recognized built-in gain.","Built-in gains tax = 21% x lesser of the $150,000 recognized built-in gain, the $400,000 net unrealized built-in gain and the $500,000 taxable income = $31,500. The tax reduces the gain that passes through ($118,500). Add $50,000 of ordinary income: $168,500.","Ignores the corporate-level built-in gains tax, which reduces the pass-through gain."],"wt":["calc","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Built-in gains tax is paid by the S corporation on recognized built-in gain in the five-year recognition period, and that tax reduces the gain passed through to shareholders.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Tax: 21% x min(150,000, 400,000, 500,000) = 31,500","Pass-through gain: 150,000 - 31,500 = 118,500","Basis increase: 118,500 + 50,000 = 168,500"],"gd":[["What is the built-in gains tax base?","The lesser of 150,000 recognized, 400,000 net unrealized and 500,000 taxable income, so 150,000."],["What is the tax and the pass-through gain?","21% x 150,000 = 31,500, so 150,000 - 31,500 = 118,500."],["What is the basis increase?","118,500 + 50,000 = 168,500."]]}}],"9":[{"i":"REG-V-D-0001","t":"Juniper Crest Partners, a calendar-year partnership, had 2026 revenue of $800,000, cost of goods sold of $300,000, wages of $150,000, guaranteed payments to partners for services of $60,000 and regular depreciation of $40,000. It also had an $8,000 charitable contribution, a $12,000 long-term capital gain, a $20,000 immediate expensing deduction and $5,000 of interest income. What is the partnership's ordinary business income?","mc":{"s":"Juniper Crest Partners, a calendar-year partnership, had 2026 revenue of $800,000, cost of goods sold of $300,000, wages of $150,000, guaranteed payments to partners for services of $60,000 and regular depreciation of $40,000. It also had an $8,000 charitable contribution, a $12,000 long-term capital gain, a $20,000 immediate expensing deduction and $5,000 of interest income. What is the partnership's ordinary business income?","d":"","o":["$222,000","$250,000","$267,000","$310,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §703(a); IRC §702(a); IRC §707(c); Form 1065 instructions","rat":"Revenue less COGS, wages, guaranteed payments and depreciation is $250,000.","ex":["Deducts the charitable contribution and the expensing deduction in ordinary income; both are separately stated.","Revenue less COGS, wages, guaranteed payments and depreciation is $250,000. The charitable gift, capital gain, expensing deduction and interest are separately stated.","Includes the capital gain and interest income, which are separately stated to partners.","Does not deduct the guaranteed payments; they are deductible in computing ordinary income."],"wt":["concept","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Partnership ordinary income deducts wages and guaranteed payments; charity, capital gains, section 179 and interest are separately stated.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["800,000 - 300,000 - 150,000 - 60,000 - 40,000 = 250,000","Separately stated: charity 8,000, capital gain 12,000, expensing 20,000, interest 5,000"],"gd":[["Which items reduce ordinary business income?","COGS, wages, guaranteed payments and regular depreciation."],["What is the result?","800,000 - 300,000 - 150,000 - 60,000 - 40,000 = 250,000."],["Which items are separately stated?","Charity, capital gain, expensing and interest."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0002","t":"Rita Sanchez owns 25% of a partnership. In 2026 the partnership paid her a $60,000 guaranteed payment for services. The partnership's ordinary business income, after deducting that payment, was $250,000, and it distributed $40,000 in cash to Rita. What ordinary income does Rita report from the partnership for 2026?","mc":{"s":"Rita Sanchez owns 25% of a partnership. In 2026 the partnership paid her a $60,000 guaranteed payment for services. The partnership's ordinary business income, after deducting that payment, was $250,000, and it distributed $40,000 in cash to Rita. What ordinary income does Rita report from the partnership for 2026?","d":"","o":["$62,500","$102,500","$122,500","$162,500"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §707(c); IRC §702(a); IRC §731(a)","rat":"Rita reports the guaranteed payment ($60,000) plus her 25% share of ordinary income ($62,500): $122,500.","ex":["Omits the guaranteed payment, which is ordinary income to the recipient partner.","Adds the cash distribution instead of the guaranteed payment; distributions are not income.","Rita reports the guaranteed payment ($60,000) plus her 25% share of ordinary income ($62,500): $122,500. The cash distribution is not income.","Adds the distribution too; distributions reduce basis and are not income unless they exceed it."],"wt":["calc","calc","","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A partner reports guaranteed payments plus her share of ordinary income; cash distributions are not income.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Guaranteed payment 60,000","Distributive share 25% x 250,000 = 62,500","Total 122,500; the distribution is not income"],"gd":[["How is the guaranteed payment reported?","As ordinary income of 60,000."],["What is her distributive share?","25% x 250,000 = 62,500."],["What is the total and is the cash distribution income?","60,000 + 62,500 = 122,500, and the distribution is not income."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0003","t":"Which item is not deducted in computing a partnership's ordinary business income and is instead separately stated?","mc":{"s":"Which item is not deducted in computing a partnership's ordinary business income and is instead separately stated?","d":"","o":["Charitable contributions made by the partnership","Rent paid for the partnership's office","Wages paid to employees who are not partners","Guaranteed payments to partners for services"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §703(a)(2); IRC §702(a)","rat":"Charitable contributions are separately stated because each partner's deduction depends on individual limits.","ex":["Charitable contributions are separately stated because each partner's deduction depends on individual limits.","Rent is an ordinary business deduction taken in computing ordinary income.","Wages are deducted in computing ordinary business income.","Guaranteed payments for services are deductible in computing ordinary income, subject to capitalization rules."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Charitable contributions are separately stated by a partnership, while rent, wages and guaranteed payments are deducted in ordinary income.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Which items are deducted in ordinary business income?","Rent, wages to non-partners and guaranteed payments."],["Which item is separately stated?","Charitable contributions, because partners have different limits."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0004","t":"Roy Hale is a partner in a service partnership. The partnership pays him $50,000 a year for his services, regardless of the partnership's income. How is the payment treated?","mc":{"s":"Roy Hale is a partner in a service partnership. The partnership pays him $50,000 a year for his services, regardless of the partnership's income. How is the payment treated?","d":"","o":["A distribution of partnership capital that reduces his basis and is not income","A part of his distributive share, taxed only to the extent the partnership has income","Capital gain to Roy, because he is an owner","Ordinary income to Roy, and a deduction (or capitalized cost) to the partnership"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §707(c)","rat":"A guaranteed payment for services is treated as a payment to a non-partner for income and deduction purposes.","ex":["Guaranteed payments are not distributions; they are income to the recipient.","Because the payment is guaranteed regardless of income, it is not part of the distributive share.","Payments for services are ordinary income.","A guaranteed payment for services is treated as a payment to a non-partner for income and deduction purposes."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A guaranteed payment for services is ordinary income to the partner and a deduction or capitalized cost to the partnership.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["How does a guaranteed payment for services compare with a payment to an outsider?","It is treated like one for income and deduction purposes."],["What does it mean for Roy and the partnership?","Ordinary income to Roy and a deduction or capitalized cost to the partnership."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0005","t":"Which item must a partnership separately state on the partners' Schedules K-1?","mc":{"s":"Which item must a partnership separately state on the partners' Schedules K-1?","d":"","o":["Wages paid to employees","Advertising expense","Long-term capital gain on the sale of investment securities","Repairs and maintenance"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §702(a); Form 1065 instructions","rat":"Capital gains and losses are separately stated because their treatment depends on each partner's situation.","ex":["Wages are ordinary business deductions and are not separately stated.","Advertising is an ordinary business deduction included in ordinary income.","Capital gains and losses are separately stated because their treatment depends on each partner's situation.","Repairs are included in ordinary business income."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Capital gains and losses are separately stated on partner K-1s, unlike wages, advertising and repairs.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Which items are separately stated on K-1?","Items whose treatment depends on each partner, such as capital gains and losses."],["Which listed item is that?","Long-term capital gain on investment securities; wages, advertising and repairs are ordinary."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0006","t":"Bridge Street Partners is a calendar-year partnership. When is its 2026 Form 1065 due, and what is the extended due date with a timely extension?","mc":{"s":"Bridge Street Partners is a calendar-year partnership. When is its 2026 Form 1065 due, and what is the extended due date with a timely extension?","d":"","o":["March 15, 2027, extended to September 15, 2027","April 15, 2027, extended to October 15, 2027","March 15, 2027, extended to July 15, 2027","April 15, 2027, extended to September 15, 2027"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §6072(b); Form 1065 instructions; Form 7004","rat":"A calendar-year partnership files by the 15th day of the third month, with an automatic six-month extension.","ex":["A calendar-year partnership files by the 15th day of the third month, with an automatic six-month extension.","Those are the C corporation dates.","The automatic extension is six months, not four.","The original due date is the 15th day of the third month, not the fourth."],"wt":["","limit","limit","limit"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A calendar-year partnership's Form 1065 is due March 15 with an automatic six-month extension to September 15.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["When is a calendar-year partnership return due?","The 15th day of the third month, March 15, 2027."],["What is the automatic extension?","Six months, to September 15, 2027."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0007","t":"Ela Fischer is a limited partner who performs services for the partnership. For 2026 her distributive share of ordinary income was $30,000, and she also received a $20,000 guaranteed payment for services. What amount is included in her net earnings from self-employment, before the 92.35% adjustment?","mc":{"s":"Ela Fischer is a limited partner who performs services for the partnership. For 2026 her distributive share of ordinary income was $30,000, and she also received a $20,000 guaranteed payment for services. What amount is included in her net earnings from self-employment, before the 92.35% adjustment?","d":"","o":["$0","$20,000","$30,000","$50,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §1402(a)(13); Form 1065 Schedule K-1 instructions","rat":"A limited partner's distributive share is excluded from self-employment income, but guaranteed payments for services are included: $20,000.","ex":["Excludes both; guaranteed payments for services are still subject to self-employment tax.","A limited partner's distributive share is excluded from self-employment income, but guaranteed payments for services are included: $20,000.","Includes the limited partner's distributive share, which is excluded.","Includes both amounts; only the guaranteed payment is subject to self-employment tax."],"wt":["concept","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A limited partner's distributive share is excluded from SE earnings, but guaranteed payments for services are included.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Is a limited partner's distributive share self-employment income?","No, it is excluded."],["What about guaranteed payments for services?","Included, so 20,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0008","t":"Draft 2026 Form 1065 for Corbel Studio Partners is shown. The $60,000 paid to partner Nia Roberts for her services, regardless of income, was recorded as a distribution and not deducted. Which correction is required?","mc":{"s":"Draft 2026 Form 1065 for Corbel Studio Partners is shown. The $60,000 paid to partner Nia Roberts for her services, regardless of income, was recorded as a distribution and not deducted. Which correction is required?","d":"","o":["Leave ordinary income at $310,000 and report the $60,000 only as a separately stated item","Treat the $60,000 as a distribution reducing Nia's capital, with no change to the return","Deduct the $60,000 but omit it from the Schedule K-1, because Nia is taxed on her share of income","Deduct the $60,000 in computing ordinary income (making it $250,000) and report it as a guaranteed payment on Nia's Schedule K-1"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §707(c); Form 1065 instructions","rat":"A guaranteed payment for services is deductible by the partnership and separately reported to the recipient partner.","ex":["The payment reduces ordinary income; it is not a separately stated item that leaves ordinary income unchanged.","It is a guaranteed payment, not a distribution, because it is fixed without regard to income.","The recipient partner must report the guaranteed payment on the K-1 and in her income.","A guaranteed payment for services is deductible by the partnership and separately reported to the recipient partner."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A guaranteed payment for services is deducted in ordinary income and reported on the recipient partner's K-1.","tb":[["Draft Form 1065 (2026)","Amount"],["Revenue","$800,000"],["Cost of goods sold","($300,000)"],["Wages and other deductions","($190,000)"],["Guaranteed payment to Nia Roberts","$0 (recorded as a distribution)"],["Ordinary business income","$310,000"]],"from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Correct ordinary income = 310,000 - 60,000 = 250,000"],"gd":[["How is a payment to a partner for services regardless of income treated?","As a guaranteed payment, deductible by the partnership."],["What is the corrected ordinary income?","310,000 - 60,000 = 250,000."],["Where is it also reported?","On Nia's Schedule K-1 as a guaranteed payment."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0009","t":"An automated diagnostic on the 2026 Form 1065 of Tidewater Partners flags that the ending profit-sharing percentages on the Schedules K-1 total 105%. The partnership agreement allocates profits 50% to Amos, 30% to Bea and 20% to Cole. The K-1s show 50% for Amos, 35% for Bea and 20% for Cole. Which correction resolves the discrepancy?","mc":{"s":"An automated diagnostic on the 2026 Form 1065 of Tidewater Partners flags that the ending profit-sharing percentages on the Schedules K-1 total 105%. The partnership agreement allocates profits 50% to Amos, 30% to Bea and 20% to Cole. The K-1s show 50% for Amos, 35% for Bea and 20% for Cole. Which correction resolves the discrepancy?","d":"","o":["Change Amos's percentage to 45%","Change Cole's percentage to 15%","Change Bea's percentage to 30%","Change the agreement so Bea receives 35% and Amos 45%"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §704(a); Form 1065 Schedule K-1 instructions","rat":"Amos and Cole match the agreement.","ex":["Amos's percentage matches the agreement, so it should not change.","Cole's percentage matches the agreement, so it should not change.","Amos and Cole match the agreement. Bea's 35% should be 30%, which makes the total 100%.","The agreement governs allocations; the K-1 should be corrected to match it."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"K-1 profit percentages must match the partnership agreement and total 100%.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["50% + 30% + 20% = 100% under the agreement","K-1s total 105%; only Bea differs from the agreement"],"gd":[["What do the agreement percentages total?","50% + 30% + 20% = 100%."],["Which K-1 differs from the agreement?","Bea's 35%, so it should be 30%."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0010","t":"Partner Gil Martins owns 25% of a partnership. The partnership has $310,000 of income before any payment to Gil. Under Plan 1 it pays Gil a $60,000 guaranteed payment for services (deductible), and under Plan 2 it distributes $60,000 in cash to him instead. Assuming Gil's basis is sufficient, by how much does his ordinary income under Plan 1 exceed his ordinary income under Plan 2?","mc":{"s":"Partner Gil Martins owns 25% of a partnership. The partnership has $310,000 of income before any payment to Gil. Under Plan 1 it pays Gil a $60,000 guaranteed payment for services (deductible), and under Plan 2 it distributes $60,000 in cash to him instead. Assuming Gil's basis is sufficient, by how much does his ordinary income under Plan 1 exceed his ordinary income under Plan 2?","d":"","o":["$15,000","$45,000","$60,000","$77,500"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §707(c); IRC §702; IRC §731","rat":"Plan 1: $60,000 + 25% x $250,000 = $122,500.","ex":["Uses only Gil's share of the payment, ignoring that the full payment is his income.","Plan 1: $60,000 + 25% x $250,000 = $122,500. Plan 2: 25% x $310,000 = $77,500 (the distribution is not income). The difference is $45,000.","Uses the full payment, ignoring that Plan 1 reduces the income shared by all partners.","Uses Gil's Plan 2 income as the difference."],"wt":["calc","","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Guaranteed payments are the partner's ordinary income and reduce shared income, unlike nontaxable distributions.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Plan 1: 60,000 + 25% x 250,000 = 122,500","Plan 2: 25% x 310,000 = 77,500","Difference = 45,000"],"gd":[["What is Plan 1 income?","60,000 + 25% x 250,000 = 122,500."],["What is Plan 2 income?","25% x 310,000 = 77,500, since the distribution is not income."],["What is the difference?","122,500 - 77,500 = 45,000."]]}}],"10":[{"i":"REG-V-D-0011","t":"Lars Beck owns 30% of a partnership. His basis at the start of 2026 was $60,000. For 2026 his share of ordinary income was $30,000, tax-exempt interest $2,000 and charitable contributions $5,000. He received a $20,000 cash distribution, and his share of partnership liabilities increased by $15,000. What is his basis at year-end?","mc":{"s":"Lars Beck owns 30% of a partnership. His basis at the start of 2026 was $60,000. For 2026 his share of ordinary income was $30,000, tax-exempt interest $2,000 and charitable contributions $5,000. He received a $20,000 cash distribution, and his share of partnership liabilities increased by $15,000. What is his basis at year-end?","d":"","o":["$67,000","$80,000","$82,000","$87,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §705(a); IRC §752(a)","rat":"Basis increases for income (including tax-exempt) and liability increases, and decreases for charitable contributions and distributions: $82,000.","ex":["Omits the $15,000 increase in Lars's share of partnership liabilities, which is treated as a contribution of money.","Omits the $2,000 of tax-exempt interest, which increases basis.","Basis increases for income (including tax-exempt) and liability increases, and decreases for charitable contributions and distributions: $82,000.","Omits the reduction for charitable contributions, which are nondeductible items that reduce basis."],"wt":["calc","calc","","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Partner basis increases with income, tax-exempt income and liability share increases, and decreases with charity and distributions.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Beginning 60,000","Increases: 30,000 + 2,000 + 15,000 = 47,000","Decreases: 5,000 + 20,000 = 25,000","60,000 + 47,000 - 25,000 = 82,000"],"gd":[["Which items increase basis?","Ordinary income 30,000, tax-exempt interest 2,000 and liability increase 15,000."],["Which decrease it?","Charity 5,000 and distribution 20,000."],["What is year-end basis?","60,000 + 47,000 - 25,000 = 82,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0012","t":"Sid, an S corporation shareholder, and Pat, a partner in a partnership, each contribute $20,000 in cash. Each entity then borrows $30,000 from a bank without any owner guarantee, and the loan increases Pat's share of partnership liabilities by $30,000. Each owner's share of the 2026 loss is $35,000. Ignoring at-risk and passive activity rules, how much more loss can Pat deduct than Sid?","mc":{"s":"Sid, an S corporation shareholder, and Pat, a partner in a partnership, each contribute $20,000 in cash. Each entity then borrows $30,000 from a bank without any owner guarantee, and the loan increases Pat's share of partnership liabilities by $30,000. Each owner's share of the 2026 loss is $35,000. Ignoring at-risk and passive activity rules, how much more loss can Pat deduct than Sid?","d":"","o":["$0","$15,000","$30,000","$35,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §704(d); IRC §752(a); IRC §1366(d)","rat":"Sid's stock basis is $20,000 and the entity's borrowing gives him no basis, so he deducts $20,000.","ex":["Assumes entity-level debt gives the same basis in both structures.","Sid's stock basis is $20,000 and the entity's borrowing gives him no basis, so he deducts $20,000. Pat's basis is $20,000 + $30,000 = $50,000, so he deducts the full $35,000. The difference is $15,000.","Uses the amount of the loan as the difference. Pat's extra deduction is limited to the part of his $35,000 loss that exceeds Sid's $20,000 basis.","Treats Sid as deducting nothing."],"wt":["concept","","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A partner's basis includes his share of partnership debt, but an S shareholder's basis excludes corporate debt to outsiders.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Sid: deductible = stock basis 20,000","Pat: basis 20,000 + 30,000 = 50,000, deducts 35,000","Difference = 15,000"],"gd":[["What does Sid deduct given entity borrowing gives an S shareholder no basis?","His stock basis, 20,000."],["What is Pat's basis and deduction?","20,000 + 30,000 = 50,000, so the full 35,000."],["What is the difference?","35,000 - 20,000 = 15,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0013","t":"Vera Lopez has a $30,000 basis in her partnership interest. During 2026 her share of partnership liabilities decreased by $12,000, and she received a $25,000 cash distribution. She has no other basis adjustments. What gain does she recognize?","mc":{"s":"Vera Lopez has a $30,000 basis in her partnership interest. During 2026 her share of partnership liabilities decreased by $12,000, and she received a $25,000 cash distribution. She has no other basis adjustments. What gain does she recognize?","d":"","o":["$0","$7,000","$12,000","$37,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §752(b); IRC §731(a)(1)","rat":"A decrease in a partner's share of liabilities is treated as a cash distribution.","ex":["Ignores the liability decrease. The $25,000 cash alone is less than her $30,000 basis.","A decrease in a partner's share of liabilities is treated as a cash distribution. Total distributions of $37,000 exceed her $30,000 basis, so she recognizes $7,000 of gain.","Uses only the liability decrease as the gain.","Treats all deemed and actual distributions as gain without subtracting basis."],"wt":["concept","","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A decrease in a partner's share of liabilities is a deemed cash distribution, taxable to the extent above basis.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Deemed distribution 12,000 + cash 25,000 = 37,000","Excess over basis = 37,000 - 30,000 = 7,000 gain"],"gd":[["How is a decrease in a partner's share of liabilities treated?","As a cash distribution."],["What are the total deemed distributions and the excess over basis?","12,000 + 25,000 = 37,000, and 37,000 - 30,000 = 7,000 gain."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0014","t":"Partner Uma's share of partnership liabilities increased in 2026 because the partnership borrowed from a bank on a recourse basis. How is the increase treated?","mc":{"s":"Partner Uma's share of partnership liabilities increased in 2026 because the partnership borrowed from a bank on a recourse basis. How is the increase treated?","d":"","o":["As a contribution of money by Uma that increases her basis in the partnership interest","As income to Uma in the year of the increase","As a distribution of money that reduces her basis","As having no effect on basis until the partnership repays the loan"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §752(a); IRC §705(a)","rat":"An increase in a partner's share of liabilities is treated as a contribution of money and increases outside basis.","ex":["An increase in a partner's share of liabilities is treated as a contribution of money and increases outside basis.","Borrowing by the partnership is not income to the partners.","A decrease in liabilities, not an increase, is treated as a distribution.","The increase in liabilities affects basis immediately."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"An increase in a partner's share of liabilities is treated as a contribution of money increasing outside basis.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["How is an increase in a partner's share of liabilities treated?","As a contribution of money."],["What is the effect on basis?","It increases outside basis."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0015","t":"Ana Torres contributed $40,000 in cash and equipment with an adjusted basis of $25,000 and a fair market value of $60,000 to a new partnership in exchange for an interest. The partnership has no liabilities. What is Ana's basis in her partnership interest?","mc":{"s":"Ana Torres contributed $40,000 in cash and equipment with an adjusted basis of $25,000 and a fair market value of $60,000 to a new partnership in exchange for an interest. The partnership has no liabilities. What is Ana's basis in her partnership interest?","d":"","o":["$40,000","$65,000","$100,000","$125,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §722","rat":"Basis equals the cash contributed plus the adjusted basis of contributed property: $65,000.","ex":["Counts the cash only and ignores the basis of the contributed equipment.","Basis equals the cash contributed plus the adjusted basis of contributed property: $65,000.","Uses the fair market value of the equipment; a partner takes the property's adjusted basis.","Adds both the basis and the fair market value of the equipment."],"wt":["calc","","concept","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A partner's basis equals cash contributed plus adjusted basis of contributed property.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Cash 40,000 + adjusted basis of equipment 25,000 = 65,000"],"gd":[["What does a partner's basis include for contributed property?","Cash plus the adjusted basis of property."],["What is the total?","40,000 + 25,000 = 65,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0016","t":"Dan Whitaker has a basis of $70,000 in his partnership interest. In a current distribution he receives $10,000 in cash and equipment with an adjusted basis to the partnership of $25,000 and a fair market value of $40,000. What is his basis in the partnership interest after the distribution?","mc":{"s":"Dan Whitaker has a basis of $70,000 in his partnership interest. In a current distribution he receives $10,000 in cash and equipment with an adjusted basis to the partnership of $25,000 and a fair market value of $40,000. What is his basis in the partnership interest after the distribution?","d":"","o":["$20,000","$35,000","$45,000","$60,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §733; IRC §732(a)","rat":"A current distribution reduces basis by the cash and the partnership's adjusted basis in the property: $70,000 - $10,000 - $25,000 = $35,000.","ex":["Reduces basis by fair market value, though the reduction uses the property's adjusted basis.","A current distribution reduces basis by the cash and the partnership's adjusted basis in the property: $70,000 - $10,000 - $25,000 = $35,000.","Reduces basis only by the property and ignores the $10,000 of cash.","Reduces basis only by the cash and ignores the equipment."],"wt":["concept","","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A current distribution reduces basis by cash and the partnership's adjusted basis in distributed property.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Cash 10,000 + basis of equipment 25,000 = 35,000 reduction","70,000 - 35,000 = 35,000"],"gd":[["What reduces basis in a current distribution?","Cash and the partnership's adjusted basis in property."],["What is the reduction and the result?","10,000 + 25,000 = 35,000, so 70,000 - 35,000 = 35,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0017","t":"Zed Okafor is a limited partner with a 40% profits interest. In 2026 the partnership borrowed $100,000 on a nonrecourse basis to buy equipment. There is no minimum gain and no contributed property, so the excess nonrecourse liability is shared according to profits interests. By how much does the loan increase Zed's basis?","mc":{"s":"Zed Okafor is a limited partner with a 40% profits interest. In 2026 the partnership borrowed $100,000 on a nonrecourse basis to buy equipment. There is no minimum gain and no contributed property, so the excess nonrecourse liability is shared according to profits interests. By how much does the loan increase Zed's basis?","d":"","o":["$0","$40,000","$60,000","$100,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §752(a); Reg. §1.752-3","rat":"Nonrecourse liabilities are allocated among partners, here by profit share, and Zed's share increases basis: 40% x $100,000 = $40,000.","ex":["Assumes nonrecourse debt never adds to basis; a partner's share of partnership liabilities does.","Nonrecourse liabilities are allocated among partners, here by profit share, and Zed's share increases basis: 40% x $100,000 = $40,000.","Uses the other partners' share of the liability.","Includes the entire liability, though Zed is allocated only 40%."],"wt":["concept","","concept","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A partner's basis includes his share of nonrecourse liabilities allocated by profit interests.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Share of nonrecourse liability = 40% x 100,000 = 40,000","Basis increases by 40,000"],"gd":[["How are nonrecourse liabilities shared when there is no minimum gain or contributed property?","According to profits interests."],["What is Zed's share and basis increase?","40% x 100,000 = 40,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0018","t":"Draft basis worksheet for Kade Ibarra's partnership interest for 2026 is shown. Kade also contributed services worth $20,000 for his interest. Which correction is required?","mc":{"s":"Draft basis worksheet for Kade Ibarra's partnership interest for 2026 is shown. Kade also contributed services worth $20,000 for his interest. Which correction is required?","d":"","o":["Remove the $10,000 cash contribution; ending basis is $53,000","Do not reduce basis for the $12,000 distribution; ending basis is $75,000","Remove the $15,000 share of ordinary income; ending basis is $48,000","Remove the $20,000 value of services; ending basis is $43,000"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §705(a); IRC §722","rat":"Services are not property and do not create basis.","ex":["Cash contributions increase basis, so the draft is right on that item.","Distributions reduce basis, so the draft is right on that item.","A partner's share of income increases basis, so the draft is right on that item.","Services are not property and do not create basis. Correct ending basis is $30,000 + $10,000 + $15,000 - $12,000 = $43,000."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Services do not create partnership basis; only cash and property contributions, income and distributions adjust it.","tb":[["Draft basis worksheet","Amount"],["Beginning basis","$30,000"],["Cash contribution","$10,000"],["Value of services contributed","$20,000"],["Share of ordinary income","$15,000"],["Cash distribution","($12,000)"],["Ending basis","$63,000"]],"from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Correct = 30,000 + 10,000 + 15,000 - 12,000 = 43,000"],"gd":[["Do contributed services create basis?","No, they are not property."],["What is the correct ending basis?","30,000 + 10,000 + 15,000 - 12,000 = 43,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0019","t":"Ines Varga started 2026 with a $40,000 basis in her partnership interest. Her Schedule K-1 shows ordinary income of $30,000, long-term capital gain of $10,000, tax-exempt interest of $5,000, charitable contributions of $2,000 and a $60,000 cash distribution. Her draft return reports a $20,000 gain on the distribution. Which statement is correct?","mc":{"s":"Ines Varga started 2026 with a $40,000 basis in her partnership interest. Her Schedule K-1 shows ordinary income of $30,000, long-term capital gain of $10,000, tax-exempt interest of $5,000, charitable contributions of $2,000 and a $60,000 cash distribution. Her draft return reports a $20,000 gain on the distribution. Which statement is correct?","d":"","o":["The $20,000 gain is correct, because distributions reduce beginning basis before income is added","No gain is recognized and ending basis is $40,000, because income does not increase basis until distributed","No gain is recognized, because basis is increased for income before the distribution is applied; ending basis is $23,000","A $20,000 gain is recognized and ending basis is $83,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §705(a); IRC §731(a)","rat":"Basis is first increased by income items and reduced by charitable contributions ($83,000), then reduced by the distribution ($60,000), leaving $23,000.","ex":["Distributions are applied after the year's income and gains.","Income increases basis when allocated, whether or not distributed.","Basis is first increased by income items and reduced by charitable contributions ($83,000), then reduced by the distribution ($60,000), leaving $23,000.","Basis cannot stay at $83,000 after a $60,000 distribution, and no gain arises."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Basis is increased for income and decreased for charity before applying distributions, so gain arises only if distributions exceed basis.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["40,000 + 30,000 + 10,000 + 5,000 - 2,000 = 83,000","83,000 - 60,000 = 23,000; no gain because distribution < basis"],"gd":[["In what order is basis adjusted?","Increases for income and decreases for charity first, then distributions."],["What is basis before the distribution?","40,000 + 30,000 + 10,000 + 5,000 - 2,000 = 83,000."],["What is the result after 60,000?","83,000 - 60,000 = 23,000, with no gain."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0020","t":"Ori Blanco contributed land with an adjusted basis of $30,000 and a fair market value of $100,000, subject to a $50,000 mortgage, to a partnership. The partnership assumed the mortgage, and Ori's share of partnership liabilities after the contribution is $25,000. Assume the mortgage was incurred more than two years before the contribution, so the disguised-sale rules do not apply. What is Ori's basis in the partnership interest?","mc":{"s":"Ori Blanco contributed land with an adjusted basis of $30,000 and a fair market value of $100,000, subject to a $50,000 mortgage, to a partnership. The partnership assumed the mortgage, and Ori's share of partnership liabilities after the contribution is $25,000. Assume the mortgage was incurred more than two years before the contribution, so the disguised-sale rules do not apply. What is Ori's basis in the partnership interest?","d":"","o":["$5,000","$25,000","$30,000","$55,000"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §722; IRC §752(a); IRC §752(b); Reg §1.707-5(a)(6)","rat":"Basis starts at the property's basis ($30,000), increases by the share of liabilities ($25,000) and decreases by the liabilities assumed by the partnership ($50,000): $5,000.","ex":["Basis starts at the property's basis ($30,000), increases by the share of liabilities ($25,000) and decreases by the liabilities assumed by the partnership ($50,000): $5,000.","Uses the net liability relief as the basis.","Ignores the liability adjustments.","Adds the share of liabilities without subtracting the mortgage relief."],"wt":["","calc","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Basis in a contributed-property partnership interest is property basis plus liability share minus liabilities assumed by the partnership.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Property basis 30,000","Plus share of liabilities 25,000","Less liabilities assumed by partnership 50,000","Basis = 5,000"],"gd":[["What is the starting basis from the land?","30,000."],["How do the liability share and liabilities assumed adjust it?","Plus 25,000 and less 50,000."],["What is the basis?","30,000 + 25,000 - 50,000 = 5,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0021","t":"Mika Sato contributed equipment (adjusted basis $25,000, fair market value $60,000) to a partnership in exchange for a partnership interest. Which statement about her capital account and outside basis is correct?","mc":{"s":"Mika Sato contributed equipment (adjusted basis $25,000, fair market value $60,000) to a partnership in exchange for a partnership interest. Which statement about her capital account and outside basis is correct?","d":"","o":["Her capital account is credited with $60,000 and her outside basis is $25,000","Her capital account is $25,000 and her outside basis is $60,000","Her capital account and outside basis are both $60,000, because fair market value applies","Her capital account and outside basis are both $25,000, because the exchange is tax-free"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §722; Reg. §1.704-1(b)(2)(iv)","rat":"The capital account reflects fair market value under the partnership's books, while outside basis reflects the property's adjusted basis.","ex":["The capital account reflects fair market value under the partnership's books, while outside basis reflects the property's adjusted basis.","The two amounts are reversed.","Outside basis is a carryover of adjusted basis, so it is not $60,000.","The capital account generally reflects fair market value, not adjusted basis."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A partner's capital account reflects FMV of contributed property while outside basis carries over adjusted basis.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["What does the capital account reflect on partnership books?","Fair market value of the contribution, 60,000."],["What does outside basis reflect?","Adjusted basis of the property, 25,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0022","t":"Cal Meyer sold his partnership interest, in which his basis was $60,000, to Bo Chen for $90,000 in cash. Bo's share of the partnership's liabilities is $10,000. What is Bo's basis in the partnership interest?","mc":{"s":"Cal Meyer sold his partnership interest, in which his basis was $60,000, to Bo Chen for $90,000 in cash. Bo's share of the partnership's liabilities is $10,000. What is Bo's basis in the partnership interest?","d":"","o":["$60,000","$80,000","$90,000","$100,000"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §742; IRC §1012; IRC §752","rat":"A purchaser's basis is cost plus his share of partnership liabilities: $90,000 + $10,000 = $100,000.","ex":["Carries over the seller's basis; a purchaser's basis is cost.","Subtracts the liabilities; they are added to a purchaser's basis.","Omits Bo's share of partnership liabilities.","A purchaser's basis is cost plus his share of partnership liabilities: $90,000 + $10,000 = $100,000."],"wt":["concept","concept","calc",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A purchaser's partnership basis is cost plus share of partnership liabilities.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Cost 90,000 + share of liabilities 10,000 = 100,000"],"gd":[["What is a purchaser's outside basis?","Cost plus his share of liabilities."],["What is the total?","90,000 + 10,000 = 100,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0023","t":"Dee Park inherited her mother's partnership interest. On the date of death its fair market value was $200,000, the mother's basis was $90,000, and Dee's share of partnership liabilities is $20,000. What is Dee's basis in the interest?","mc":{"s":"Dee Park inherited her mother's partnership interest. On the date of death its fair market value was $200,000, the mother's basis was $90,000, and Dee's share of partnership liabilities is $20,000. What is Dee's basis in the interest?","d":"","o":["$90,000","$180,000","$200,000","$220,000"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §1014; IRC §742; IRC §752; Reg §1.742-1","rat":"An inherited interest takes a basis equal to fair market value at death, plus the heir's share of partnership liabilities: $220,000.","ex":["Carries over the decedent's basis; inherited property takes fair market value.","Subtracts liabilities from fair market value.","Omits Dee's share of partnership liabilities.","An inherited interest takes a basis equal to fair market value at death, plus the heir's share of partnership liabilities: $220,000."],"wt":["concept","calc","calc",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"An inherited partnership interest takes FMV at death plus the heir's share of liabilities.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Fair market value 200,000 + share of liabilities 20,000 = 220,000"],"gd":[["What basis does an heir get in a partnership interest?","Fair market value at death plus her share of liabilities."],["What is the total?","200,000 + 20,000 = 220,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0040","t":"Partners Abe and Bea share losses equally. Abe's basis in his partnership interest is $10,000 and Bea's is $50,000. The partnership's 2026 ordinary loss is $80,000. Ignoring at-risk and passive activity rules, what total loss may they deduct in 2026?","mc":{"s":"Partners Abe and Bea share losses equally. Abe's basis in his partnership interest is $10,000 and Bea's is $50,000. The partnership's 2026 ordinary loss is $80,000. Ignoring at-risk and passive activity rules, what total loss may they deduct in 2026?","d":"","o":["$20,000","$50,000","$60,000","$80,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §704(d)","rat":"Each partner's share is $40,000, limited to basis.","ex":["Applies Abe's basis to both partners.","Each partner's share is $40,000, limited to basis. Abe deducts $10,000 and Bea deducts $40,000, a total of $50,000.","Uses Bea's whole basis rather than her $40,000 share of the loss.","Allows the entire loss, ignoring basis limits."],"wt":["concept","","calc","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Each partner's loss is limited to his own basis, so the total deductible is the sum of each partner's allowed amount.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Abe: min(40,000, 10,000) = 10,000; suspended 30,000","Bea: min(40,000, 50,000) = 40,000","Total = 50,000"],"gd":[["What is each partner's loss share?","80,000 / 2 = 40,000."],["What does each deduct given basis?","Abe min(40,000, 10,000) = 10,000; Bea min(40,000, 50,000) = 40,000."],["What is the total?","10,000 + 40,000 = 50,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-1002","t":"Faye Owens contributed $50,000 in cash to Lakeview Partners for a 30% interest. During 2026 the partnership borrowed from a bank, and Faye's share of the partnership's liabilities at year-end is $30,000. Her share of 2026 ordinary income is $20,000, and the partnership distributed $10,000 in cash to her. Which statement correctly gives her basis in the partnership interest and the tax-basis capital account on her Schedule K-1?","mc":{"s":"Faye Owens contributed $50,000 in cash to Lakeview Partners for a 30% interest. During 2026 the partnership borrowed from a bank, and Faye's share of the partnership's liabilities at year-end is $30,000. Her share of 2026 ordinary income is $20,000, and the partnership distributed $10,000 in cash to her. Which statement correctly gives her basis in the partnership interest and the tax-basis capital account on her Schedule K-1?","d":"","o":["Interest basis $90,000; tax-basis capital $60,000","Interest basis $60,000; tax-basis capital $60,000","Interest basis $90,000; tax-basis capital $90,000","Interest basis $60,000; tax-basis capital $90,000"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §705(a); IRC §752(a); IRC §722; Form 1065 Schedule K-1 instructions","rat":"Outside basis includes her $30,000 share of liabilities: $50,000 + $30,000 + $20,000 - $10,000 = $90,000.","ex":["Outside basis includes her $30,000 share of liabilities: $50,000 + $30,000 + $20,000 - $10,000 = $90,000. Tax-basis capital excludes liabilities: $50,000 + $20,000 - $10,000 = $60,000.","Leaves the $30,000 share of partnership liabilities out of outside basis.","Puts the liability share into the capital account too. Tax-basis capital does not include liabilities.","Reverses the two figures. Liabilities increase outside basis, not the capital account."],"wt":["","calc","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Outside basis includes the partner's share of partnership liabilities, while tax-basis capital on the K-1 does not.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Outside basis: 50,000 + 30,000 + 20,000 - 10,000 = 90,000","Tax capital: 50,000 + 20,000 - 10,000 = 60,000"],"gd":[["What does outside basis include?","Her share of liabilities: 50,000 + 30,000 + 20,000 - 10,000 = 90,000."],["Does tax-basis capital include liabilities?","No, so 50,000 + 20,000 - 10,000 = 60,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-1004","t":"Zoe Kim is a general partner who materially participates in a partnership. At the start of 2026 her basis in the partnership interest is $45,000, which includes $25,000 of her share of a nonrecourse loan that is not qualified nonrecourse financing, and her amount at risk is $20,000. Her share of the partnership's 2026 ordinary loss is $60,000, and no passive activity limit applies. How much of the loss can she deduct in 2026, and how is the rest treated?","mc":{"s":"Zoe Kim is a general partner who materially participates in a partnership. At the start of 2026 her basis in the partnership interest is $45,000, which includes $25,000 of her share of a nonrecourse loan that is not qualified nonrecourse financing, and her amount at risk is $20,000. Her share of the partnership's 2026 ordinary loss is $60,000, and no passive activity limit applies. How much of the loss can she deduct in 2026, and how is the rest treated?","d":"","o":["$45,000 is deductible; $15,000 is suspended by the basis limit","$20,000 is deductible; $15,000 is suspended by the basis limit and $25,000 is suspended by the at-risk limit","$60,000 is deductible, because she is a general partner who materially participates","$20,000 is deductible; the other $40,000 is suspended by the basis limit"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §704(d); IRC §465(a); Reg. §1.704-1(d)","rat":"The basis limit allows $45,000 and suspends $15,000.","ex":["Applies only the basis limit. The nonrecourse loan share is in basis but not at risk, so the at-risk limit cuts the deduction to $20,000.","The basis limit allows $45,000 and suspends $15,000. Of the $45,000, only her $20,000 at risk is deductible, and $25,000 is suspended by the at-risk rules.","Material participation affects the passive loss rules, not the basis and at-risk limits.","Lumps the whole disallowed amount into the basis limit. Only $15,000 exceeds basis; $25,000 is suspended by the at-risk limit."],"wt":["concept","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A partner's loss must clear the basis limit, then the at-risk limit, then the passive limit; each excess is suspended separately.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Basis: allowed 45,000; suspended 60,000 - 45,000 = 15,000","At-risk: allowed 20,000; suspended 45,000 - 20,000 = 25,000","Deductible 20,000"],"gd":[["What does the basis limit allow?","45,000, suspending 60,000 - 45,000 = 15,000."],["What does the at-risk limit allow within that?","Only her 20,000 at risk, suspending 45,000 - 20,000 = 25,000, since the nonrecourse loan is not qualified financing."],["What is deductible?","20,000."]]}}],"11":[{"i":"REG-V-D-0024","t":"Neil Ferris has a basis of $50,000 in his partnership interest. In a current distribution he receives equipment with an adjusted basis to the partnership of $20,000 and a fair market value of $40,000, and no cash. What is his basis in the equipment?","mc":{"s":"Neil Ferris has a basis of $50,000 in his partnership interest. In a current distribution he receives equipment with an adjusted basis to the partnership of $20,000 and a fair market value of $40,000, and no cash. What is his basis in the equipment?","d":"","o":["$20,000","$30,000","$40,000","$50,000"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §732(a); IRC §731(a)","rat":"In a current distribution the partner takes the partnership's adjusted basis in the property, limited to his outside basis: $20,000.","ex":["In a current distribution the partner takes the partnership's adjusted basis in the property, limited to his outside basis: $20,000.","Uses his remaining outside basis after the distribution instead of the property's basis.","Uses fair market value; a partner takes carryover basis in property distributed by a partnership.","Uses the partner's whole outside basis, which is only a limit on the property's basis."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"In a current distribution the partner takes the partnership's adjusted basis in distributed property, limited to outside basis.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Partnership basis in equipment = 20,000, below outside basis 50,000","Partner's basis in equipment = 20,000; outside basis becomes 30,000"],"gd":[["What is the partner's basis in property in a current distribution?","The partnership's adjusted basis, limited to outside basis."],["What is the basis here?","20,000, below the 50,000 outside basis, so outside basis falls to 30,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0025","t":"Opal Nakamura has a $15,000 basis in her partnership interest. In a current distribution she receives land with an adjusted basis to the partnership of $20,000 and a fair market value of $45,000, and no cash. What is her basis in the land?","mc":{"s":"Opal Nakamura has a $15,000 basis in her partnership interest. In a current distribution she receives land with an adjusted basis to the partnership of $20,000 and a fair market value of $45,000, and no cash. What is her basis in the land?","d":"","o":["$0","$15,000","$20,000","$35,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §732(a)(2)","rat":"In a current distribution the property's basis cannot exceed the partner's outside basis.","ex":["Assigns her whole basis to the partnership interest and none to the land.","In a current distribution the property's basis cannot exceed the partner's outside basis. Her land basis is limited to $15,000, and her partnership basis falls to $0.","Uses the partnership's basis without applying the limit of outside basis.","Adds the outside basis and the partnership's basis together."],"wt":["concept","","concept","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"In a current distribution, property basis cannot exceed the partner's outside basis.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Partnership's basis in land 20,000 exceeds outside basis 15,000","Land basis = 15,000; outside basis after = 0"],"gd":[["What limits a partner's basis in distributed property?","Her outside basis."],["What is the land basis and outside basis afterward?","15,000 instead of 20,000, and outside basis drops to 0."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0026","t":"Pete Lindgren liquidated his entire partnership interest, in which his basis was $60,000, and received only $45,000 in cash. The partnership has no unrealized receivables or inventory. What loss does Pete recognize?","mc":{"s":"Pete Lindgren liquidated his entire partnership interest, in which his basis was $60,000, and received only $45,000 in cash. The partnership has no unrealized receivables or inventory. What loss does Pete recognize?","d":"","o":["$0","$15,000","$45,000","$60,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §731(a)(2)","rat":"In a liquidation, a loss is recognized only if the partner receives just money, unrealized receivables and inventory.","ex":["Assumes losses are never recognized on a distribution; in a liquidation for money only, the loss is recognized.","In a liquidation, a loss is recognized only if the partner receives just money, unrealized receivables and inventory. Here he receives only cash, so the loss is $60,000 - $45,000 = $15,000.","Uses the cash received as the loss.","Uses the whole basis as the loss without offsetting the cash received."],"wt":["concept","","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"In a liquidation receiving only money, unrealized receivables and inventory, the partner recognizes a loss for basis minus cash.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Basis 60,000 - cash received 45,000 = 15,000 capital loss"],"gd":[["When is a loss recognized in a liquidation?","Only when the partner receives just money, unrealized receivables and inventory."],["What is the loss?","60,000 - 45,000 = 15,000 capital loss."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0027","t":"Quinn Alder sold her entire partnership interest for $80,000 in cash. Her basis in the interest, including her $20,000 share of partnership liabilities, was $50,000, and the buyer assumed her $20,000 share of liabilities. Of her gain, $15,000 is attributable to unrealized receivables. How much capital gain does Quinn recognize?","mc":{"s":"Quinn Alder sold her entire partnership interest for $80,000 in cash. Her basis in the interest, including her $20,000 share of partnership liabilities, was $50,000, and the buyer assumed her $20,000 share of liabilities. Of her gain, $15,000 is attributable to unrealized receivables. How much capital gain does Quinn recognize?","d":"","o":["$15,000","$35,000","$50,000","$65,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §741; IRC §751(a); IRC §752(d)","rat":"Amount realized is $80,000 + $20,000 = $100,000, and the total gain is $50,000.","ex":["Uses only the ordinary income portion.","Amount realized is $80,000 + $20,000 = $100,000, and the total gain is $50,000. Subtract the $15,000 ordinary income from unrealized receivables: $35,000 is capital gain.","Treats the whole $50,000 as capital gain, ignoring the ordinary income on unrealized receivables.","Adds the ordinary income to the gain instead of subtracting it."],"wt":["calc","","concept","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"On sale of a partnership interest, gain from unrealized receivables is ordinary, and amount realized includes liability relief.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Amount realized = 80,000 + 20,000 liabilities relieved = 100,000","Total gain = 100,000 - 50,000 = 50,000","Capital gain = 50,000 - 15,000 ordinary = 35,000"],"gd":[["What is the amount realized including liability relief?","80,000 + 20,000 = 100,000."],["What is the total gain?","100,000 - 50,000 = 50,000."],["What part is capital gain?","50,000 - 15,000 ordinary from receivables = 35,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0028","t":"Ivo Petrov sells his interest in a cash-basis service partnership. Which part of the gain is taxed as ordinary income rather than capital gain?","mc":{"s":"Ivo Petrov sells his interest in a cash-basis service partnership. Which part of the gain is taxed as ordinary income rather than capital gain?","d":"","o":["The part attributable to the partnership's goodwill","The part attributable to the partner's share of liabilities","No part of the gain, since the sale of an interest is capital gain in full","The part attributable to the partnership's unrealized receivables"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §751(a); IRC §741","rat":"Amounts attributable to unrealized receivables and inventory items are taxed as ordinary income on a sale of an interest.","ex":["Goodwill generally produces capital gain when a partnership interest is sold.","The share of liabilities affects amount realized and basis, not character.","Amounts attributable to unrealized receivables and inventory are ordinary.","Amounts attributable to unrealized receivables and inventory items are taxed as ordinary income on a sale of an interest."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"On sale of a partnership interest, amounts from unrealized receivables and inventory are ordinary; goodwill gain is capital.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Which part of a sale of a partnership interest is ordinary income?","The part attributable to hot assets."],["Which listed component is that?","Unrealized receivables; goodwill and liabilities are not ordinary."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0029","t":"Bea Norwood bought Cal's partnership interest for $150,000. Her share of the partnership's basis in its assets (inside basis) is $100,000. The partnership has a basis adjustment election in effect. What is the basis adjustment to the partnership's assets with respect to Bea?","mc":{"s":"Bea Norwood bought Cal's partnership interest for $150,000. Her share of the partnership's basis in its assets (inside basis) is $100,000. The partnership has a basis adjustment election in effect. What is the basis adjustment to the partnership's assets with respect to Bea?","d":"","o":["$0","$50,000","$100,000","$150,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §743(b); IRC §754","rat":"With the election, Bea's share of asset basis is increased by the difference between her outside basis (cost) and her share of inside basis: $50,000.","ex":["Assumes no adjustment, which is the result without the election.","With the election, Bea's share of asset basis is increased by the difference between her outside basis (cost) and her share of inside basis: $50,000.","Uses her share of inside basis as the adjustment.","Uses her cost as the adjustment."],"wt":["concept","","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"With a section 754 election, the transferee's basis adjustment is her outside basis minus her share of inside basis.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Cost 150,000 - share of inside basis 100,000 = 50,000 increase"],"gd":[["What does the basis adjustment election compare?","The buyer's cost against her share of inside basis."],["What is the adjustment?","150,000 - 100,000 = 50,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0030","t":"In which situation must a partnership adjust the basis of its assets for a transferee partner even though no basis adjustment election is in effect?","mc":{"s":"In which situation must a partnership adjust the basis of its assets for a transferee partner even though no basis adjustment election is in effect?","d":"","o":["A partner buys an interest when the partnership's adjusted basis exceeds asset value by $200,000","A partner gives his interest to a child as a gift when the assets are worth much more than basis","A partner buys an interest when the partnership's adjusted basis in its assets exceeds their fair market value by $300,000","A new partner contributes cash to the partnership for a new interest"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §743(a); IRC §743(d)","rat":"A mandatory adjustment applies on a transfer if the partnership has a substantial built-in loss (more than $250,000).","ex":["The loss is below the $250,000 threshold, so no mandatory adjustment applies.","A gift is not a sale, exchange or death transfer, so the adjustment rules do not apply.","A mandatory adjustment applies on a transfer if the partnership has a substantial built-in loss (more than $250,000).","A contribution to the partnership is not a transfer of an existing interest."],"wt":["limit","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A mandatory basis adjustment applies on a transfer if the partnership has a substantial built-in loss over $250,000.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["When is an adjustment mandatory without an election?","On a transfer where the partnership has a substantial built-in loss over 250,000."],["Which transfer meets that?","The purchase when inside basis exceeds asset value by 300,000; the 200,000 case, a gift and a contribution do not."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0031","t":"Draft calculation of the gain on the sale of Hana Kwon's partnership interest is shown. Hana's basis included her $20,000 share of partnership liabilities, which the buyer assumed. Which correction is needed?","mc":{"s":"Draft calculation of the gain on the sale of Hana Kwon's partnership interest is shown. Hana's basis included her $20,000 share of partnership liabilities, which the buyer assumed. Which correction is needed?","d":"","o":["Include the $20,000 of liabilities assumed in the amount realized; total gain is $50,000","Add another $20,000 to basis for the liabilities; total gain is $10,000","Leave the gain at $30,000, because liabilities do not affect a sale of an interest","Increase the gain to $70,000, by adding the $20,000 of liabilities to both sides"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §752(d); IRC §741","rat":"Relief from partnership liabilities is part of the amount realized, so the amount realized is $100,000 and the gain is $50,000.","ex":["Relief from partnership liabilities is part of the amount realized, so the amount realized is $100,000 and the gain is $50,000.","Her basis already includes the $20,000 share of liabilities, so adding it again double counts and understates the gain.","Liability relief increases amount realized on the sale of a partnership interest.","Adding it to both sides cancels out; only the amount realized changes."],"wt":["","calc","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Liability relief is part of the amount realized on the sale of a partnership interest, and basis already includes the liability share.","tb":[["Draft gain calculation","Amount"],["Cash received","$80,000"],["Adjusted basis of interest (including $20,000 share of liabilities)","($50,000)"],["Gain on sale","$30,000"]],"from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Amount realized = 80,000 + 20,000 = 100,000","Gain = 100,000 - 50,000 = 50,000"],"gd":[["Is liability relief part of the amount realized?","Yes."],["What is the amount realized and the gain?","80,000 + 20,000 = 100,000, so 100,000 - 50,000 = 50,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0032","t":"Two entities each own land with an adjusted basis of $30,000 and a fair market value of $100,000, and each distributes the land to a 50% owner as a current distribution. One entity is an S corporation and the other a partnership. Which statement compares the entity-level results correctly?","mc":{"s":"Two entities each own land with an adjusted basis of $30,000 and a fair market value of $100,000, and each distributes the land to a 50% owner as a current distribution. One entity is an S corporation and the other a partnership. Which statement compares the entity-level results correctly?","d":"","o":["Both entities recognize a $70,000 gain on distributing the land to a 50% owner","Neither entity recognizes gain, because both are pass-through entities that are not themselves taxed","The partnership recognizes a $70,000 gain and the S corporation recognizes no gain on the distribution","The S corporation recognizes a $70,000 gain that passes through to shareholders; the partnership generally recognizes none"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §311(b); IRC §1371(a); IRC §731(b)","rat":"A corporation, including an S corporation, recognizes gain on distributing appreciated property.","ex":["The partnership does not recognize gain on a current distribution of property.","An S corporation is taxed like a corporation on the distribution of appreciated property.","The results are reversed.","A corporation, including an S corporation, recognizes gain on distributing appreciated property. A partnership generally recognizes no gain on a current distribution."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"An S corporation recognizes gain on distributing appreciated property, while a partnership generally recognizes none on a current distribution.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Does a corporation recognize gain on distributing appreciated property?","Yes, including an S corporation."],["How does a partnership treat a current distribution?","Generally with no gain, so only the S corporation recognizes the 70,000 that passes through."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0033","t":"Ray Chavez liquidated his partnership interest, in which his basis was $80,000. He received $20,000 in cash and land with a partnership adjusted basis of $30,000 and a fair market value of $90,000. What is his basis in the land?","mc":{"s":"Ray Chavez liquidated his partnership interest, in which his basis was $80,000. He received $20,000 in cash and land with a partnership adjusted basis of $30,000 and a fair market value of $90,000. What is his basis in the land?","d":"","o":["$30,000","$60,000","$80,000","$90,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §732(b); IRC §731(a)","rat":"In a liquidation the partner's basis in distributed property (other than money) is his remaining outside basis after the cash: $80,000 - $20,000 = $60,000.","ex":["Carries over the partnership's basis, which applies to current distributions but not to a liquidation.","In a liquidation the partner's basis in distributed property (other than money) is his remaining outside basis after the cash: $80,000 - $20,000 = $60,000.","Uses the whole outside basis without reducing it by the cash.","Uses fair market value, which does not determine a partner's basis in distributed property."],"wt":["concept","","calc","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"In a liquidation the partner's basis in distributed property is remaining outside basis after cash.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Outside basis 80,000 - cash 20,000 = 60,000 allocated to the land"],"gd":[["What is Ray's basis in the land in a liquidation?","His remaining outside basis after the cash."],["What is it?","80,000 - 20,000 = 60,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0034","t":"Tara Wolfe liquidated her partnership interest, in which her basis was $25,000. She received $20,000 in cash and land with a partnership adjusted basis of $30,000 and a fair market value of $90,000. Which statement is correct?","mc":{"s":"Tara Wolfe liquidated her partnership interest, in which her basis was $25,000. She received $20,000 in cash and land with a partnership adjusted basis of $30,000 and a fair market value of $90,000. Which statement is correct?","d":"","o":["She recognizes no gain, and her basis in the land is $30,000","She recognizes a $25,000 gain because the cash and land basis exceed her basis","She recognizes no gain, and her basis in the land is $5,000","She recognizes a $65,000 gain, and her basis in the land is $90,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §732(b); IRC §731(a)(1)","rat":"Cash of $20,000 does not exceed her $25,000 basis, so no gain.","ex":["The land's basis is limited to her remaining outside basis after the cash.","Gain arises only if the cash exceeds her outside basis.","Cash of $20,000 does not exceed her $25,000 basis, so no gain. The land takes her remaining basis of $5,000.","Fair market value is not used, and no gain arises here."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"In a liquidation with cash below basis, no gain arises and the property takes the remaining outside basis.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Cash 20,000 < basis 25,000, so no gain","Land basis = 25,000 - 20,000 = 5,000"],"gd":[["Does the cash exceed her basis?","No, 20,000 is below 25,000, so no gain."],["What is the land basis?","25,000 - 20,000 = 5,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0041","t":"Partner Mia Lowry has a $30,000 basis in her partnership interest. She received a $45,000 cash distribution in 2026 that did not involve unrealized receivables or inventory. Her draft return reports $15,000 of ordinary income on the distribution. Which correction is needed?","mc":{"s":"Partner Mia Lowry has a $30,000 basis in her partnership interest. She received a $45,000 cash distribution in 2026 that did not involve unrealized receivables or inventory. Her draft return reports $15,000 of ordinary income on the distribution. Which correction is needed?","d":"","o":["Report $15,000 of ordinary income, so no correction is needed","Report no gain, and carry the $15,000 excess forward to increase basis next year","Report a $15,000 capital gain, because cash in excess of basis is gain from the sale of the partnership interest","Report a $45,000 capital gain, because the entire distribution is gain"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §731(a)(1); IRC §741","rat":"Money distributed in excess of the partner's basis is treated as gain from the sale of the interest, which is capital gain.","ex":["Gain on a distribution in excess of basis is capital in nature unless hot assets are involved.","Excess distributions do not carry forward; they are gain.","Money distributed in excess of the partner's basis is treated as gain from the sale of the interest, which is capital gain.","Only the excess over basis is gain; the first $30,000 reduces basis."],"wt":["concept","concept","","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Money distributed in excess of partnership basis is gain from the sale of the interest, taxed as capital gain.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Distribution 45,000 - basis 30,000 = 15,000 excess","Character: capital gain, absent unrealized receivables or inventory"],"gd":[["How is cash over basis in a partnership distribution treated?","As gain from the sale of the interest."],["What is the excess and its character?","45,000 - 30,000 = 15,000 capital gain."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-1001","t":"Nora Beck contributed land with an adjusted basis of $40,000 and a fair market value of $100,000 to Eastgate Partners. The land has no debt. One month later the partnership distributed $30,000 in cash to Nora; the payment was not a return of operating profits and was not dependent on partnership income. How much gain does Nora recognize, and what is the partnership's basis in the land?","mc":{"s":"Nora Beck contributed land with an adjusted basis of $40,000 and a fair market value of $100,000 to Eastgate Partners. The land has no debt. One month later the partnership distributed $30,000 in cash to Nora; the payment was not a return of operating profits and was not dependent on partnership income. How much gain does Nora recognize, and what is the partnership's basis in the land?","d":"","o":["No gain, and a $40,000 basis","$18,000 of gain, and a $58,000 basis","$30,000 of gain, and a $40,000 basis","$18,000 of gain, and a $40,000 basis"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §707(a)(2)(B); Reg. §1.707-3(a),(f); IRC §723; IRC §1012","rat":"A contribution followed within two years by a related distribution is presumed to be a sale of part of the property (30% here).","ex":["Treats the exchange as fully tax-free under the contribution rules. The related cash payment within two years is a disguised sale.","A contribution followed within two years by a related distribution is presumed to be a sale of part of the property (30% here). Gain = $30,000 - 30% x $40,000 = $18,000. The partnership's basis is $28,000 carryover for the contributed 70% plus $30,000 cost for the purchased 30%: $58,000.","Treats the entire cash as gain without recovering any basis, and gives no cost basis for the purchased portion.","Gets the gain right but forgets that the partnership bought 30% of the land for $30,000, which gives it a cost basis in that part."],"wt":["concept","","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A contribution and a related distribution within two years is presumed a disguised sale of the part of the property paid for.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Sold portion: 30,000 / 100,000 = 30% of the land","Gain: 30,000 - 30% x 40,000 = 18,000","Partnership basis: 70% x 40,000 = 28,000 carryover + 30,000 cost = 58,000"],"gd":[["What is the presumption for a distribution soon after a contribution?","A sale of part of the property."],["What share was sold and what is the gain?","30,000 / 100,000 = 30%, so 30,000 - 30% x 40,000 = 18,000."],["What is the partnership's basis?","70% x 40,000 = 28,000 carryover plus 30,000 cost = 58,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-1005","t":"Rowan Tate retired from Tate & Fenn, a cash-method law partnership in which capital is not a material income-producing factor, and received $150,000 in cash in complete liquidation of her interest. Her basis in the interest was $100,000. Her share of the partnership's assets was $120,000 of cash and other capital property and $30,000 of unrealized receivables with a zero basis. The agreement makes no payment for goodwill, and the payment is a fixed sum. What is the character of her income?","mc":{"s":"Rowan Tate retired from Tate & Fenn, a cash-method law partnership in which capital is not a material income-producing factor, and received $150,000 in cash in complete liquidation of her interest. Her basis in the interest was $100,000. Her share of the partnership's assets was $120,000 of cash and other capital property and $30,000 of unrealized receivables with a zero basis. The agreement makes no payment for goodwill, and the payment is a fixed sum. What is the character of her income?","d":"","o":["$30,000 of ordinary income and $20,000 of capital gain","$50,000 of capital gain","$50,000 of ordinary income","$20,000 of ordinary income and $30,000 of capital gain"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §736(a); IRC §736(b); IRC §731(a)(1); IRC §751(c)","rat":"The $30,000 paid for unrealized receivables is a section 736(a) payment (ordinary income).","ex":["The $30,000 paid for unrealized receivables is a section 736(a) payment (ordinary income). The other $120,000 is a section 736(b) distribution: $120,000 - $100,000 basis = $20,000 capital gain.","Treats the whole payment as a sale of the interest. The part paid for unrealized receivables in a service partnership is ordinary income.","Treats every dollar as ordinary. Only the $30,000 for unrealized receivables is a section 736(a) payment; the rest gives capital gain.","Reverses the two amounts. The receivables portion ($30,000) is ordinary and the excess over basis ($20,000) is capital gain."],"wt":["","concept","concept","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"In a service partnership, liquidating payments for unrealized receivables (and unagreed goodwill) are ordinary section 736(a) income; payments for other property are section 736(b) distributions.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Payment for receivables 30,000: ordinary income under 736(a)","Property payment 120,000 vs basis 100,000: 20,000 capital gain","Total 50,000 = 150,000 - 100,000"],"gd":[["Which part of a retiring partner's payment is for unrealized receivables?","30,000, a section 736(a) payment taxed as ordinary income."],["How is the property payment treated?","It is a section 736(b) distribution: 120,000 - 100,000 basis = 20,000 capital gain."],["What are the two characters?","30,000 ordinary and 20,000 capital gain, totaling 50,000 = 150,000 - 100,000."]]}}],"12":[{"i":"REG-V-D-0035","t":"Ann and Ben each own 50% of Camden Partners. Ann contributed land with an adjusted basis of $40,000 and a fair market value of $100,000. Ben contributed $100,000 in cash. Three years later the partnership sold the land for $130,000. Using the traditional method, how much of the gain is allocated to Ben?","mc":{"s":"Ann and Ben each own 50% of Camden Partners. Ann contributed land with an adjusted basis of $40,000 and a fair market value of $100,000. Ben contributed $100,000 in cash. Three years later the partnership sold the land for $130,000. Using the traditional method, how much of the gain is allocated to Ben?","d":"","o":["$0","$15,000","$45,000","$75,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §704(c)(1)(A); Reg. §1.704-3(b)","rat":"The $60,000 built-in gain is allocated to Ann first.","ex":["Allocates all gain to Ann.","The $60,000 built-in gain is allocated to Ann first. The remaining $30,000 of gain ($130,000 - $100,000) is shared 50/50, so Ben gets $15,000.","Splits the total $90,000 gain equally, ignoring the built-in gain that belongs to the contributor.","This is Ann's total gain, not Ben's."],"wt":["concept","","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Under the traditional method, built-in gain on contributed property is allocated to the contributor before remaining gain is shared.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Total gain = 130,000 - 40,000 = 90,000","Built-in gain to Ann = 100,000 - 40,000 = 60,000","Remaining 30,000 shared 50/50: Ben 15,000, Ann 15,000"],"gd":[["What is the total gain and the built-in gain?","130,000 - 40,000 = 90,000 total and 100,000 - 40,000 = 60,000 built-in, allocated to Ann."],["What remains to share?","90,000 - 60,000 = 30,000 split 50/50."],["What does Ben get?","15,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0036","t":"Which special allocation of partnership items among partners is most likely to be respected for federal tax purposes?","mc":{"s":"Which special allocation of partnership items among partners is most likely to be respected for federal tax purposes?","d":"","o":["An allocation of 90% of depreciation to one partner, with capital accounts maintained, liquidation by capital accounts and deficit restoration","An allocation of tax-exempt income to the partner in the lowest bracket that does not change any partner's capital account or distributions at all","An allocation of income to one partner in one year and offsetting loss in the next, made only to change the partners' tax brackets","An allocation of gain that follows equal shares while the partners' capital accounts and distributions follow a 70/30 split of profits"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §704(b); Reg. §1.704-1(b)(2)","rat":"An allocation has substantial economic effect when it follows the partners' economic arrangement, including capital accounts, liquidation by capital accounts and deficit restoration.","ex":["An allocation has substantial economic effect when it follows the partners' economic arrangement, including capital accounts, liquidation by capital accounts and deficit restoration.","An allocation with no effect on the economic deal does not have economic effect.","An allocation that merely shifts tax consequences without changing amounts the partners receive is not substantial.","The allocation disagrees with the partners' economic arrangement, so it would not be respected."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A special allocation is respected only with substantial economic effect, following capital accounts and the partners' economic deal.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["What makes a special allocation respected?","Substantial economic effect: it follows capital accounts, liquidation by capital accounts and deficit restoration."],["Which allocation has those features?","The depreciation allocation with capital accounts maintained and deficit restoration; bracket-shifting or mismatched allocations fail."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0037","t":"Partner Lin owns 55% of the profits and capital of a partnership and uses a June 30 fiscal year. Partner Moe owns 45% and uses a calendar year. No other partners exist. What tax year must the partnership generally use?","mc":{"s":"Partner Lin owns 55% of the profits and capital of a partnership and uses a June 30 fiscal year. Partner Moe owns 45% and uses a calendar year. No other partners exist. What tax year must the partnership generally use?","d":"","o":["December 31, because the partnership is a calendar-year business entity by default","December 31, because Moe has the calendar year and partnerships must use the year of the smallest partner","Either year, at the partners' choice on Form 1065","June 30, the tax year of the partners owning a majority interest in profits and capital"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §706(b)(1)(B)","rat":"A partnership generally adopts the tax year of partners who own more than 50% of profits and capital.","ex":["There is no default of December 31 when a majority-interest year exists.","There is no rule favoring the smaller partner.","The majority interest rule controls unless another permitted year is elected.","A partnership generally adopts the tax year of partners who own more than 50% of profits and capital."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A partnership generally adopts the tax year of partners owning more than 50% of profits and capital.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Which partners set the partnership's tax year?","Those owning more than 50% of profits and capital."],["Who is that here and what year do they use?","Lin at 55%, so the June 30 year."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0038","t":"Two service providers each work for a partnership. Ola receives a 20% profits interest with no right to current partnership capital. Rex receives a 20% capital interest worth $30,000 at the grant date. Both interests vest immediately. Which statement about the grant is correct?","mc":{"s":"Two service providers each work for a partnership. Ola receives a 20% profits interest with no right to current partnership capital. Rex receives a 20% capital interest worth $30,000 at the grant date. Both interests vest immediately. Which statement about the grant is correct?","d":"","o":["Both have $30,000 of ordinary income, because any interest received for services is compensation","Neither has income, because interests received in partnerships are not taxed on receipt","Rex has $30,000 of ordinary income, while Ola generally has no income if the profits interest conditions are met","Ola has ordinary income, and Rex has none because a capital interest is not compensation"],"a":3,"cite":"Rev. Proc. 93-27; IRC §83; Reg. §1.721-1(b)(1)","rat":"A capital interest received for services is compensation at its value.","ex":["A profits interest generally is not taxed on receipt.","A capital interest received for services is taxed at its value.","A capital interest received for services is compensation at its value. A profits interest generally is not taxed on receipt under the IRS safe harbor.","This reverses the results."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A capital interest received for services is taxable compensation at value, while a profits interest generally is not taxed on receipt.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["How is a capital interest received for services treated?","As compensation at its value, 30,000."],["How is a profits interest generally treated?","Not taxed on receipt if the safe harbor conditions are met."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-0039","t":"Vic Aaron contributed land with an adjusted basis of $20,000 and a fair market value of $80,000 to a partnership in exchange for a 50% interest. What is the partnership's basis in the land?","mc":{"s":"Vic Aaron contributed land with an adjusted basis of $20,000 and a fair market value of $80,000 to a partnership in exchange for a 50% interest. What is the partnership's basis in the land?","d":"","o":["$0","$20,000","$60,000","$80,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §723; IRC §721(a)","rat":"The partnership takes the contributing partner's adjusted basis: $20,000.","ex":["Assigns no basis to the land, though the partnership takes carryover basis.","The partnership takes the contributing partner's adjusted basis: $20,000. Neither Vic nor the partnership recognizes gain.","Uses the built-in gain, which is not a basis amount.","Uses fair market value; the partnership takes carryover basis."],"wt":["concept","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A partnership takes carryover basis in property contributed by a partner.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Partnership basis = contributor's adjusted basis = 20,000"],"gd":[["What basis does the partnership take in contributed property?","The contributing partner's adjusted basis."],["What is it?","20,000, with no gain to either."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-1003","t":"Nina Park owns land with an adjusted basis of $30,000 and a fair market value of $100,000, subject to a $50,000 recourse mortgage she took out several years ago. She and a second owner, who will contribute $50,000 in cash, will form either (1) a partnership, in which both are general partners sharing liabilities equally, or (2) a corporation in which each receives 50% of the stock. In either case the entity assumes the mortgage. What result does Nina have under each structure?","mc":{"s":"Nina Park owns land with an adjusted basis of $30,000 and a fair market value of $100,000, subject to a $50,000 recourse mortgage she took out several years ago. She and a second owner, who will contribute $50,000 in cash, will form either (1) a partnership, in which both are general partners sharing liabilities equally, or (2) a corporation in which each receives 50% of the stock. In either case the entity assumes the mortgage. What result does Nina have under each structure?","d":"","o":["(1) $20,000 of gain and a $30,000 basis in the partnership interest; (2) $20,000 of gain and a $0 basis in the stock","(1) No gain and a $30,000 basis in the partnership interest; (2) $20,000 of gain and a $0 basis in the stock","(1) No gain and a $5,000 basis in the partnership interest; (2) no gain and a $30,000 basis in the stock","(1) No gain and a $5,000 basis in the partnership interest; (2) $20,000 of gain and a $0 basis in the stock"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §721(a); IRC §722; IRC §752(a),(b); IRC §357(c)(1); IRC §358(d)","rat":"Partnership: liability relief of $50,000 less her $25,000 share is a net $25,000, below basis, so no gain; basis $30,000 - $25,000 = $5,000.","ex":["Applies the corporate excess-liability rule to the partnership. A partnership contribution triggers gain only if net liability relief exceeds basis.","Ignores the liability adjustments in the partnership, where relief and her share reduce basis to $5,000.","Ignores that the corporation's assumption of liabilities exceeding basis is gain, and that the liabilities reduce her stock basis.","Partnership: liability relief of $50,000 less her $25,000 share is a net $25,000, below basis, so no gain; basis $30,000 - $25,000 = $5,000. Corporation: liabilities of $50,000 exceed her $30,000 basis by $20,000, which is gain, and stock basis is $30,000 - $50,000 + $20,000 = $0."],"wt":["concept","calc","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Liabilities exceeding basis are taxable gain on a corporate transfer, but in a partnership only net liability relief above basis is gain.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Partnership: relief 50,000 - retained share 25,000 = 25,000 < basis 30,000, no gain; basis 30,000 - 25,000 = 5,000","Corporation: 50,000 - 30,000 = 20,000 gain; stock basis 30,000 - 50,000 + 20,000 = 0"],"gd":[["What is the partnership result?","Liability relief 50,000 less her retained 25,000 is a net 25,000, below basis, so no gain and basis 30,000 - 25,000 = 5,000."],["What is the corporation result?","Liabilities 50,000 exceed basis 30,000, so 20,000 of gain."],["What is the stock basis?","30,000 - 50,000 + 20,000 = 0."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-1006","t":"Zane Ortiz bought a 20% interest in Delta Partners, a calendar-year partnership, from an existing partner on July 1, 2026. Delta's 2026 ordinary business income was $240,000, earned evenly through the year, on gross income of $600,000. It also sold a capital asset outside the ordinary course on November 1, 2026 for a $100,000 gain. The partners have agreed in writing to use the proration method for a change in interests, and Zane's percentage did not change after July 1. What total 2026 income does Zane report from these items?","mc":{"s":"Zane Ortiz bought a 20% interest in Delta Partners, a calendar-year partnership, from an existing partner on July 1, 2026. Delta's 2026 ordinary business income was $240,000, earned evenly through the year, on gross income of $600,000. It also sold a capital asset outside the ordinary course on November 1, 2026 for a $100,000 gain. The partners have agreed in writing to use the proration method for a change in interests, and Zane's percentage did not change after July 1. What total 2026 income does Zane report from these items?","d":"","o":["$24,197","$34,279","$44,197","$68,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §706(d)(1); Reg. §1.706-4(a)(1); Reg. §1.706-4(e)(2)","rat":"Ordinary income is prorated: $240,000 x 20% x 184/365 = $24,197.","ex":["Includes only the prorated ordinary income and leaves out his 20% of the November gain.","Prorates the gain as well as ordinary income. Extraordinary items are allocated on the day they occur.","Ordinary income is prorated: $240,000 x 20% x 184/365 = $24,197. The $100,000 gain is an extraordinary item allocated to the partners on the date it occurred, and Zane was a partner: 20% = $20,000. Total $44,197.","Gives Zane 20% of the full-year amounts, as if he had been a partner all year."],"wt":["calc","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Under an agreed proration method a new partner's share of ordinary income is prorated by days, but extraordinary items such as a capital asset sale are allocated to the partners at the date of the item.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Days July 1 to December 31 = 184","Ordinary: 240,000 x 20% x 184/365 = 24,197","Extraordinary gain on Nov 1: 20% x 100,000 = 20,000","Total 44,197"],"gd":[["How many days did Zane own the interest?","July 1 to December 31 is 184 days."],["What is ordinary income under the proration method?","240,000 x 20% x 184/365 = 24,197."],["How is the November gain treated and what is the total?","An extraordinary item allocated on its date, 20% x 100,000 = 20,000, so 24,197 + 20,000 = 44,197."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-D-1007","t":"Ann and Ben are equal partners in Camden Partners. Ann contributed land with an adjusted basis of $40,000 and a fair market value of $100,000, and Ben contributed $100,000 in cash. Three years later the partnership sold the land for $70,000. The partnership has no other items. What does each partner report from the sale under the traditional method with the ceiling rule and under the remedial method?","mc":{"s":"Ann and Ben are equal partners in Camden Partners. Ann contributed land with an adjusted basis of $40,000 and a fair market value of $100,000, and Ben contributed $100,000 in cash. Three years later the partnership sold the land for $70,000. The partnership has no other items. What does each partner report from the sale under the traditional method with the ceiling rule and under the remedial method?","d":"","o":["Traditional: Ann reports $30,000 of gain and Ben reports a $15,000 loss. Remedial: the same amounts","Traditional: Ann reports $45,000 of gain and Ben reports a $15,000 loss. Remedial: Ann reports $30,000 of gain and Ben reports no loss","Both methods: Ann reports $30,000 of gain and Ben reports no loss, because the ceiling rule applies under every method","Traditional: Ann reports $30,000 of gain and Ben reports no loss. Remedial: Ann reports $45,000 of gain and Ben reports a $15,000 loss"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §704(c)(1)(A); Reg. §1.704-3(b); Reg. §1.704-3(d)","rat":"The tax gain is $70,000 - $40,000 = $30,000, and the book loss is $30,000, shared equally.","ex":["Gives Ben a loss under the traditional method. The ceiling rule limits allocations to the partnership's actual tax items, and there is only a gain.","Swaps the methods. The remedial method, not the traditional method, creates offsetting remedial items.","The remedial method is designed to override the ceiling rule by creating offsetting remedial income and loss.","The tax gain is $70,000 - $40,000 = $30,000, and the book loss is $30,000, shared equally. The ceiling rule stops the traditional method from giving Ben a tax loss. The remedial method creates a $15,000 loss for Ben and equal income for Ann."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Under the traditional method the ceiling rule can leave the non-contributing partner with less tax loss than book loss, while the remedial method creates offsetting items to fix it.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Book: 70,000 - 100,000 = -30,000, so -15,000 each","Tax: 70,000 - 40,000 = +30,000 gain; traditional ceiling rule: Ann 30,000, Ben 0","Remedial: Ben gets a 15,000 remedial loss, Ann 15,000 remedial income: Ann 45,000, Ben -15,000"],"gd":[["What are the book and tax results of the sale?","Book: 70,000 - 100,000 = a 30,000 loss, 15,000 each; tax: 70,000 - 40,000 = a 30,000 gain."],["What does the ceiling rule do under the traditional method?","It gives Ann 30,000 of gain and Ben no tax loss."],["What does the remedial method add?","A 15,000 remedial loss to Ben and 15,000 remedial income to Ann, so Ann 45,000 and Ben -15,000."]]}}],"13":[{"i":"REG-V-E-0001","t":"Sol Fernandez is the only member of Fernandez Landscaping, LLC, a domestic limited liability company. It has made no entity classification election. How is the LLC classified for federal income tax purposes, and how does Sol report its income?","mc":{"s":"Sol Fernandez is the only member of Fernandez Landscaping, LLC, a domestic limited liability company. It has made no entity classification election. How is the LLC classified for federal income tax purposes, and how does Sol report its income?","d":"","o":["It is disregarded, and Sol reports the income and expenses on his own return, such as Schedule C","It is a partnership, and Sol reports his share on a Schedule K-1 from Form 1065","It is a C corporation, and Sol reports only the dividends he receives from it","It is an S corporation, and Sol reports his share on a Schedule K-1 from Form 1120-S"],"a":1,"cite":"Reg. §301.7701-3(b)(1); Reg. §301.7701-2(c)(2)","rat":"A domestic single-member LLC with no election is a disregarded entity, so its owner reports the activity directly.","ex":["A domestic single-member LLC with no election is a disregarded entity, so its owner reports the activity directly.","A partnership requires at least two members.","A corporation classification requires an election or an incorporated entity.","An S corporation requires a valid S election, which the LLC has not made."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"E","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A domestic single-member LLC with no election is disregarded, and its owner reports income on his own return.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["How is a domestic single-member LLC with no election classified?","As a disregarded entity."],["How does the owner report its income?","Directly on his own return, such as Schedule C."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-E-0002","t":"A domestic LLC with two members files Form 8832 on May 20, 2026, electing to be taxed as a corporation. It requests the earliest effective date the rules allow. What is that date?","mc":{"s":"A domestic LLC with two members files Form 8832 on May 20, 2026, electing to be taxed as a corporation. It requests the earliest effective date the rules allow. What is that date?","d":"","o":["January 1, 2026, the start of the tax year","May 20, 2026, the filing date only","March 20, 2026, which is 61 days before the filing date","March 6, 2026, which is 75 days before the filing date"],"a":4,"cite":"Reg. §301.7701-3(c)(1)(iii); Form 8832 instructions","rat":"An election may be effective up to 75 days before it is filed, and up to 12 months after.","ex":["Retroactivity is limited to 75 days, not to the beginning of the year.","The effective date may be earlier, up to 75 days before the filing date.","The rule allows 75 days, not 61 days.","An election may be effective up to 75 days before it is filed, and up to 12 months after."],"wt":["limit","limit","limit",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"E","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A Form 8832 entity classification election may be effective up to 75 days before the filing date.","from":"2026-01-01","steps":["May 20 minus 20 days = April 30","Minus 30 more days = March 31","Minus 25 more days = March 6 (total 75 days)"],"gd":[["How far back may a classification election be effective?","Up to 75 days before filing."],["What is the earliest date for a May 20, 2026 filing?","March 6, 2026."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-E-0003","t":"Harbor View Partners, LLC has three members and is taxed as a partnership. It wants to be taxed as an S corporation beginning in 2027. Which statement is correct?","mc":{"s":"Harbor View Partners, LLC has three members and is taxed as a partnership. It wants to be taxed as an S corporation beginning in 2027. Which statement is correct?","d":"","o":["It must first dissolve the LLC and organize a new corporation under state law","It can file Form 2553, and the filing is treated as an election to be classified as an association as well","It cannot make an S election, because LLCs are not eligible for S status","It must file Form 8832 and Form 2553 together, and S status is effective only after two years"],"a":2,"cite":"Reg. §301.7701-3(c)(1)(v)(C); Form 2553 instructions","rat":"A timely S election by an eligible entity is also treated as a deemed election to be taxed as a corporation.","ex":["No state law conversion is required.","A timely S election by an eligible entity is also treated as a deemed election to be taxed as a corporation.","An eligible LLC may elect S status if it meets the requirements.","Form 2553 alone is enough, and there is no two-year delay."],"wt":["concept","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"E","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A timely Form 2553 by an eligible LLC is also treated as an election to be taxed as a corporation.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Can an LLC taxed as a partnership elect S status?","Yes, if eligible, by filing Form 2553."],["What does the S election also do regarding classification?","It is treated as a deemed election to be classified as a corporation."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-E-0004","t":"Dune Ridge, LLC elected to be taxed as a corporation effective January 1, 2026. In 2028 its members want to change its classification back to a partnership. Which statement is correct?","mc":{"s":"Dune Ridge, LLC elected to be taxed as a corporation effective January 1, 2026. In 2028 its members want to change its classification back to a partnership. Which statement is correct?","d":"","o":["The change is permitted at any time by filing Form 8832","The change is not permitted after a corporation election","The change is generally not permitted before 60 months after the election's effective date","The change is permitted after 12 months by filing Form 8832"],"a":3,"cite":"Reg. §301.7701-3(c)(1)(iv); Form 8832 instructions","rat":"After a classification election, another election is generally barred for 60 months, with limited exceptions.","ex":["A 60-month limitation applies.","A new election is allowed after the 60-month period.","After a classification election, another election is generally barred for 60 months, with limited exceptions.","The waiting period is 60 months, not 12."],"wt":["concept","concept","","limit"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"E","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"After an entity classification election, another change is generally barred for 60 months.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Is a change of classification allowed soon after an election?","Generally not."],["How long is the bar?","60 months after the effective date, so 2028 is too early."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-E-0005","t":"Tess Whalen is the only member of Whalen Design, LLC, which has three employees and made no election. Which statement describes the federal tax treatment correctly?","mc":{"s":"Tess Whalen is the only member of Whalen Design, LLC, which has three employees and made no election. Which statement describes the federal tax treatment correctly?","d":"","o":["For income tax the LLC is disregarded, but for employment taxes on its employees it is treated as a separate corporation","For all federal taxes the LLC is disregarded, so Tess reports the employees' payroll taxes on her own return as an individual","The LLC is treated as a partnership for income tax and as a corporation for employment tax","The LLC is treated as a corporation for income tax and disregarded for employment tax"],"a":1,"cite":"Reg. §301.7701-2(c)(2)(iv); Form 941 instructions","rat":"A single-member LLC is disregarded for income tax, but it is treated as a corporation for employment tax purposes regarding its employees.","ex":["A single-member LLC is disregarded for income tax, but it is treated as a corporation for employment tax purposes regarding its employees.","Employment taxes for wages paid to employees are the entity's responsibility.","A single-member LLC cannot be a partnership.","The rule is the reverse: disregarded for income tax and a corporation for employment tax."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"E","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A single-member LLC is disregarded for income tax but treated as a corporation for employment taxes on its employees.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["How is a single-member LLC treated for income tax?","Disregarded."],["How is it treated for employment taxes on its employees?","As a separate corporation."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-E-1001","t":"Quinn Dawson and Rex Holt are equal members of an LLC that expects $400,000 of net income in 2026 and will distribute all of its after-tax income. If it keeps its default classification, each member pays 35% on his share. If it elects to be taxed as a corporation, it pays 21% and each member pays 20% on the dividends he receives. Ignore the qualified business income deduction, self-employment tax, net investment income tax and state taxes. Which structure produces less total federal income tax, and by how much?","mc":{"s":"Quinn Dawson and Rex Holt are equal members of an LLC that expects $400,000 of net income in 2026 and will distribute all of its after-tax income. If it keeps its default classification, each member pays 35% on his share. If it elects to be taxed as a corporation, it pays 21% and each member pays 20% on the dividends he receives. Ignore the qualified business income deduction, self-employment tax, net investment income tax and state taxes. Which structure produces less total federal income tax, and by how much?","d":"","o":["The corporate election, by $7,200","The corporate election, by $56,000","The default partnership classification, by $7,200","The default partnership classification, by $63,200"],"a":3,"cite":"Reg. §301.7701-3(b)(1)(i); IRC §11(b); IRC §701; IRC §1(h)(11)","rat":"Partnership: 35% x $400,000 = $140,000.","ex":["Reverses the comparison. The dividend tax on the after-tax income more than offsets the lower 21% corporate rate.","Compares only the 35% individual rate with the 21% corporate rate and ignores the second tax on the dividends.","Partnership: 35% x $400,000 = $140,000. Corporation: $84,000 of corporate tax plus 20% x $316,000 = $63,200 of dividend tax = $147,200. The partnership is $7,200 lower.","Uses only the dividend tax as the difference. The corporation's $84,000 of tax is also part of the comparison."],"wt":["concept","concept","","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"E","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Corporate classification adds a second layer of tax on distributed income, so a 21% corporate rate does not beat a 35% pass-through rate once dividends are taxed.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Partnership: 35% x 400,000 = 140,000","Corporation: 21% x 400,000 = 84,000; dividends 400,000 - 84,000 = 316,000 x 20% = 63,200; total 147,200","147,200 - 140,000 = 7,200, so the partnership costs less"],"gd":[["What is the tax under the default partnership classification?","35% x 400,000 = 140,000."],["What is the tax with a corporate election?","21% x 400,000 = 84,000, then dividends of 316,000 x 20% = 63,200, total 147,200."],["Which is lower and by how much?","147,200 - 140,000 = 7,200, so the partnership costs less."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-E-1002","t":"Tri-Lakes Holdings, LLC has two equal members and is classified as a partnership. Its assets have an adjusted basis of $160,000 and a fair market value of $400,000, and it has $200,000 of business liabilities. Effective January 1, 2027, it files Form 8832 to be taxed as a corporation. Treat the deemed transactions as the partnership contributing its assets and liabilities to a new corporation for stock and then liquidating. How much gain is recognized on the deemed contribution, and what is the corporation's aggregate basis in the assets?","mc":{"s":"Tri-Lakes Holdings, LLC has two equal members and is classified as a partnership. Its assets have an adjusted basis of $160,000 and a fair market value of $400,000, and it has $200,000 of business liabilities. Effective January 1, 2027, it files Form 8832 to be taxed as a corporation. Treat the deemed transactions as the partnership contributing its assets and liabilities to a new corporation for stock and then liquidating. How much gain is recognized on the deemed contribution, and what is the corporation's aggregate basis in the assets?","d":"","o":["No gain and a $160,000 basis","$40,000 of gain and a $200,000 basis","$240,000 of gain and a $400,000 basis","$40,000 of gain and a $160,000 basis"],"a":2,"cite":"Reg. §301.7701-3(g)(1)(i); IRC §351(a); IRC §357(c)(1); IRC §362(a)","rat":"The liabilities assumed ($200,000) exceed the assets' basis ($160,000), so section 357(c) triggers $40,000 of gain.","ex":["Assumes the deemed transfer is fully tax-free. When liabilities exceed basis, the excess is gain.","The liabilities assumed ($200,000) exceed the assets' basis ($160,000), so section 357(c) triggers $40,000 of gain. The corporation's basis is the $160,000 carryover plus the $40,000 gain: $200,000.","Taxes the transfer as a sale at fair market value. Section 351 still applies; only the liability excess is gain.","Recognizes the gain but forgets that recognized gain increases the corporation's carryover basis."],"wt":["concept","","concept","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"E","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"In the deemed assets-over conversion of a partnership to a corporation, liabilities exceeding asset basis are taxable gain under section 357(c).","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Liabilities 200,000 - basis 160,000 = 40,000 gain","Corporation basis: 160,000 + 40,000 = 200,000"],"gd":[["What happens when liabilities exceed asset basis on the deemed contribution?","Section 357(c) creates gain."],["What is the gain?","200,000 - 160,000 = 40,000."],["What is the corporation's basis?","160,000 + 40,000 = 200,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-E-1003","t":"Ivy Marsh owns 100% of Marsh Equipment, LLC, which has made no election, and Pat buys her entire membership interest. Jo Kim owns 50% of Kim & Lee, LLC, which is taxed as a partnership, and Lou buys Jo's entire 50% interest for $45,000. Each LLC's only asset is equipment with an adjusted basis of $30,000 and a fair market value of $90,000, and no election to adjust the basis of partnership assets is in effect. Which statement about the buyers' basis in the equipment is correct?","mc":{"s":"Ivy Marsh owns 100% of Marsh Equipment, LLC, which has made no election, and Pat buys her entire membership interest. Jo Kim owns 50% of Kim & Lee, LLC, which is taxed as a partnership, and Lou buys Jo's entire 50% interest for $45,000. Each LLC's only asset is equipment with an adjusted basis of $30,000 and a fair market value of $90,000, and no election to adjust the basis of partnership assets is in effect. Which statement about the buyers' basis in the equipment is correct?","d":"","o":["Pat's basis in the equipment is $90,000, and Lou's share of the partnership's basis in the equipment is $15,000","Pat's basis in the equipment is $30,000, and Lou's share of the partnership's basis is $45,000","Pat's basis in the equipment is $90,000, and Lou's share of the partnership's basis is $45,000","Pat's basis in the equipment is $30,000, and Lou's share of the partnership's basis is $15,000"],"a":1,"cite":"Rev. Rul. 99-5; IRC §743(a); IRC §754; Reg. §301.7701-3(b)(1)(ii)","rat":"Buying all of a single-member LLC is treated as buying its assets, so Pat takes a cost basis of $90,000.","ex":["Buying all of a single-member LLC is treated as buying its assets, so Pat takes a cost basis of $90,000. Lou buys a partnership interest; without a section 754 election the partnership's inside basis is unchanged, so his share is 50% x $30,000 = $15,000.","Reverses the treatment. The single-member LLC purchase is an asset purchase, and a partnership interest purchase does not change inside basis without an election.","Gives Lou a cost basis inside the partnership. His $45,000 cost is his outside basis in the interest, not the partnership's basis in the equipment.","Treats Pat as buying stock-like interest with carryover basis. A disregarded entity's owner is treated as owning the assets directly, so Pat buys the assets."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"E","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Buying all of a disregarded LLC is an asset purchase with cost basis, but buying a partnership interest leaves inside asset basis unchanged unless a section 754 election is made.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Single-member LLC: asset purchase, basis = 90,000 (cost)","Partnership interest: outside basis 45,000; inside share 50% x 30,000 = 15,000 without section 754"],"gd":[["How is buying all of a single-member LLC treated?","As buying its assets at cost, 90,000."],["How is buying a partnership interest treated without a section 754 election?","Inside basis is unchanged, so the buyer's share is 50% x 30,000 = 15,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-E-1004","t":"Baywater Advisors, LLC has three members and made no Form 8832 election, so it has been taxed as a partnership. One member is Halo Ventures, LLC, which is itself classified as a partnership. The other two members are U.S. individuals. On February 1, 2026, Baywater timely filed Form 2553 asking to be an S corporation effective January 1, 2026, and did not file Form 8832. Which statement is correct for 2026?","mc":{"s":"Baywater Advisors, LLC has three members and made no Form 8832 election, so it has been taxed as a partnership. One member is Halo Ventures, LLC, which is itself classified as a partnership. The other two members are U.S. individuals. On February 1, 2026, Baywater timely filed Form 2553 asking to be an S corporation effective January 1, 2026, and did not file Form 8832. Which statement is correct for 2026?","d":"","o":["The deemed association election takes effect anyway, so Baywater is a C corporation for 2026 and files Form 1120","The S election is valid because an LLC member is treated as a corporation for S corporation eligibility purposes","Baywater is an S corporation, but Halo Ventures' interest is treated as a second class of stock","The S election is invalid because a partnership is not an eligible shareholder, no association election is deemed made, and Baywater remains a partnership that files Form 1065"],"a":4,"cite":"Reg. §301.7701-3(c)(1)(v)(C); IRC §1361(b)(1)(B); IRC §1362(a)","rat":"Filing Form 2553 is a deemed election to be an association only if the entity otherwise meets the small business corporation requirements.","ex":["The deemed association election is made only if the entity meets all other requirements to be a small business corporation as of the effective date.","A partnership, even one that is an LLC, is not a permitted S corporation shareholder.","Ineligible shareholders do not create a second class of stock; they make the corporation ineligible.","Filing Form 2553 is a deemed election to be an association only if the entity otherwise meets the small business corporation requirements. With a partnership member it does not, so nothing changes."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"E","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A timely Form 2553 from an LLC is a deemed association election only if the entity already meets the small business corporation rules, such as having eligible shareholders.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["When is a Form 2553 also a deemed election to be a corporation?","Only if the entity otherwise meets the small business corporation requirements."],["Does a partnership member meet them?","No, a partnership is not an eligible shareholder, so the S election is invalid and Baywater stays a partnership filing Form 1065."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-E-1005","t":"Redwood Supply, LLC was formed in 2020 with two members and was taxed as a partnership until it filed Form 8832 electing to be taxed as a corporation effective January 1, 2024. In 2026 the members ask two questions. (1) Can the LLC elect S status effective January 1, 2026, by filing Form 2553 by the March 2026 due date? (2) Can it file Form 8832 effective January 1, 2026, to go back to partnership classification? Assume all S corporation requirements are met and the IRS has granted no relief. Which statement is correct?","mc":{"s":"Redwood Supply, LLC was formed in 2020 with two members and was taxed as a partnership until it filed Form 8832 electing to be taxed as a corporation effective January 1, 2024. In 2026 the members ask two questions. (1) Can the LLC elect S status effective January 1, 2026, by filing Form 2553 by the March 2026 due date? (2) Can it file Form 8832 effective January 1, 2026, to go back to partnership classification? Assume all S corporation requirements are met and the IRS has granted no relief. Which statement is correct?","d":"","o":["(1) Yes; (2) Yes, because the 60-month limit applies only to a newly formed entity's first election","(1) No, because an S election is a change in classification barred until 2029; (2) No","(1) Yes, it can file Form 2553 as a corporation without a new Form 8832; (2) No, a change in classification is barred until January 1, 2029","(1) No, because an LLC taxed as a corporation cannot make an S election; (2) Yes"],"a":3,"cite":"Reg. §301.7701-3(c)(1)(iv); IRC §1362(b); Reg. §301.7701-3(c)(1)(v)(C)","rat":"An entity already classified as a corporation may elect S status without changing its classification.","ex":["The 60-month limit applies to any change in classification after an election, not only the first one.","An S election by an entity already classified as a corporation is not a change in classification, so the 60-month rule does not block it.","An entity already classified as a corporation may elect S status without changing its classification. A change of classification by election cannot occur during the 60 months after the effective date of the last election (January 1, 2024 plus 60 months).","An LLC taxed as a corporation can elect S status, and returning to partnership status is barred for 60 months."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"E","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"An LLC already taxed as a corporation can elect S status freely, but a further change of classification by election is barred for 60 months.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["March 15, 2026 is a Sunday (weekday 6), so the due date moves to Monday, March 16","Election effective 1/1/2024 + 60 months: earliest change 1/1/2029"],"gd":[["Can an entity already classified as a corporation elect S status?","Yes, by Form 2553 without a new Form 8832."],["How long is a classification change barred after the last election?","60 months from the January 1, 2024 effective date."],["When is the earliest change back to partnership?","January 1, 2029, so the 2026 change is barred."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-E-1006","t":"Gray & Pine, LLC has two equal members, Abel and Bea, and is taxed as a partnership. Its assets have an adjusted basis of $160,000 and a fair market value of $400,000, and it has no liabilities. Abel's basis in his interest is $80,000. On July 1, 2026, Abel buys Bea's entire interest for $200,000 in cash, so the LLC has one member and becomes a disregarded entity. What is Abel's total basis in the LLC's assets after the purchase?","mc":{"s":"Gray & Pine, LLC has two equal members, Abel and Bea, and is taxed as a partnership. Its assets have an adjusted basis of $160,000 and a fair market value of $400,000, and it has no liabilities. Abel's basis in his interest is $80,000. On July 1, 2026, Abel buys Bea's entire interest for $200,000 in cash, so the LLC has one member and becomes a disregarded entity. What is Abel's total basis in the LLC's assets after the purchase?","d":"","o":["$160,000","$200,000","$280,000","$400,000"],"a":3,"cite":"Rev. Rul. 99-6; IRC §732(b); IRC §1012","rat":"Abel is treated as buying the assets attributable to Bea's interest for $200,000, and as receiving the rest in liquidation of the partnership with a basis equal to his $80,000 outside basis.","ex":["Uses the partnership's inside basis and ignores the purchase price paid for Bea's half.","Counts only the cost of the purchased half and forgets the $80,000 basis attributable to Abel's original half.","Abel is treated as buying the assets attributable to Bea's interest for $200,000, and as receiving the rest in liquidation of the partnership with a basis equal to his $80,000 outside basis. Total $280,000.","Uses fair market value. Only the purchased portion gets a cost basis; Abel's own portion carries his outside basis."],"wt":["calc","calc","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"E","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"When one member buys out the other and the partnership becomes disregarded, the buyer takes cost basis in the purchased half and his outside basis in the rest.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Purchased half: cost 200,000","Own half: outside basis 80,000 (liquidating distribution)","Total 280,000"],"gd":[["How is Abel's purchase of Bea's interest treated?","As buying the assets attributable to her interest, at cost 200,000."],["What basis does his own half take on liquidation?","His outside basis, 80,000."],["What is the total?","200,000 + 80,000 = 280,000."]]}}],"14":[{"i":"REG-V-F-0001","t":"Which situation would disqualify a charitable organization from tax exemption?","mc":{"s":"Which situation would disqualify a charitable organization from tax exemption?","d":"","o":["It spends a small portion of its budget on lobbying that is not substantial","It charges reasonable fees for the educational programs it offers","It pays reasonable salaries to its employees for their services","A portion of its net earnings benefits its founder, who is a private individual"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §501(c)(3); Reg. §1.501(c)(3)-1(c)","rat":"Exemption requires that no part of net earnings inure to the benefit of a private shareholder or individual.","ex":["Insubstantial lobbying does not disqualify a charitable organization.","Reasonable fees for exempt activities are permitted.","Reasonable compensation is not private inurement.","Exemption requires that no part of net earnings inure to the benefit of a private shareholder or individual."],"wt":["trap","trap","trap",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"F","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Exemption is lost if any net earnings inure to a private individual; insubstantial lobbying, reasonable fees and reasonable salaries are permitted.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["What must be true of net earnings of an exempt organization?","None may inure to a private shareholder or individual."],["Which listed situation breaks that?","Part of net earnings benefiting the founder; reasonable fees, salaries and insubstantial lobbying are fine."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-F-0002","t":"Skyline Business Alliance is a local chamber of commerce that promotes area businesses and is not organized for profit. Under which type of exempt organization does it usually qualify?","mc":{"s":"Skyline Business Alliance is a local chamber of commerce that promotes area businesses and is not organized for profit. Under which type of exempt organization does it usually qualify?","d":"","o":["A social welfare organization","A business league","A charitable organization","A social club"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §501(c)(6)","rat":"Chambers of commerce and business leagues are exempt organizations of a type described in the business league category.","ex":["That category covers civic groups and organizations that promote the community's welfare, not business leagues.","Chambers of commerce and business leagues are exempt organizations of a type described in the business league category.","A charity serves a broad public purpose, not the common business interests of members.","A social club is organized for pleasure and recreation of its members."],"wt":["concept","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"F","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Chambers of commerce and similar groups promoting common business interests qualify as business leagues.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Which exempt category covers chambers of commerce?","Business leagues."],["Are charities, social clubs and social welfare groups the type?","No, the chamber promotes area businesses as a business league."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-F-0003","t":"United Tradesworkers Local 42 is a labor union organized to improve the working conditions of its members. Under which type of exempt organization does it qualify?","mc":{"s":"United Tradesworkers Local 42 is a labor union organized to improve the working conditions of its members. Under which type of exempt organization does it qualify?","d":"","o":["A business league","A charitable organization","A labor, agricultural or horticultural organization","A social welfare organization"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §501(c)(5)","rat":"Labor unions are described in the category for labor, agricultural and horticultural organizations.","ex":["Business leagues represent common business interests, not workers.","A union is not organized for charitable purposes.","Labor unions are described in the category for labor, agricultural and horticultural organizations.","A union's exemption is under its own labor category."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"F","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Labor unions qualify as labor, agricultural or horticultural organizations.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Which category describes labor unions?","Labor, agricultural or horticultural organizations."],["Do business leagues or social welfare groups fit?","No."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-F-1002","t":"Meadow Health Trust is a tax-exempt charity that is not a church or a hospital. Its total support for the test period was $1,000,000: $500,000 from one donor, Mr. Lang; $150,000 from many small donors, none giving more than 2% of total support; $100,000 in government grants; and $250,000 of investment income. Which statement is correct?","mc":{"s":"Meadow Health Trust is a tax-exempt charity that is not a church or a hospital. Its total support for the test period was $1,000,000: $500,000 from one donor, Mr. Lang; $150,000 from many small donors, none giving more than 2% of total support; $100,000 in government grants; and $250,000 of investment income. Which statement is correct?","d":"","o":["It passes the one-third test at 75%, because all contributions from Mr. Lang count as public support","It fails the one-third public support test at 27%, but it may still qualify as publicly supported under the 10% facts-and-circumstances test","It fails the one-third test at 27% and must be a private foundation, because no other public charity test is available","It passes the one-third test at 50%, because Mr. Lang's contributions are disregarded entirely"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §170(b)(1)(A)(vi); Reg. §1.170A-9(f)(3),(f)(4); IRC §509(a)(1)","rat":"Public support counts small donors ($150,000), government grants ($100,000) and only 2% of total support ($20,000) from Mr.","ex":["Contributions from one donor count as public support only up to 2% of total support, although the excess stays in total support.","Public support counts small donors ($150,000), government grants ($100,000) and only 2% of total support ($20,000) from Mr. Lang: $270,000 / $1,000,000 = 27%. That is below 33 1/3%, but at least 10% allows the facts-and-circumstances test.","A second route exists for organizations with at least 10% public support that meet the facts-and-circumstances test.","The excess over 2% is excluded from public support but remains in total support (the denominator)."],"wt":["concept","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"F","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Under the one-third support test a large donor counts only up to 2% of total support in the numerator but in full in the denominator, and a 10% floor opens the facts-and-circumstances test.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Public support: 150,000 + 100,000 + 2% x 1,000,000 (20,000) = 270,000","270,000 / 1,000,000 = 27% < 33 1/3%","27% >= 10%, so the facts-and-circumstances test is available"],"gd":[["What counts as public support?","Small donors 150,000, government grants 100,000, and only 2% of total support from the large donor, 20,000."],["What is the public support percentage?","270,000 / 1,000,000 = 27%, below 33 1/3%."],["What alternative test is available?","At least 10% allows the facts-and-circumstances test."]]}}],"15":[{"i":"REG-V-F-0004","t":"Meadowlark Museum, a tax-exempt charity, operates an unrelated business regularly. In 2026 the business had gross income of $90,000 and directly connected expenses of $50,000. What is its unrelated business taxable income after the standard specific deduction of $1,000?","mc":{"s":"Meadowlark Museum, a tax-exempt charity, operates an unrelated business regularly. In 2026 the business had gross income of $90,000 and directly connected expenses of $50,000. What is its unrelated business taxable income after the standard specific deduction of $1,000?","d":"","o":["$39,000","$40,000","$89,000","$90,000"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §512(a); IRC §512(b)(12); Form 990-T instructions","rat":"UBTI is gross unrelated business income less directly connected expenses and the $1,000 specific deduction: $39,000.","ex":["UBTI is gross unrelated business income less directly connected expenses and the $1,000 specific deduction: $39,000.","Omits the $1,000 specific deduction.","Omits the directly connected expenses.","Uses gross unrelated income as UBTI."],"wt":["","calc","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"F","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"UBTI is gross unrelated business income minus directly connected expenses and the $1,000 specific deduction.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["90,000 - 50,000 = 40,000","40,000 - 1,000 = 39,000"],"gd":[["What is UBTI before the specific deduction?","90,000 - 50,000 = 40,000."],["What is the specific deduction and the result?","1,000, so 40,000 - 1,000 = 39,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-F-0005","t":"Silver Lake Foundation, a tax-exempt organization, had $1,500 of gross income from an unrelated trade or business in 2026. What return filing obligation results?","mc":{"s":"Silver Lake Foundation, a tax-exempt organization, had $1,500 of gross income from an unrelated trade or business in 2026. What return filing obligation results?","d":"","o":["It must file Form 990-T, because gross unrelated business income is at least $1,000","It need not file any return, because unrelated income under $5,000 is ignored","It must file Form 1120 instead of Form 990-T","It must file Form 990-T only if the unrelated income exceeds its exempt income"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §6012(a)(2); Form 990-T instructions; Reg §1.6012-2(e)","rat":"An exempt organization with gross unrelated business income of $1,000 or more must file Form 990-T.","ex":["An exempt organization with gross unrelated business income of $1,000 or more must file Form 990-T.","There is no $5,000 exemption; the threshold is $1,000.","Form 990-T is the return for unrelated business income.","The filing threshold is $1,000 of gross unrelated income; exempt income does not matter."],"wt":["","limit","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"F","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"An exempt organization with $1,000 or more of gross unrelated business income must file Form 990-T.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["What is the gross unrelated income filing threshold?","1,000."],["Does 1,500 meet it and which form applies?","Yes, so Form 990-T must be filed."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-F-0006","t":"Arbor Youth Services, a public charity, had gross receipts of $150,000 and total assets of $300,000 at the end of 2026. It is not a private foundation and has no other unusual attributes. Which annual information return may it file?","mc":{"s":"Arbor Youth Services, a public charity, had gross receipts of $150,000 and total assets of $300,000 at the end of 2026. It is not a private foundation and has no other unusual attributes. Which annual information return may it file?","d":"","o":["Form 990-N (e-Postcard), because it is a small organization","Form 990-PF, because it holds assets for charitable purposes","Form 990 only, because every charity must file the long form","Form 990-EZ, because gross receipts are under $200,000 and total assets are under $500,000"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §6033; Form 990-EZ instructions","rat":"An organization with gross receipts under $200,000 and total assets under $500,000 may file Form 990-EZ instead of Form 990.","ex":["The e-Postcard is for organizations with gross receipts normally of $50,000 or less.","Form 990-PF is filed by private foundations, and Arbor is a public charity.","Form 990-EZ is allowed below the thresholds.","An organization with gross receipts under $200,000 and total assets under $500,000 may file Form 990-EZ instead of Form 990."],"wt":["limit","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"F","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Organizations with gross receipts under $200,000 and total assets under $500,000 may file Form 990-EZ.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["What are the size limits for the short annual information return?","Gross receipts under 200,000 and total assets under 500,000."],["Does Arbor meet both and which form results?","Yes, 150,000 and 300,000, so Form 990-EZ."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-F-0007","t":"Two exempt organizations each sell used goods to the public. Haven Thrift Shop sells only goods donated to it and is staffed entirely by volunteers. Crest Resale purchases new goods from wholesalers and sells them through paid staff. Which statement is correct?","mc":{"s":"Two exempt organizations each sell used goods to the public. Haven Thrift Shop sells only goods donated to it and is staffed entirely by volunteers. Crest Resale purchases new goods from wholesalers and sells them through paid staff. Which statement is correct?","d":"","o":["Both organizations' sales are unrelated business income","Haven's sales are not unrelated business income, but Crest's sales generally are","Neither organization's sales are unrelated business income, because they are tax-exempt","Crest's sales are excluded and Haven's sales are unrelated business income"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §513(a)(1); IRC §513(a)(3)","rat":"Sales of donated merchandise or activity conducted by volunteers are excluded from unrelated business income; Crest's regularly conducted sales of purchased goods are unrelated.","ex":["The volunteer and donated merchandise exceptions exclude Haven's income.","Sales of donated merchandise or activity conducted by volunteers are excluded from unrelated business income; Crest's regularly conducted sales of purchased goods are unrelated.","Exempt organizations are taxed on income from regularly carried on trades or businesses unrelated to their purposes.","The results are reversed."],"wt":["concept","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"F","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Sales of donated goods by volunteers are excluded from UBTI, but regular sales of purchased goods by paid staff are unrelated business income.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Which activities are excluded from unrelated business income?","Sales of donated merchandise and activities run by volunteers."],["How do the two shops compare?","Haven sells donated goods with volunteers and is excluded; Crest buys goods and uses paid staff, so its sales are generally unrelated."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-F-0008","t":"Reviewing Pine Barrens Society's draft Form 990-T, a CPA sees gross unrelated business income of $80,000 that includes $10,000 of dividends from a stock portfolio, directly connected expenses of $45,000, no specific deduction, and UBTI of $35,000. Which correction is needed?","mc":{"s":"Reviewing Pine Barrens Society's draft Form 990-T, a CPA sees gross unrelated business income of $80,000 that includes $10,000 of dividends from a stock portfolio, directly connected expenses of $45,000, no specific deduction, and UBTI of $35,000. Which correction is needed?","d":"","o":["Keep the dividends but claim the $1,000 specific deduction, making UBTI $34,000","Exclude the dividends but no specific deduction is allowed, making UBTI $25,000","Exclude the $10,000 of dividends and claim the $1,000 specific deduction, making UBTI $24,000","Add the $10,000 of dividends to directly connected expenses, making UBTI $25,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §512(b)(1); IRC §512(b)(12); Form 990-T instructions","rat":"Dividends are excluded from unrelated business income, and the $1,000 specific deduction is available: $70,000 - $45,000 - $1,000 = $24,000.","ex":["Dividends are excluded passive income, so they should come out.","The specific deduction is available to the organization.","Dividends are excluded from unrelated business income, and the $1,000 specific deduction is available: $70,000 - $45,000 - $1,000 = $24,000.","Dividends are not expenses; they are excluded from income."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"F","skill":"Application","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Dividends are excluded from UBTI, and the $1,000 specific deduction applies.","tb":[["Draft Form 990-T","Amount"],["Gross unrelated business income (includes $10,000 of dividends)","$80,000"],["Directly connected expenses","($45,000)"],["Specific deduction","$0"],["Unrelated business taxable income","$35,000"]],"from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Gross income excluding dividends = 80,000 - 10,000 = 70,000","70,000 - 45,000 - 1,000 = 24,000"],"gd":[["Are dividends part of unrelated business income?","No, they are excluded."],["What is gross income after that?","80,000 - 10,000 = 70,000."],["What is UBTI after expenses and the specific deduction?","70,000 - 45,000 - 1,000 = 24,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-F-1001","t":"Harbor Arts Society, a tax-exempt charity, had these 2026 items: $30,000 of net income from advertising in its magazine that is unrelated to its exempt purpose and regularly carried on; $40,000 of rent from a building it owns, with $15,000 of expenses attributable to the building; $10,000 of interest and dividends; and a $50,000 gain from selling donated stock. The building was bought with a mortgage, and the average acquisition indebtedness for 2026 was 50% of the building's average adjusted basis. It claims the $1,000 specific deduction. What is its unrelated business taxable income?","mc":{"s":"Harbor Arts Society, a tax-exempt charity, had these 2026 items: $30,000 of net income from advertising in its magazine that is unrelated to its exempt purpose and regularly carried on; $40,000 of rent from a building it owns, with $15,000 of expenses attributable to the building; $10,000 of interest and dividends; and a $50,000 gain from selling donated stock. The building was bought with a mortgage, and the average acquisition indebtedness for 2026 was 50% of the building's average adjusted basis. It claims the $1,000 specific deduction. What is its unrelated business taxable income?","d":"","o":["$29,000","$41,500","$49,000","$54,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §512(a)(1); IRC §512(b)(1),(3),(5),(12); IRC §514(a)(1),(c)","rat":"Advertising income of $30,000 is unrelated.","ex":["Leaves out the debt-financed rent. Rent from property bought with acquisition indebtedness is taxed in proportion to the debt.","Advertising income of $30,000 is unrelated. Rent is normally excluded, but the debt-financed 50% of the net rent ($25,000 x 50% = $12,500) is included. Interest, dividends and gain on donated stock are excluded. $30,000 + $12,500 - $1,000 = $41,500.","Applies the debt percentage to gross rent instead of net rent after the related $15,000 of expenses.","Includes all of the net rent. Only the debt-financed percentage (50%) is included."],"wt":["calc","","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"F","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Rent, interest, dividends and gain on donated property are excluded from UBTI, except that debt-financed property income is included in proportion to the debt.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Advertising: 30,000","Debt-financed net rent: (40,000 - 15,000) x 50% = 12,500","Interest, dividends, gain on donated stock: excluded","30,000 + 12,500 - 1,000 = 41,500"],"gd":[["Which items are unrelated business income?","Advertising income of 30,000 and the debt-financed part of the net rent."],["What is the debt-financed part?","(40,000 - 15,000) x 50% = 12,500, while interest, dividends and gain on donated stock are excluded."],["What is UBTI?","30,000 + 12,500 - 1,000 = 41,500."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-F-1003","t":"Lakemont Museum, a tax-exempt charity, has two unrelated trades or businesses in 2026, each regularly carried on. Its parking business had net income of $30,000, and its catering business had a net loss of $45,000. It claims the $1,000 specific deduction. Which statement is correct for 2026?","mc":{"s":"Lakemont Museum, a tax-exempt charity, has two unrelated trades or businesses in 2026, each regularly carried on. Its parking business had net income of $30,000, and its catering business had a net loss of $45,000. It claims the $1,000 specific deduction. Which statement is correct for 2026?","d":"","o":["Unrelated business taxable income is $29,000, and the $45,000 catering loss carries forward to offset only future catering income","Unrelated business taxable income is $0, and a $15,000 net loss carries forward","Unrelated business taxable income is $30,000, because the specific deduction is available only for a single trade or business","Unrelated business taxable income is $29,000, and the $45,000 loss carries forward against any future unrelated business income from any activity"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §512(a)(6); IRC §512(b)(12); Reg. §1.512(a)-6","rat":"Unrelated business taxable income is computed separately for each trade or business, so the catering loss cannot offset the parking income.","ex":["Unrelated business taxable income is computed separately for each trade or business, so the catering loss cannot offset the parking income. The $30,000 less the $1,000 specific deduction is taxable, and the loss carries forward within the catering business.","Nets the two activities, which was the rule before 2018. Losses now stay with their own business.","The $1,000 specific deduction applies to the organization's total unrelated business income.","The carryforward applies only against income of the same trade or business."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"F","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Unrelated business income is computed separately for each activity, so a loss in one business cannot offset income from another.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Parking: 30,000; catering loss cannot offset it","30,000 - 1,000 specific deduction = 29,000","Catering loss of 45,000 carries forward against catering income only"],"gd":[["Is UBTI computed separately for each unrelated business?","Yes."],["What is taxable from the parking business?","30,000 - 1,000 = 29,000, and the catering loss cannot offset it."],["Where does the 45,000 loss go?","It carries forward against catering income only."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-F-1004","t":"Coastal Land Trust, a tax-exempt charity, is a limited partner in three partnerships. Its 2026 Schedules K-1 show: Partnership 1 (operates a restaurant), $60,000 of ordinary business income; Partnership 2 (owns rental buildings with no debt), $40,000 of rental real estate income; and Partnership 3 (an investment fund), $20,000 of interest, $10,000 of dividends and $15,000 of long-term capital gain. Coastal has no other income and claims the $1,000 specific deduction. What is its unrelated business taxable income?","mc":{"s":"Coastal Land Trust, a tax-exempt charity, is a limited partner in three partnerships. Its 2026 Schedules K-1 show: Partnership 1 (operates a restaurant), $60,000 of ordinary business income; Partnership 2 (owns rental buildings with no debt), $40,000 of rental real estate income; and Partnership 3 (an investment fund), $20,000 of interest, $10,000 of dividends and $15,000 of long-term capital gain. Coastal has no other income and claims the $1,000 specific deduction. What is its unrelated business taxable income?","d":"","o":["$0","$59,000","$99,000","$144,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §512(c)(1); IRC §512(b)(1),(3),(5); Reg. §1.512(c)-1","rat":"A partner's share of partnership income keeps its character.","ex":["Treats limited partner status as making all K-1 income passive and excluded. The restaurant's ordinary business income flows through as unrelated business income.","A partner's share of partnership income keeps its character. The restaurant income is unrelated business income ($60,000). Rent from unleveraged real property, interest, dividends and capital gain are excluded. $60,000 - $1,000 = $59,000.","Includes the unleveraged rent, which is excluded from unrelated business income.","Includes every K-1 item. The rent, interest, dividends and capital gain are modifications that exclude them."],"wt":["concept","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"F","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A tax-exempt partner's share of a partnership's income keeps its character, so only the operating business income is UBTI.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Restaurant ordinary income: 60,000 (UBTI)","Unleveraged rent, interest, dividends, capital gain: excluded","60,000 - 1,000 = 59,000"],"gd":[["How does partnership income keep its character?","Through the partner's share."],["Which K-1 item is unrelated business income?","The restaurant's 60,000; unleveraged rent, interest, dividends and capital gain are excluded."],["What is UBTI?","60,000 - 1,000 = 59,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-F-1005","t":"Two organizations each have, in 2026, $20,000 of net income from an unrelated business regularly carried on and $15,000 of interest on savings, and each claims the $1,000 specific deduction. Pine Hollow Club is a tax-exempt social club, and the business income is not from members. Cedar Aid Foundation is a tax-exempt charity. What is each organization's unrelated business taxable income?","mc":{"s":"Two organizations each have, in 2026, $20,000 of net income from an unrelated business regularly carried on and $15,000 of interest on savings, and each claims the $1,000 specific deduction. Pine Hollow Club is a tax-exempt social club, and the business income is not from members. Cedar Aid Foundation is a tax-exempt charity. What is each organization's unrelated business taxable income?","d":"","o":["The club's is $19,000, and the foundation's is $19,000","The club's is $34,000, and the foundation's is $34,000","The club's is $19,000, and the foundation's is $34,000","The club's is $34,000, and the foundation's is $19,000"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §512(a)(3)(A),(B); IRC §512(b)(1); IRC §512(b)(12)","rat":"A social club's investment income is included in unrelated business income, so the club has $20,000 + $15,000 - $1,000 = $34,000.","ex":["Excludes the club's interest. Social clubs, unlike charities, are taxed on investment income.","Includes the interest for the charity, for which interest is excluded.","Reverses the two. The club is the one whose investment income is taxable.","A social club's investment income is included in unrelated business income, so the club has $20,000 + $15,000 - $1,000 = $34,000. A charity's interest is excluded, so the foundation has $20,000 - $1,000 = $19,000."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"F","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A social club's investment income is unrelated business income, while a charity's interest and dividends are excluded.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Club: 20,000 + 15,000 - 1,000 = 34,000","Foundation: 20,000 - 1,000 = 19,000"],"gd":[["Is a social club's investment income excluded?","No, it is included in unrelated business income."],["What is the club's UBTI?","20,000 + 15,000 - 1,000 = 34,000."],["What is the charity's UBTI?","Interest is excluded, so 20,000 - 1,000 = 19,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-F-1006","t":"Sunridge Historical Society, a tax-exempt charity, has two leases in 2026. Lease X is of a building and equipment to a tenant for a fixed rent of $96,000, of which $6,000 is for the equipment. Lease Y is of a building and equipment to another tenant for a fixed rent of $100,000, of which $20,000 is for the equipment. None of the property is debt-financed, and the rents do not depend on the tenants' income. Ignoring expenses and assuming the $1,000 specific deduction, what is the society's unrelated business taxable income from the leases?","mc":{"s":"Sunridge Historical Society, a tax-exempt charity, has two leases in 2026. Lease X is of a building and equipment to a tenant for a fixed rent of $96,000, of which $6,000 is for the equipment. Lease Y is of a building and equipment to another tenant for a fixed rent of $100,000, of which $20,000 is for the equipment. None of the property is debt-financed, and the rents do not depend on the tenants' income. Ignoring expenses and assuming the $1,000 specific deduction, what is the society's unrelated business taxable income from the leases?","d":"","o":["$0","$19,000","$25,000","$99,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §512(b)(3)(A),(B); Reg. §1.512(b)-1(c)(2)","rat":"Lease X: personal property rent is 6.25% of the total (10% or less), so all rent is excluded.","ex":["Treats all rent as excluded real property rent. Rent for personal property above the 10% incidental level is included.","Lease X: personal property rent is 6.25% of the total (10% or less), so all rent is excluded. Lease Y: personal property rent is 20% (more than 10% but not more than 50%), so the $20,000 for equipment is unrelated business income. $20,000 - $1,000 = $19,000.","Also includes the equipment rent in Lease X, which is only 6.25% of that lease and so is treated as incidental.","Includes all of Lease Y. That happens only if more than 50% of the rent is for personal property."],"wt":["concept","","calc","limit"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"F","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Rent for personal property leased with real property is excluded if it is 10% or less of total rent; above 10% the personal-property rent is included, and above 50% all of the rent is included.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Lease X: 6,000 / 96,000 = 6.25% <= 10%, all excluded","Lease Y: 20,000 / 100,000 = 20% > 10% and <= 50%, so the 20,000 is included","20,000 - 1,000 = 19,000"],"gd":[["What share of rent is for personal property in Lease X?","6,000 / 96,000 = 6.25%, at or below 10%, so all rent is excluded."],["What about Lease Y?","20,000 / 100,000 = 20%, above 10% and up to 50%, so the 20,000 equipment rent is included."],["What is UBTI?","20,000 - 1,000 = 19,000."]]}}],"16":[{"i":"REG-V-G-0001","t":"The Lark Family Trust is a simple trust that must distribute all of its income currently. For 2026 its fiduciary accounting income was $40,000, and trustee fees of $3,000 are charged against that income. It also had a $10,000 capital gain that is allocated to principal. What amount does the sole income beneficiary include in income from the trust?","mc":{"s":"The Lark Family Trust is a simple trust that must distribute all of its income currently. For 2026 its fiduciary accounting income was $40,000, and trustee fees of $3,000 are charged against that income. It also had a $10,000 capital gain that is allocated to principal. What amount does the sole income beneficiary include in income from the trust?","d":"","o":["$37,000","$40,000","$47,000","$50,000"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §643(a); IRC §651; IRC §652","rat":"Distributable net income is $37,000 (income less fees; the gain allocated to principal is excluded).","ex":["Distributable net income is $37,000 (income less fees; the gain allocated to principal is excluded). A simple trust's beneficiary reports the income required to be distributed, limited to DNI.","Ignores the $3,000 of trustee fees charged against income.","Adds the capital gain that is allocated to principal and is not distributed.","Adds the capital gain and ignores the fees."],"wt":["","calc","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"G","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A simple trust's beneficiary reports income required to be distributed, limited to DNI, after fees and excluding principal gains.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Income 40,000 - fees 3,000 = 37,000 DNI","The $10,000 gain stays in the trust (taxed to the trust)"],"gd":[["What is the trust's distributable net income?","Income less fees, 40,000 - 3,000 = 37,000, with the principal gain excluded."],["What does the simple trust beneficiary report?","Income required to be distributed, limited to DNI, so 37,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-G-0002","t":"Which statement about the personal exemption amounts of trusts and estates is correct?","mc":{"s":"Which statement about the personal exemption amounts of trusts and estates is correct?","d":"","o":["An estate has an exemption of $600, a simple trust has $300, and a complex trust has $100","An estate has an exemption of $300, a simple trust has $600, and a complex trust has $100","An estate has an exemption of $100, a simple trust has $300, and a complex trust has $600","All three have the same exemption of $300"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §642(b)","rat":"The fiduciary exemptions are $600 for an estate, $300 for a simple trust and $100 for a complex trust.","ex":["The fiduciary exemptions are $600 for an estate, $300 for a simple trust and $100 for a complex trust.","The estate and simple trust amounts are reversed.","The estate and complex trust amounts are reversed.","The exemptions differ by type of fiduciary."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"G","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Fiduciary exemptions are $600 for an estate, $300 for a simple trust and $100 for a complex trust.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["What is the estate's exemption?","600."],["What are the simple and complex trust exemptions?","300 and 100."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-G-0003","t":"Which statement about the tax year of a decedent's estate and of a non-exempt trust is correct?","mc":{"s":"Which statement about the tax year of a decedent's estate and of a non-exempt trust is correct?","d":"","o":["Both may choose any fiscal year","Both must use a calendar year","The trust may choose a fiscal year, but an estate must use a calendar year","The estate may choose a fiscal year, but a non-exempt trust generally must use a calendar year"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §441(b), §644(a); Form 1041 instructions","rat":"An estate may elect any fiscal year end up to 12 months after death; most trusts must use the calendar year.","ex":["Trusts generally must use a calendar year.","An estate may elect a fiscal year.","The results are reversed.","An estate may elect any fiscal year end up to 12 months after death; most trusts must use the calendar year."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"G","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"An estate may elect a fiscal year, while most trusts must use a calendar year.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Who may choose a fiscal year?","An estate, up to 12 months after death."],["What year does a non-exempt trust generally use?","The calendar year."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-G-0004","t":"A complex trust has distributable net income of $50,000. It must distribute $30,000 currently to Beneficiary A. The trustee also distributes $40,000 in its discretion to Beneficiary B. How much of B's distribution is taxable to B?","mc":{"s":"A complex trust has distributable net income of $50,000. It must distribute $30,000 currently to Beneficiary A. The trustee also distributes $40,000 in its discretion to Beneficiary B. How much of B's distribution is taxable to B?","d":"","o":["$0","$20,000","$28,571","$40,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §661; IRC §662(a)","rat":"DNI is allocated first to income required to be distributed ($30,000 to A), leaving $20,000 for the discretionary distribution.","ex":["Assumes discretionary distributions are never taxable.","DNI is allocated first to income required to be distributed ($30,000 to A), leaving $20,000 for the discretionary distribution. B includes $20,000 and receives the other $20,000 tax-free.","Prorates DNI across both distributions; the tier rule gives the required distribution priority.","Treats the entire discretionary distribution as taxable, ignoring the tier system and DNI limit."],"wt":["concept","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"G","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"DNI goes first to income required to be distributed, then to discretionary distributions in tiers.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["First tier (required) to A = 30,000","DNI remaining = 50,000 - 30,000 = 20,000","B taxable = min(40,000, 20,000) = 20,000"],"gd":[["Who is served first from DNI?","The beneficiary with required distributions, A with 30,000."],["How much DNI remains for B?","50,000 - 30,000 = 20,000."],["What is taxable to B?","The lesser of 40,000 and 20,000, so 20,000."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-G-0005","t":"Which of the following trusts is a simple trust for the year?","mc":{"s":"Which of the following trusts is a simple trust for the year?","d":"","o":["A trust that must distribute all of its income currently, and also distributes $10,000 of principal to a beneficiary","A trust that must distribute all of its income currently, makes no charitable payments and distributes no principal","A trust that may accumulate income in the trustee's discretion","A trust that must distribute all of its income currently, but pays part of it to a charity"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §651(a); IRC §661(a)","rat":"A simple trust must distribute all income currently, has no charitable beneficiaries and distributes no principal.","ex":["Distributing principal makes it a complex trust for that year.","A simple trust must distribute all income currently, has no charitable beneficiaries and distributes no principal.","A trust that may accumulate income is not a simple trust.","A payment to a charity makes it a complex trust."],"wt":["concept","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"G","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A simple trust must distribute all income currently, make no charitable payments and distribute no principal.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Which three features mark a simple trust?","Current distribution of all income, no charitable payments, and no principal distributions."],["Which listed trust has all three?","The one that pays out all income currently and does nothing else."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-G-0006","t":"Draft 2026 Form 1041 for the estate of Paul Ortiz claims a $100 exemption. The estate is a domestic decedent estate with no other special facts. Which correction is required?","mc":{"s":"Draft 2026 Form 1041 for the estate of Paul Ortiz claims a $100 exemption. The estate is a domestic decedent estate with no other special facts. Which correction is required?","d":"","o":["Claim a $600 exemption, because a decedent's estate has a $600 exemption","Claim a $300 exemption, because the estate must distribute income currently","Claim no exemption, because estates do not receive exemptions","Leave the $100 exemption, because the estate accumulates income"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §642(b)(1); Form 1041 instructions","rat":"A decedent's estate has an exemption of $600.","ex":["A decedent's estate has an exemption of $600.","Estates do not use the simple trust exemption, and there is no such requirement.","Estates receive a $600 exemption.","The $100 exemption applies to complex trusts, not estates."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"G","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A decedent's estate has a $600 exemption.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["What exemption does a decedent's estate have?","600."],["Which correction does the 100 draft need?","Claim the 600 estate exemption."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-G-1001","t":"The Ellis Trust is a simple trust that must distribute all of its accounting income currently to its sole beneficiary, Dora. For 2026 it had $40,000 of interest and a $10,000 long-term capital gain that the trust instrument allocates to principal. Trustee fees of $3,000, charged entirely to income under the instrument, are deductible. The trust distributed all of its accounting income to Dora and kept the gain. What do Dora and the trust report?","mc":{"s":"The Ellis Trust is a simple trust that must distribute all of its accounting income currently to its sole beneficiary, Dora. For 2026 it had $40,000 of interest and a $10,000 long-term capital gain that the trust instrument allocates to principal. Trustee fees of $3,000, charged entirely to income under the instrument, are deductible. The trust distributed all of its accounting income to Dora and kept the gain. What do Dora and the trust report?","d":"","o":["Dora reports $37,000, and the trust's taxable income is $10,000","Dora reports $40,000, and the trust's taxable income is $6,700","Dora reports $37,000, and the trust's taxable income is $9,700","Dora reports $37,000, and the trust's taxable income is $12,700"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §643(a)(3); IRC §651(a),(b); IRC §642(b)(1); IRC §652(a)","rat":"Accounting income and distributable net income are $40,000 - $3,000 = $37,000, because the gain is allocated to principal.","ex":["Omits the $300 exemption for a simple trust.","Ignores that the fees reduce distributable net income and the amount distributed.","Accounting income and distributable net income are $40,000 - $3,000 = $37,000, because the gain is allocated to principal. The trust deducts the $37,000 distribution and its $300 exemption from $47,000 of income after fees: $9,700.","Does not deduct the $3,000 of trustee fees in computing the trust's taxable income."],"wt":["calc","calc","","calc"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"G","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A simple trust's distribution deduction is limited to income (DNI), which excludes capital gains allocated to principal, so the trust pays tax on those gains.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["DNI = 40,000 - 3,000 = 37,000 (gain allocated to principal is excluded)","Trust taxable income = 40,000 + 10,000 - 3,000 - 37,000 - 300 = 9,700"],"gd":[["What is DNI when the gain is allocated to principal?","40,000 - 3,000 = 37,000."],["What does Dora report?","37,000."],["What is the trust's taxable income?","40,000 + 10,000 - 3,000 - 37,000 - 300 = 9,700."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-G-1002","t":"Nolan Price died on March 12, 2026. His estate elected a fiscal year ending January 31, 2027, for its first tax year. Nolan had also created a nonexempt irrevocable trust that has always used a calendar year. On August 1, 2026, the estate and the trust each distributed $60,000 to the same calendar-year beneficiary, and each distribution carries out distributable net income of at least $60,000. In which year does the beneficiary report each amount?","mc":{"s":"Nolan Price died on March 12, 2026. His estate elected a fiscal year ending January 31, 2027, for its first tax year. Nolan had also created a nonexempt irrevocable trust that has always used a calendar year. On August 1, 2026, the estate and the trust each distributed $60,000 to the same calendar-year beneficiary, and each distribution carries out distributable net income of at least $60,000. In which year does the beneficiary report each amount?","d":"","o":["Both in 2026, because the beneficiary uses the calendar year and received both on August 1","The estate distribution in 2027 and the trust distribution in 2026","The estate distribution in 2026 and the trust distribution in 2027","Both in 2027, because the distributions were made after the decedent's death"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §662(c); IRC §441(e); IRC §644(a); IRC §645","rat":"A beneficiary reports income for the fiduciary's tax year that ends within the beneficiary's tax year.","ex":["The year of inclusion is set by the fiduciary's year-end, not the date of receipt.","A beneficiary reports income for the fiduciary's tax year that ends within the beneficiary's tax year. The estate's year ends January 31, 2027 (within 12 months of death) and the calendar-year trust's ends December 31, 2026.","Reverses the years. The fiscal-year estate's distribution falls in 2027.","The trust uses a calendar year, so its distribution is reported for the year ending December 31, 2026."],"wt":["concept","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"G","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A fiscal-year estate's distributions are reported in the beneficiary's year in which the estate's year ends, while a nonexempt trust must use a calendar year.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Which fiduciary year does a beneficiary use?","The fiduciary year that ends within the beneficiary's own tax year."],["When does each fiduciary year end?","The estate's ends January 31, 2027 and the calendar-year trust's ends December 31, 2026."],["When are the amounts reported?","The estate distribution in 2027 and the trust distribution in 2026."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-G-1003","t":"The estate of Wanda Cruz had $30,000 of interest income in 2026 and no deductions. The executor paid $40,000 in one payment to Wanda's niece under a specific bequest of $40,000, and, in her discretion, distributed $25,000 to Wanda's son as part of the residuary estate. The estate's exemption is $600. What do the niece and the son report, and what is the estate's taxable income?","mc":{"s":"The estate of Wanda Cruz had $30,000 of interest income in 2026 and no deductions. The executor paid $40,000 in one payment to Wanda's niece under a specific bequest of $40,000, and, in her discretion, distributed $25,000 to Wanda's son as part of the residuary estate. The estate's exemption is $600. What do the niece and the son report, and what is the estate's taxable income?","d":"","o":["The niece reports $0, the son reports $25,000, and the estate's taxable income is $4,400","The niece reports $30,000, the son reports $0, and the estate's taxable income is $0","The niece reports $0, the son reports $25,000, and the estate's taxable income is $5,000","The niece reports $18,462, the son reports $11,538, and the estate's taxable income is $0"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §663(a)(1); IRC §661(a); IRC §642(b)(1); Reg. §1.663(a)-1","rat":"A bequest of a specific sum paid in three or fewer installments does not carry out income, so the niece reports nothing.","ex":["A bequest of a specific sum paid in three or fewer installments does not carry out income, so the niece reports nothing. The son's $25,000 carries out DNI. The estate deducts $25,000 and its $600 exemption from $30,000: $4,400.","Treats the specific bequest as carrying out income. It does not, because it is a specific sum paid at once.","Leaves out the $600 exemption.","Splits DNI pro rata over both payments. The specific bequest is excluded from the distribution deduction."],"wt":["","concept","calc","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"G","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A specific bequest of money paid in three or fewer installments carries out no income, so only the other distributions share in DNI.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Specific bequest 40,000: excluded from the distribution deduction and from the niece's income","Distribution deduction = min(25,000, DNI 30,000) = 25,000","Estate taxable income = 30,000 - 25,000 - 600 = 4,400"],"gd":[["Does a specific sum bequest carry out income?","No, when paid in three or fewer installments, so the niece reports nothing."],["What is the estate's distribution deduction?","The lesser of 25,000 and DNI 30,000, so 25,000."],["What is taxable income?","30,000 - 25,000 - 600 = 4,400."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-G-1004","t":"Three trusts each earned $20,000 of interest in 2026. Trust 1 is revocable by its grantor, Sam, alone. Trust 2 is irrevocable, and its independent trustee may pay its income to Sam's wife or accumulate it for her, without the consent of any adverse party. Trust 3 is irrevocable; its independent trustee may pay income to Sam's adult daughter or accumulate it, and Sam holds no powers and has no interest in it. Which statement is correct?","mc":{"s":"Three trusts each earned $20,000 of interest in 2026. Trust 1 is revocable by its grantor, Sam, alone. Trust 2 is irrevocable, and its independent trustee may pay its income to Sam's wife or accumulate it for her, without the consent of any adverse party. Trust 3 is irrevocable; its independent trustee may pay income to Sam's adult daughter or accumulate it, and Sam holds no powers and has no interest in it. Which statement is correct?","d":"","o":["Sam reports the income of Trust 1 only, and Trusts 2 and 3 are separate taxpayers because they are irrevocable","Sam reports the income of all three trusts, because he created each of them","Sam reports the income of Trusts 1 and 3, and Trust 2 is a separate taxpayer because its trustee is independent","Sam reports the income of Trusts 1 and 2, and Trust 3 is taxed as a separate taxpayer, with a distribution deduction for amounts distributed"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §676(a); IRC §677(a)(1); IRC §671","rat":"A power to revoke makes Trust 1 a grantor trust, and income that may be paid to the grantor's spouse without an adverse party's consent makes Trust 2 a grantor trust.","ex":["Irrevocability does not prevent grantor trust status. Income payable to the grantor's spouse is attributed to the grantor.","Creating a trust does not make it a grantor trust. Trust 3 has no retained power or interest.","An independent trustee does not prevent grantor trust status where income may go to the grantor's spouse without an adverse party's consent.","A power to revoke makes Trust 1 a grantor trust, and income that may be paid to the grantor's spouse without an adverse party's consent makes Trust 2 a grantor trust. Trust 3 has neither feature."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"G","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A trust is taxed to its grantor if the grantor can revoke it or its income can go to the grantor or the grantor's spouse without an adverse party's consent.","from":"2026-01-01","gd":[["Which trust features make a grantor trust?","A power to revoke, and income payable to the grantor's spouse without an adverse party's consent."],["Which trusts have them?","The revocable trust and the trust that may pay income to his wife."],["How is the third taxed?","As a separate taxpayer with a distribution deduction."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-G-1005","t":"The estate of Ruth Vale has a gross estate of $4,000,000. Assume the 2026 federal estate tax basic exclusion amount is $15,000,000, so no estate tax will be owed. The executor paid $30,000 of administration expenses. The estate has $200,000 of taxable interest in 2026 taxed at a 37% marginal rate on Form 1041, and it will make no distributions in 2026. Where should the expenses be deducted, and what is the tax saving?","mc":{"s":"The estate of Ruth Vale has a gross estate of $4,000,000. Assume the 2026 federal estate tax basic exclusion amount is $15,000,000, so no estate tax will be owed. The executor paid $30,000 of administration expenses. The estate has $200,000 of taxable interest in 2026 taxed at a 37% marginal rate on Form 1041, and it will make no distributions in 2026. Where should the expenses be deducted, and what is the tax saving?","d":"","o":["On Form 706, saving $12,000 at the 40% estate tax rate","On both Form 706 and Form 1041, saving $23,100","On Form 1041, saving $11,100","On Form 1041, saving $12,000, because the 40% estate tax rate applies to the deduction"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §642(g); Reg. §1.642(g)-1; IRC §2053; IRC §2010(c)(3)","rat":"With no estate tax, a Form 706 deduction saves nothing.","ex":["No estate tax is owed, so the estate tax deduction has no value.","Expenses cannot be deducted twice. A deduction claimed for estate tax cannot also be claimed for income tax.","With no estate tax, a Form 706 deduction saves nothing. Deducting the expenses on Form 1041 saves 37% x $30,000 = $11,100.","The income tax saving is measured at the income tax rate (37%), not the estate tax rate."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"G","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Administration expenses can be deducted on the estate tax return or the income tax return but not both, so choose the one where the deduction actually saves tax.","from":"2026-07-01","calc":1,"steps":["Gross estate 4,000,000 < 15,000,000 exclusion: no estate tax, so a Form 706 deduction saves 0","Form 1041: 37% x 30,000 = 11,100"],"gd":[["What does a Form 706 deduction save with no estate tax due?","Nothing, since 4,000,000 is under the 15,000,000 exclusion."],["What does Form 1041 save?","37% x 30,000 = 11,100."]]}},{"i":"REG-V-G-1006","t":"The Aster Trust is a calendar-year complex trust with distributable net income of $60,000 for 2026, all of it also accounting income. It distributed $30,000 to a beneficiary on December 20, 2026, $12,000 on February 20, 2027, and $8,000 on March 15, 2027. The trustee makes the 65-day election for 2026 on a timely filed 2026 return. The trust's exemption is $100. What is the trust's 2026 taxable income?","mc":{"s":"The Aster Trust is a calendar-year complex trust with distributable net income of $60,000 for 2026, all of it also accounting income. It distributed $30,000 to a beneficiary on December 20, 2026, $12,000 on February 20, 2027, and $8,000 on March 15, 2027. The trustee makes the 65-day election for 2026 on a timely filed 2026 return. The trust's exemption is $100. What is the trust's 2026 taxable income?","d":"","o":["$9,900","$17,900","$21,900","$29,900"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §663(b); IRC §661(a); IRC §642(b)(2)(C)","rat":"The first 65 days of 2027 run through March 6, so the February 20 payment ($12,000) can be treated as made in 2026, but the March 15 payment cannot.","ex":["Treats the March 15 payment as made in 2026. It falls after the 65th day, so it does not qualify.","The first 65 days of 2027 run through March 6, so the February 20 payment ($12,000) can be treated as made in 2026, but the March 15 payment cannot. The distribution deduction is $42,000: $60,000 - $42,000 - $100 = $17,900.","Counts the March 15 payment instead of the February 20 payment. Only the February payment is within 65 days.","Ignores the election and counts only the December payment."],"wt":["limit","","limit","concept"],"tags":{"area":"V","group":"G","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A trustee may elect to treat distributions made in the first 65 days of the next year as made in the prior year, up to the income not otherwise distributed.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["First 65 days of 2027 end March 6, 2027 (65 days from January 1)","February 20 is day 51 (qualifies); March 15 is day 74 (does not)","Distribution deduction 30,000 + 12,000 = 42,000; election limit 60,000 - 30,000 = 30,000 is not exceeded","Taxable income 60,000 - 42,000 - 100 = 17,900"],"gd":[["What are the first 65 days of 2027?","January 1 through March 6."],["Which 2027 payments qualify?","February 20, day 51, qualifies; March 15, day 74, does not."],["What are the deduction and taxable income?","30,000 + 12,000 = 42,000, so 60,000 - 42,000 - 100 = 17,900."]]}}]};
window.PAIRS=[];
